-- allium: 3
-- skilj.allium

-- Scope: SkilJ, a Rust library for building event-sourced, DDD-style
--   applications backed by a single shared Postgres database, exposing
--   two clearly separated callers: a GraphQL surface for admin and
--   website consumers, and a REST surface for machine callers.
-- Includes: Bounded contexts, Commands, Events and their possible
--   origins, Command/Event type registration with JSON-Schema payload
--   definitions, the admin bounded context that registers other bounded
--   contexts and their types, projections built from events - registered
--   from the compiled binary the same way event and command types are,
--   updated asynchronously by default with an opt-in synchronous mode,
--   and queried over GraphQL as richly-typed fields with an optional
--   read-your-writes wait - raw event stream subscriptions with
--   per-field filtering, gated by the same RoleAccessMapping-based read
--   access and sensitive-field protection as querying, forward-only from
--   an optional caller-supplied sequence so a live feed never replays
--   history a caller is expected to have queried separately,
--   an in-memory per-bounded-context cache of the most recent events,
--   warmed at startup from Postgres up to a configurable count
--   (default 1000), so recent reads need no database round trip,
--   per-field sensitivity marking on events and commands alike - both
--   resolving to the same per-subject EncryptionKey, so crypto-shredding
--   erases both at once (SensitiveField, EncryptionKey,
--   right-to-be-forgotten via ForgetSubject) - per-field private marking
--   on events and commands alike, the plain read-time redaction rule
--   standing beside that cryptographic one for a field that should be
--   visible only to whoever created the record, only to a named team, or
--   only to the one other party the record itself names, with sharing and
--   revocation the creator drives itself and no encryption or erasure
--   involved anywhere (PrivateField, PrivateFieldGrant) - admin-facing retrieval of
--   stored commands, by command type, by time window, or by the event a
--   command produced (CommandQuery), an optional caller-supplied
--   idempotency key on command submission over either track, so a caller
--   retrying a submission it never heard back from is answered with the
--   original outcome rather than applying the command a second time
--   (see rule ProcessCommand), an optional caller-supplied partition key
--   and sequence number on external event submission, so an at-least-once
--   source redelivering a message it already delivered creates no second
--   event (see rule CreateExternalEvent), and two independent
--   authentication tracks:
--   - GraphQL: identity is delegated to a trusted external identity
--     provider. A caller presents a JWT the IdP issued; SkilJ verifies
--     that JWT's signature against the IdP's JWKS (a black box here,
--     same as protect_sensitive_fields) and trusts its subject claim,
--     which resolves to the active Role bound to that value via
--     Role.external_subject. SkilJ issues and stores no credential of
--     its own for a Role - there is nothing to "log in" to on SkilJ's
--     side. A Role reaches a bounded context through a
--     RoleAccessMapping - a per-bounded-context grant at
--     read/write/admin level - plus a superadmin flag on Role for
--     creating new bounded contexts. Used by admin roles for
--     maintenance and by website consumers. See the identity
--     resolution note above entity Role.
--   - REST (and, likely, websocket later): a family of narrowly scoped
--     bearer tokens (AccessToken, with one variant per target type -
--     ExternalEventToken, DirectCreationToken, CommandToken,
--     EventReadToken), issued by SkilJ itself and presented as
--     "Authorization: Bearer <id>.<secret>". Each token authorises
--     exactly one capability against exactly one registered type, and
--     each is issued and revoked independently by an admin over
--     GraphQL. Used to submit external events, to let AI/automated
--     callers trigger commands, and to fetch events for remote
--     workflows.
-- Excludes:
--   - Retroactive encryption of historical events and commands when a
--     field is newly marked sensitive: event sourcing is append-only and
--     a stored command is never rewritten either, so this is a
--     structural consequence, not a gap (see EventType.sensitive_fields
--     with Event.encryption_keys, and CommandType.sensitive_fields with
--     Command.encryption_keys)
--   - The encryption scheme itself (algorithm, key derivation, physical
--     key storage) - protect_sensitive_fields is a black box, consistent
--     with how AccessToken.secret generation and JWT signature
--     verification are already treated
--   - Distributed-systems machinery: partitioning or sharding of a
--     bounded context's stream, consensus, leader election, cross-region
--     replication, and instance discovery or a service registry. What is
--     no longer excluded with it is the one deployment shape this
--     library is actually expected to run in: several stateless
--     instances behind a load balancer, all of them symmetric and all
--     sharing exactly one Postgres database. Real-time push completeness
--     across those instances - a committed event, a revocation and a
--     registration change each reaching every instance rather than only
--     the one that handled the call - and the cross-instance schema
--     consistency that follows from it are in scope, and specified as
--     guarantees rather than as a mechanism (see EventSubscription's
--     DeliverySpansInstances and TypeRegistration's
--     RegistrationReachesEveryInstance). What stays excluded is
--     everything that would require instances to know about each other
--     at all: nothing here elects, discovers, addresses or partitions
--     across them, and an instance reaches every other only through the
--     shared database, which remains the only shared infrastructure this
--     library assumes
--   - The GraphQL wire contract itself, beyond identity resolution and
--     the surfaces specified here: how a Projection.schema becomes a
--     concrete GraphQL type, field and argument naming, paging and error
--     shape are all still deferred to a later, more detailed pass
--   - The external IdP trust configuration values themselves - JWKS
--     endpoint, issuer, expected audience, signing algorithm, and which
--     claim carries the subject (default "sub"). Resolved as runtime/library
--     configuration for exactly one trusted IdP, in the same register
--     as the event cache's startup warm-up count, rather than modelled
--     as domain state; the additive path to trusting several IdPs later
--     is recorded in the identity resolution note above entity Role
--   - The websocket complement to token-authenticated event fetching:
--     excluded as a surface here, not undecided. It is the same
--     capability under a different binding - the same EventReadToken and
--     the same EventType.event_read_allowed opt-in, presented in an
--     Authorization header at the connection handshake (the audience is
--     server-side remote workflows, not browser JS, so header-based auth
--     at handshake is viable), reusing Subscription /
--     EventTypeSubscription / Filter rather than a second filter model,
--     and taking EventSubscription's delivery and resume answer
--     unchanged: at most once while the connection is live, nothing held
--     for a connection that is not, and a reconnect that creates a fresh
--     subscription from the last sequence received rather than resuming
--     the old one (see EventSubscription's DeliveryIsAtMostOnce). What
--     stays excluded is its wire contract, alongside the GraphQL wire
--     contract already excluded above
--   - Orchestrating a long-running, multi-step business process that
--     spans real I/O, real time, or another system entirely (reserve
--     inventory, call an external payment gateway, wait up to a day for
--     a webhook, ship or compensate): excluded by design, not an
--     oversight. decide()'s own synchronous, no-I/O contract already
--     rules it out structurally, and it is a different problem from the
--     one DCB tagging solves - a command deriving more than one tag
--     already lets decide() check several facts in one synchronous,
--     same-database transaction (see the note above rule ProcessCommand
--     on consistency_tags/matching_events), replacing the classic
--     saga/process-manager need for exactly that case, never for a
--     process that cannot be one transaction at all. What
--     is in scope, and already built, is the one piece such a process
--     from outside SkilJ actually needs: an idempotency key on command
--     submission (see the note above rule ProcessCommand), which an
--     external orchestrator (Temporal or otherwise) uses the same way
--     any other retrying caller does. Orchestrating the process itself -
--     durable timers, retries, compensation, fan-out/fan-in - is left to
--     that external system entirely; SkilJ's own event store stays the
--     system of record for what happened to the domain, never for what
--     a process did to get there
-- Dependencies: none yet

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

value Filter {
    field: String              -- names a field in the event type's schema, either as a bare
                                -- top-level field name or as a two-segment dotted path reaching
                                -- one leaf inside a nested shape (e.g. address.country). Never
                                -- deeper than two segments - the one-level nesting cap makes
                                -- that structurally impossible - and never a nested-object-typed
                                -- field itself: there is no filtering on the whole address, only
                                -- on a scalar leaf within it (see the payload schema shape note
                                -- above entity CommandType)
    operator: FilterOperator
    value: String               -- comparison value, serialised to match the field's declared type
}

-- correlation_id and causation_id below are durable domain state, and are
-- deliberately not distributed tracing. Tracing - OpenTelemetry or
-- anything else wearing its shape - is out of scope for this file
-- entirely, in the same register as the external IdP's trust
-- configuration and the event cache's warm-up count: runtime/library
-- configuration a deploying team wires up around this library, never
-- something this spec models (see the scope header's exclusions). A trace
-- id is ephemeral, sampled, and gone once the span it belongs to has been
-- exported or dropped; these two are written into the stored record
-- alongside its payload, kept for as long as the event or command is kept,
-- and queryable as themselves. A reader asking "what else happened as part
-- of this business transaction" is asking a question about the domain's
-- own history, and must get its answer from the event store rather than
-- from whatever observability backend happened to be running at the time.
--
-- Nothing stops a caller putting a W3C traceparent-style value in
-- correlation_id if that is the id its own upstream chain is keyed by -
-- see valid_correlation_id in the correlation note above the rules, which
-- imposes no shape on either field beyond a length bound. That is a caller's choice
-- about what to correlate by, not this library adopting a tracing model.
value Metadata {
    type: String                -- denormalised name of the event/command type this message conforms to
    version: Integer             -- schema_version of the type at the time this message was created.
                                 -- Audit and provenance only. A reader never resolves it to the
                                 -- schema text that was in force then: none is kept, and none is
                                 -- needed, because backwards-compatible-only evolution makes the
                                 -- type's current schema a superset of every schema it has ever
                                 -- had, so that current schema still describes this payload in
                                 -- full. See the schema_is_backwards_compatible note above rule
                                 -- RegisterEventType.
    client_id: String          -- identifies the client/caller that produced this message in some cases it might refer to the original call, for an event it might be the client that issues the command
    created_at: Timestamp
    correlation_id: String?     -- identifies the whole business transaction this message belongs
                                 -- to: every command and event in one causal chain carries the same
                                 -- value, so the chain can be reassembled from the store afterwards.
                                 -- Supplied by the caller when it already has one - an adapter
                                 -- forwarding an upstream broker's own id, a UI tying several
                                 -- submissions to one user action - and generated here when it does
                                 -- not, so a stored Command always has one (see rule ProcessCommand).
                                 -- Never interpreted: an opaque caller-chosen string, compared for
                                 -- equality and nothing else, the same treatment an idempotency key
                                 -- and a RoleAccessMapping.scope already get.
    causation_id: String?       -- identifies the immediate cause of this message: the id of the
                                 -- command or event that directly produced it, one link back rather
                                 -- than the whole chain correlation_id names. Null when there is no
                                 -- upstream - a plain submission a caller originated itself, a
                                 -- scheduler occurrence - which is the root of a chain, not a gap in
                                 -- one. Opaque and uninterpreted, exactly as correlation_id is.
}

value Tag {
    key: String                 -- shared across event types, e.g. "account"
    value: String?              -- e.g. "123". Null is the "None" state: the mapped payload
                                 -- field was absent from this particular payload, because the
                                 -- schema declares it optional and this instance omitted it
                                 -- (or, for a list-typed field, because the list was empty or
                                 -- absent - see the derive_tags note above the rules). An
                                 -- absent field still produces a Tag under its mapped key,
                                 -- with value null, rather than no tag at all. That is
                                 -- deliberate, not an oversight: it means a consistency
                                 -- boundary or a filter can address "every event or command
                                 -- where this field was never set" as a first-class case,
                                 -- instead of that case being invisible. It is expected to be
                                 -- a broad and rarely-useful match in practice - one tag key
                                 -- with one null value covers the whole never-set population -
                                 -- but a capability that is available and seldom wanted is a
                                 -- different thing from one that does not exist.
}

value TagMapping {
    key: String                 -- the tag key this mapping produces, e.g. "account" - the same key
                                 -- can be declared by multiple event types, so a consistency boundary
                                 -- can span all of them regardless of type (e.g. MoneyAdded and
                                 -- MoneySubtracted both mapping their account_id field to "account").
                                 -- What a shared key buys is that reach across types, at one value of
                                 -- the key - not a boundary over every value of it. There is no
                                 -- type-wide, valueless form of tag, and the derive_tags note above
                                 -- the rules says why that is settled rather than missing.
                                 --
                                 -- Keys are plain strings: no namespace prefix, no central registry.
                                 -- None is needed, because every place a tag key is read partitions
                                 -- by bounded context before comparing anything. ProcessCommand's
                                 -- matching_events reads only Events where bounded_context =
                                 -- command_type.bounded_context; DynamicConsistencyBoundaryHonoured
                                 -- requires e.bounded_context = c.bounded_context; matches_filters is
                                 -- only ever reached through an EventType, which belongs to exactly
                                 -- one bounded context (FetchEvents, DeliverToSubscriptions); and
                                 -- both Subscription variants are scoped by
                                 -- access_mapping.bounded_context or event_type.bounded_context.
                                 -- So two unrelated bounded contexts that both declare "account"
                                 -- never interact - a key string is effectively namespaced by the
                                 -- bounded context it is declared and matched within, without an
                                 -- explicit prefix. Sharing a key is a within-context act, and only
                                 -- that. This is also why ConsistencyTagKeysAreDeclared scopes its
                                 -- check to the command's own bounded context.
    field: String                -- name of the payload field this key's value is read from, for this
                                 -- event type specifically. It may name a scalar field or a
                                 -- list-of-scalars field, and the field may be optional; how
                                 -- many Tags each of those shapes derives, and what an absent
                                 -- or empty one derives, is the derive_tags contract - see the
                                 -- note above the rules. It may be written either as a bare
                                 -- top-level field name or as a two-segment dotted path reaching
                                 -- one leaf inside a nested shape (e.g. address.country); never
                                 -- deeper, and never a nested-object-typed field itself, since
                                 -- there is no tag whose value is a whole address (see the
                                 -- payload schema shape note above entity CommandType).
}

value EventSpec {
    event_type: EventType
    payload: String              -- JSON-encoded, conforms to event_type.schema
}

value CommandDecision {
    accepted: Boolean
    events: List<EventSpec>      -- the events to append; read only when accepted = true
    rejection_reason: String?    -- domain-specific explanation from the decider; surfaced verbatim
                                  -- to the caller when accepted = false. Producing a
                                  -- meaningful message here is the decider's responsibility - it
                                  -- needs domain knowledge this library doesn't have.
    rejection_kind: String?      -- machine-readable label for what kind of rejection this is, set by
                                  -- the decider alongside rejection_reason and always together with
                                  -- it (see the shape note below). It exists because a caller - a UI
                                  -- reached over GraphQL, typically - needs to branch on the kind of
                                  -- rejection programmatically, e.g. to decide whether to offer a
                                  -- retry, and must not have to parse the free-text English of
                                  -- rejection_reason to do it. A command that would push an account
                                  -- balance below zero is a business-rule rejection a decider might
                                  -- tag "insufficient_balance", distinct in kind from some other
                                  -- rejection the same decider can produce, even though both arrive
                                  -- as a plain message today.
                                  --
                                  -- Deliberately an opaque String rather than an enum declared here,
                                  -- the same treatment EventType.schema, CommandType.schema and
                                  -- rejection_reason already get. The possible kinds are genuinely
                                  -- per-bounded-context: different deciders, different domains,
                                  -- different error taxonomies. A fixed closed set in this file would
                                  -- force every bounded context this library hosts - unrelated
                                  -- domains, by design - into one shared error vocabulary, which is
                                  -- exactly the business knowledge the library does not have. Which
                                  -- values exist, and how stable they are, is each bounded context's
                                  -- own contract with its own callers.
                                  --
                                  -- Like rejection_reason it is surfaced to the caller when a command
                                  -- is rejected, alongside the message rather than instead of it. The
                                  -- exact wire shape stays deferred with the rest of the wire
                                  -- contract - see the scope header's exclusions, and the same
                                  -- framing above rule QueryProjection.

    -- Shape. These four fields are two shapes, not four independent
    -- values. Exactly one of the following holds of every decision
    -- decide() returns:
    --
    --   accepted = true   events carries the events to append, possibly
    --                     empty. rejection_reason and rejection_kind are
    --                     both null; no caller may read either.
    --   accepted = false  rejection_reason and rejection_kind are both
    --                     non-null - always the two together, never one
    --                     without the other - and both are surfaced to the
    --                     caller. events is ignored; no caller may read it.
    --
    -- Nothing else is a well-formed decision. A rejection carrying a
    -- reason but no kind is exactly as malformed as one carrying neither:
    -- the pair is one unit, because a caller that can only read English
    -- prose cannot branch, which is the whole reason rejection_kind
    -- exists. So a caller may rely on rejection_kind being present
    -- whenever accepted = false, with no null check and no fallback, and
    -- must never rely on it otherwise.
    --
    -- The type does not enforce that pairing, and cannot. The `?` on both
    -- rejection fields means "does not apply in the other case", not
    -- "optional within its own case" - a distinction a value type in this
    -- language has no way to draw, since its fields are one flat list with
    -- no discriminator to hang presence off. Sum types here are entity
    -- machinery: a discriminator field over separately declared
    -- `variant X : BaseEntity` shapes, as AccessToken.purpose and
    -- Event.origin both use. A decision has no identity, no lifecycle and
    -- no stored form, so promoting it to an entity purely to buy a
    -- discriminator would misrepresent it considerably more than this flat
    -- shape understates it. The pairing is therefore an obligation on
    -- decide(), written down here and unchecked, in the same register as
    -- the rest of that plugin contract - see rule ProcessCommand, where
    -- decide() is deliberately a prose contract rather than a formally
    -- specified interface.
    --
    -- An implementation is free to model this as a real two-case sum, with
    -- the rejection fields mandatory and non-optional in the rejection
    -- case and absent entirely from the acceptance case; skilj's own does.
    -- That is a faithful refinement of what is written here - it can
    -- represent every well-formed decision and no malformed one - not a
    -- divergence from it.
}

value SensitiveField {
    field: String                -- name of the payload field to store encrypted
    subject_key: String          -- canonical subject dimension this field belongs to
                                  -- (e.g. "user") - shared across event types, so the
                                  -- same EncryptionKey covers a subject regardless of
                                  -- which event type or payload field name captured it
    subject_field: String        -- name of the payload field this event type reads the
                                  -- concrete subject value from (e.g. "user_id")

    -- Both field and subject_field may be written either as a bare
    -- top-level field name or as a two-segment dotted path reaching one
    -- leaf inside a nested shape (e.g. address.country) - never deeper,
    -- and never a nested-object-typed field itself. A whole nested object
    -- cannot be marked sensitive; only a scalar leaf within it can, and
    -- that leaf alone is what gets replaced by ciphertext. See the payload
    -- schema shape note above entity CommandType for why encrypting the
    -- enclosing object is the wrong shape rather than merely unbuilt.
}

value EncryptedPayload {
    payload: String                      -- payload with each declared sensitive field's
                                          -- value replaced by ciphertext
    encryption_keys: Set<EncryptionKey>  -- the EncryptionKeys used, one per distinct
                                          -- subject a sensitive field referenced
}

-- The third field-level protection mechanism, and deliberately the
-- cheapest of the three. SensitiveField above protects a third party's
-- PII with real encryption, because that protection has to survive a
-- database compromise and a legal erasure request - it is what
-- crypto-shredding shreds (see EncryptionKey and ForgetSubject).
-- RoleAccessMapping.scope, the second, decides which whole records a
-- grant reaches at all. This one does neither. It decides, per field and
-- at read time, whether an ordinary application-level reader sees a leaf
-- that was stored in plaintext all along.
--
-- Nothing here involves EncryptionKey, resolve_data_key or ForgetSubject
-- anywhere, and that is the whole point of it rather than an omission. A
-- private field is not about surviving a stolen database or answering an
-- erasure request; it is about who, among the callers this library
-- already authenticates, is meant to look at a field. So it needs no key
-- to provision, none to destroy, and nothing to be undone when a subject
-- is forgotten.
--
-- What it does at read time is the identical shape to what
-- render_event/render_command already do for a sensitive field, one rule
-- along: for a caller who is not entitled, the JSON leaf is replaced by
-- null, exactly where an unentitled caller already finds a sensitive
-- field's leaf left as stored ciphertext. One leaf, and only that leaf -
-- never the enclosing object, never the whole record, and never withheld
-- rather than redacted (see the render_event note above rule
-- DeliverToSubscriptions for the full three-rule test).
--
-- A payload field is at most one of the three. private_fields may not
-- overlap tag_mappings, and may not overlap sensitive_fields either, both
-- refused at registration and for the same leak reason the existing
-- tag_mappings/sensitive_fields overlap is - see the note above rule
-- RegisterEventType.
value PrivateField {
    field: String                -- name of the payload field to redact from a reader
                                  -- who is not entitled to it. Written under the same
                                  -- convention SensitiveField.field is: a bare
                                  -- top-level field name or a two-segment dotted path
                                  -- reaching one leaf inside a nested shape (e.g.
                                  -- address.country) - never deeper, and never a
                                  -- nested-object-typed field itself, since a whole
                                  -- nested object cannot be made private any more than
                                  -- it can be made sensitive (see the payload schema
                                  -- shape note above entity CommandType)
    kind: PrivateFieldKind       -- which of the three visibility rules this field
                                  -- follows - see the enum for what each means
    team: String?                -- the Role.name this field is visible to, and set
                                  -- exactly when kind = team; null for the other two
                                  -- kinds. Checked at every registration by
                                  -- valid_private_fields (see rule RegisterEventType)
    addressee_field: String?     -- name of the payload field this type reads the
                                  -- addressee's own identifier from - the identical
                                  -- role SensitiveField.subject_field plays for a
                                  -- sensitive field's subject, addressed with the same
                                  -- dotted-path convention, and matched at read time
                                  -- against Role.external_subject the same way a
                                  -- sensitive field's subject already is. Set exactly
                                  -- when kind = addressed; null for the other two
                                  -- kinds, and checked the same way
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum FilterOperator { equals | contains | is_like | greater_than | less_than | near | similar_color | in_subnet | in }

enum AccessLevel { read | write | admin }

enum AckMode { auto_advance | manual_ack }

-- Where an EventReadToken's very first ConsumeEvents call begins reading.
-- Named rather than inline for the same reason AckMode beside it is: one
-- rule takes it as an argument and records it (CreateEventReadToken) and
-- another compares against it much later (ConsumeEvents), so it reads as
-- a type rather than as two coincidentally matching literal lists. The
-- four values are defined in full on variant EventReadToken.start_from,
-- and the two that need a value to go with them - at_sequence and
-- at_time - take it from EventReadToken.start_at_sequence and
-- EventReadToken.start_at_time beside it.
enum EventReadStartPosition { beginning | latest | at_sequence | at_time }

-- Which visibility rule a PrivateField follows. Named rather than inline
-- because three separate things read it: valid_private_fields at
-- registration, is_default_private_reader at grant time and at read time,
-- and the render step itself when it decides what to redact.
--
--   - own. Visible by default only to whoever created the record: the
--     Role.id sitting in Event.metadata.client_id or
--     Command.metadata.client_id, which is access_mapping.role.id for
--     anything a GraphQL caller produced (see rule
--     AuthoriseCommandSubmission). Shareable, and shareable by that
--     creator alone: it may grant another Role read access to one
--     specific record or to every record of its own it has made or will
--     make, and revoke that again later.
--   - team. Visible to any Role whose own name equals the fixed string
--     the field declares in PrivateField.team. Nothing is granted and
--     nothing needs to be: membership is the grant, instantly and
--     automatically, and it stops the moment the Role is no longer named
--     that. A "staff-only" field is one instance of this rather than a
--     mechanism of its own.
--   - addressed. Visible by default to the one other party the payload
--     itself names, read from the second payload field
--     PrivateField.addressee_field points at and compared against the
--     reader's own Role.external_subject - the same identifier space, and
--     the same comparison, a sensitive field's subject already uses (see
--     the render_event note above rule DeliverToSubscriptions). Shareable
--     exactly as own is, by the addressee once the payload has determined
--     who that is.
--
-- Each of the three is decidable from the stored record and the reading
-- caller's own identity alone. Nothing else is consulted - no projection,
-- no other record, no clock - which is what makes them cheap enough to
-- evaluate on every field of every record a read returns.
--
-- A fourth kind, draft - a field visible only until some external status
-- changes - is a real and deliberately deferred future kind rather than
-- one considered and rejected. What defers it is its shape rather than
-- its worth: answering it needs a live lookup into Projection state at
-- the moment of every read, so its answer can change while nothing about
-- the record or the caller has changed at all. That is a materially
-- different evaluability register from the three above, with its own
-- questions about staleness and about what a read owes when the
-- projection it depends on is lagging, and it is left whole for a later
-- pass rather than built halfway here. Nothing in this file is shaped
-- around it and nothing in this file forecloses it.
enum PrivateFieldKind { own | team | addressed }

-- What a scheduled EventType owes for an occurrence of its own schedule
-- that passed while nothing was watching for it. Named rather than inline
-- because two rules compare against it (CreateSystemEvent and
-- SkipMissedOccurrences) and a third reconciles it (RegisterEventType).
--
-- There is deliberately no default value, here or anywhere else: both
-- directions are dangerous enough that this library is not entitled to
-- guess on a bounded context's behalf - a skipped nightly reconciliation
-- is a day's drift nobody was told about, and a replayed backlog of a
-- per-minute schedule is thousands of events a downstream consumer never
-- expected. A registration that opts into scheduling without choosing is
-- rejected rather than defaulted (see rule RegisterEventType); a
-- registration that does not opt in is not asked to choose at all. The
-- three are defined in full in the note above rule CreateSystemEvent.
enum MissedOccurrencePolicy { skip | fire_once | replay_backlog }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

entity BoundedContext {
    name: String
    status: active | archived              -- archived: no longer accepts new commands/events, but its
                                            -- history is retained (event sourcing is append-only;
                                            -- archiving deactivates a bounded context, it never
                                            -- erases one). Erasing is a separate, later, deliberate
                                            -- act, and archiving is its precondition rather than a
                                            -- softer form of it - see rule DeleteBoundedContext
    created_at: Timestamp                  -- when this context came into being: the moment
                                            -- AddBoundedContext was called, for every context an
                                            -- admin adds, and the moment of SkilJ's own first
                                            -- startup for the admin context (see the note above the
                                            -- Defaults section)
    created_by: ContextCreator             -- on whose authority it came into being - a real
                                            -- superadmin Role, or SkilJ itself for the admin
                                            -- context alone (see entity ContextCreator below).
                                            -- Recorded rather than derived: creation is not itself a
                                            -- Command or Event yet (see the note above the admin
                                            -- rules), so without these two fields nothing anywhere
                                            -- says when a context appeared or on whose authority
    template: BoundedContext?              -- the context whose declared types this one was stamped
                                            -- out from, set by rule
                                            -- CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate and by nothing else.
                                            -- Absent for every context AddBoundedContext makes and
                                            -- for the admin context, which is the ordinary case.
                                            --
                                            -- Chosen once, exactly as name is, and for the same
                                            -- reason: there is no retemplating any more than there
                                            -- is a rename, so a context is a tenant of one template
                                            -- or of none, decided at the moment it comes into being.
                                            -- No rule points this field at a different context than
                                            -- the one it was created with, which is what makes that
                                            -- hold rather than a sentence asking for it.
                                            --
                                            -- The one write after creation clears it rather than
                                            -- moves it: DeleteBoundedContext sets this to null on
                                            -- every context templated from the one it deletes, so a
                                            -- tenant whose template is gone becomes an ordinary
                                            -- untemplated context rather than pointing at nothing
                                            -- (see the note above that rule). The link ends with its
                                            -- target; it never changes target.
                                            --
                                            -- Being a template is not a state a context enters:
                                            -- nothing here marks a context as one, and a context is
                                            -- a template only for as long as some other context's
                                            -- field points at it. What is forbidden is the second
                                            -- level - a context with a template of its own can never
                                            -- be another's (see invariant
                                            -- TemplateIsNeverItselfTemplated and the note above rule
                                            -- CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate). In every other
                                            -- respect a templated context is an ordinary bounded
                                            -- context: it registers, evolves, archives and is
                                            -- deleted exactly like any other, and what it inherited
                                            -- becomes its own the instant it is registered

    event_types: EventType with bounded_context = this
    command_types: CommandType with bounded_context = this
    commands: Command with bounded_context = this
    events: Event with bounded_context = this
    projections: Projection with bounded_context = this
    subscriptions: Subscription with bounded_context = this
    access_mappings: RoleAccessMapping with bounded_context = this
    encryption_keys: EncryptionKey with bounded_context = this

    transitions status {
        active -> archived
        terminal: archived
    }
}

-- Who a BoundedContext was created by. The two cases differ in kind
-- rather than one being a missing instance of the other: every bounded
-- context an admin adds is created by a real superadmin Role that called
-- AddBoundedContext, while the admin bounded context is created by SkilJ
-- itself, which is not a Role and cannot be made into one - a Role is an
-- external IdP identity that can authenticate (see entity Role and the
-- GraphQL identity resolution note above it), and the library is not
-- that.
--
-- Same shape, and the same reason, as Event.origin's SystemTriggered
-- variant: when the system itself is the originator there is no external
-- identity to point back at, so that case is modelled as its own variant
-- rather than as a null Role whose meaning lives only in prose. What a
-- superadmin reads in BoundedContextDirectory is therefore a real value
-- for every context, the admin one included.
entity ContextCreator {
    kind: SuperadminCreator | SystemCreator
}

variant SuperadminCreator : ContextCreator {
    role: Role                  -- the superadmin Role that called AddBoundedContext
}

variant SystemCreator : ContextCreator {
    -- No variant-specific fields, exactly as SystemTriggered has none:
    -- SkilJ is the creator, so there is no Role to point at. Exactly one
    -- instance exists - the `skilj` default - and it creates exactly one
    -- thing, the admin bounded context (see
    -- SystemCreatedContextIsTheAdminContext).
}

-- Payload schema shape, shared by CommandType.schema and
-- EventType.schema below. An event or command payload schema describes a
-- structure whose leaves are all scalar and whose nesting is capped at
-- exactly one level. Each top-level field is declared as one of three
-- things: a scalar, which may be declared optional; a list of scalars; or
-- a named, reusable nested object shape (e.g. address: Address). A nested
-- shape's own fields must themselves be scalars or lists of scalars -
-- there is no second level of nesting inside it - and a nested shape may
-- only be the type of a single field, never the element type of a list,
-- so there are no lists of nested objects. What the one permitted level
-- buys is reuse: one shape declared once and embedded by several event
-- and command types. It is not a licence for arbitrary structure.
--
-- TagMapping.field, SensitiveField.field, SensitiveField.subject_field
-- and Filter.field each name a target within that shape, written one of
-- two ways: a bare top-level field name, exactly as before, or a
-- two-segment dotted path reaching one leaf inside a nested shape (e.g.
-- address.country). Two segments is the deepest a path can ever be, and
-- that is structural rather than a rule to remember - the one-level
-- nesting cap leaves nothing at a third segment to address.
--
-- What is still not a target is a nested-object-typed field itself. There
-- is no tagging, protecting or filtering by "the whole address"; only by
-- a scalar leaf within it. valid_tag_mappings, valid_sensitive_fields and
-- valid_filters resolve a dotted path by walking one level into the named
-- shape's own declared fields, and reject anything that does not land on
-- a scalar or list-of-scalar leaf - the same existing check as for a bare
-- top-level name, resolved one level deeper, not a second mechanism. A
-- bounded context therefore no longer needs a separate, parallel
-- top-level field to tag, protect or filter by something that lives
-- inside a reusable shape: the shape can be reused and its leaves
-- addressed, rather than reuse being bought at the cost of addressing.
--
-- For sensitivity specifically, what gets encrypted is the leaf and only
-- the leaf. protect_sensitive_fields replaces that one leaf's value with
-- ciphertext in place, leaving the rest of the enclosing object and the
-- surrounding payload structurally untouched (see its note above the
-- rules). That is the correct shape, and marking the whole nested object
-- sensitive would be the wrong one: encrypting an object would make its
-- declared type depend on the reader - an object for an entitled one, a
-- ciphertext string for everyone else - which breaks the
-- field-type-immutability rule the compatibility contract already fixes
-- (see the fourth bullet of schema_is_backwards_compatible in the note
-- above RegisterEventType). A leaf has no such problem: a scalar keeps
-- the same declared type whether it holds plaintext or ciphertext,
-- exactly as today's flat sensitive fields already work.
--
-- The nesting cap itself is a stated expectation on what a bounded
-- context's schema may declare, not something this spec enforces - the
-- same register the flat-only convention it widens was already stated
-- in. A
-- schema is an opaque JSON Schema string here and is never structurally
-- parsed at this level (see the black boxes that do parse it:
-- valid_tag_mappings, valid_sensitive_fields, valid_filters,
-- valid_schema, valid_payload and schema_is_backwards_compatible), so
-- nothing in this
-- file can express
-- "at most one level of nesting" as a check. A bounded context that
-- declares a deeper schema anyway is outside the contract, and what its
-- tags, sensitive fields and filters would mean there is undefined rather
-- than defined-and-forbidden. What is enforced is the other side of the
-- same coin: a path that does not resolve to a scalar or list-of-scalar
-- leaf within one level is rejected at registration or call time by the
-- valid_* black boxes above, so a schema that stays inside the contract
-- cannot address its way out of it.
--
-- Schema evolution is separately constrained, and that one is enforced:
-- see schema_is_backwards_compatible in the note above RegisterEventType.
-- Its contract governs the top-level field only; a nested shape's own
-- internal evolution is not tracked there. The exception is a leaf some
-- TagMapping, SensitiveField or Filter addresses, which cannot be dropped
-- from its shape - pinned by the valid_* existence check rather than by
-- that contract. See that note for both halves.
--
-- valid_payload(schema, payload) answers the one question about an
-- individual payload this spec does enforce: does payload, read as JSON,
-- validate against the JSON Schema string schema. It is a black box in
-- the same register as valid_tag_mappings, valid_sensitive_fields and
-- valid_filters - this library owns that a payload not matching the
-- schema its type registered is rejected outright rather than stored and
-- discovered later by whoever reads it back, not how a JSON Schema
-- validator walks a document. Deterministic in the ordinary way: the same
-- schema and the same payload always give the same verdict.
--
-- Its schema-only half is valid_schema(schema), which asks whether schema
-- is a usable JSON Schema at all, with nothing to validate against it -
-- see the note above rule RegisterEventType.
--
-- It is checked wherever a caller-supplied payload crosses into the
-- system, and only there: CreateExternalEvent and CreateDirectEvent for
-- an event's payload, and AuthoriseCommandSubmission and
-- AuthoriseCommandTrigger for a command's - at authorisation, before
-- ProcessCommand and decide() ever see it, which is the same boundary
-- crossing the two event rules check at. A payload produced by a bounded
-- context's own compiled-in code carries the same obligation but is not
-- re-checked at runtime: see decide()'s EventSpec payloads in
-- ProcessCommand, and scheduled_payload in the note above rule
-- CreateSystemEvent.
--
-- What it checks is conformance to whatever schema that type actually
-- registered, and nothing beyond that. It says nothing about whether the
-- schema itself stays inside the one-level-nesting cap stated above: that
-- cap remains an expectation this spec does not enforce, so a payload
-- matching a deeper schema is a conforming payload for that type. The two
-- questions are independent, and this is only ever the instance one.

entity CommandType {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    name: String
    schema: String              -- JSON Schema describing the command's payload fields and optionality;
                                -- scalar leaves, at most one level of reusable nesting - see the
                                -- payload schema shape note above
    schema_version: Integer     -- increments on each schema change; changes must stay backwards
                                -- compatible, enforced by schema_is_backwards_compatible at
                                -- registration (see the note above RegisterEventType)

    -- Mirrors EventType.tag_mappings: derives this command's
    -- consistency_tags from its own payload, using the same shared keys
    -- events are tagged with, so the two are directly comparable.
    tag_mappings: Set<TagMapping>

    -- Names which of this type's own tag_mappings keys above is its
    -- "owner" dimension, in the identical shape and with the identical
    -- meaning EventType.owner_tag_key has: a pointer into tag_mappings
    -- rather than a second declaration standing beside it, and the key
    -- named here must be one those mappings actually produce, checked at
    -- every registration by the very same valid_owner_tag_key (see rule
    -- RegisterCommandType). Null - which is the default, what every
    -- registration omitting it holds, and what every type registered
    -- before this field existed holds - means the type has no owner
    -- dimension at all: commands of this type are unrestricted by any
    -- grant's scope, whatever that scope holds (see
    -- RoleAccessMapping.scope). A command's owner value is derived from
    -- its own payload exactly as every other tag is - it is the entry
    -- under this key in Command.consistency_tags, never caller-supplied,
    -- and never supplied by the caller reading it either (see the
    -- derive_tags note above the rules, and rule FetchCommands' guidance
    -- on command_owner_scope_satisfied for the read-time contract).
    --
    -- There is no token-side counterpart to RoleAccessMapping.scope here
    -- the way EventReadToken.scope is one on the event side: the REST
    -- track reads no commands back at all (see surface CommandQuery).
    owner_tag_key: String?

    -- Opt-in, in the same shape as EventType.sensitive_fields and using
    -- the identical SensitiveField convention, so a subject named by a
    -- command resolves to the very same EncryptionKey an event naming it
    -- resolves to (see EncryptionKey and the protect_sensitive_fields
    -- note above the rules). A command's payload is stored permanently
    -- too, and CommandQuery reads it back. Adding an entry here only
    -- affects commands submitted from this point forward - it does not
    -- retroactively encrypt this type's existing history, since a stored
    -- command is never rewritten. See Command.encryption_keys to tell
    -- which historical commands predate a given entry (and so still hold
    -- that field in plaintext). Nothing ever protects that plaintext
    -- later, and nothing short of deleting the whole bounded context
    -- removes it: there is no backfill or purge path, so this must
    -- be declared before sensitive data starts flowing through the type
    -- (see the protect_sensitive_fields note above the rules).
    sensitive_fields: Set<SensitiveField>

    -- Opt-in, in the same shape as EventType.private_fields and standing
    -- beside sensitive_fields above rather than inside it: a plain
    -- read-time redaction rule with no encryption and no erasure anywhere
    -- behind it (see value PrivateField). Unlike sensitive_fields, this
    -- declaration is not a fact about how a command was stored - a
    -- private field is stored in plaintext, whenever it was submitted -
    -- so adding, changing or removing an entry here changes what every
    -- reader sees of this type's whole history immediately, not merely of
    -- the commands submitted next. There is nothing on a stored Command
    -- to tell historical ones apart the way Command.encryption_keys does
    -- for sensitivity, and nothing that needs to be.
    private_fields: Set<PrivateField>

    -- Opt-in, mirroring EventType.external_creation_allowed: submitting
    -- this command over GraphQL with a write-level RoleAccessMapping is
    -- always permitted, but letting an automated caller trigger it over
    -- REST with a CommandToken requires explicit opt-in per command type.
    rest_trigger_allowed: Boolean
    command_tokens: CommandToken with command_type = this
}

entity EventType {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    name: String
    schema: String              -- JSON Schema describing the event's payload fields and optionality;
                                -- scalar leaves, at most one level of reusable nesting - see the
                                -- payload schema shape note above entity CommandType
    schema_version: Integer     -- increments on each schema change; changes must stay backwards
                                -- compatible, enforced by schema_is_backwards_compatible at
                                -- registration (see the note above RegisterEventType)

    -- Deliberately opt-in and small: each mapping promotes one payload
    -- field to an indexed, queryable tag. Only mapped fields are
    -- indexed - not every field - to keep the index footprint bounded.
    tag_mappings: Set<TagMapping>

    -- Names which of this type's own tag_mappings keys above is its
    -- "owner" dimension - "company", say, where every event of the type
    -- carries the company it belongs to under that key. Null - which is
    -- the default, what every registration omitting it holds, and what
    -- every type registered before this field existed holds - means the
    -- type has no owner dimension at all: events of this type are
    -- unrestricted by any grant's or token's scope, whatever that scope
    -- holds (see RoleAccessMapping.scope, EventReadToken.scope, and rule
    -- QueryEvents' guidance on event_owner_scope_satisfied for the
    -- read-time contract). A pointer into tag_mappings rather than a
    -- second declaration standing beside it: the key named here must be
    -- one those mappings actually produce, checked at every registration
    -- by valid_owner_tag_key (see rule RegisterEventType). An event's
    -- owner value is therefore derived from its own payload exactly as
    -- every other tag is - never caller-supplied, and never supplied by
    -- the caller reading it either (see the derive_tags note above the
    -- rules).
    owner_tag_key: String?

    -- Opt-in, mirrors tag_mappings in shape. Adding an entry here only
    -- affects events created from this point forward - it does not
    -- retroactively encrypt this type's existing history, since events
    -- are immutable. See Event.encryption_keys to tell which historical
    -- events predate a given entry (and so still hold that field in
    -- plaintext). Nothing ever protects that plaintext later, and nothing
    -- short of deleting the whole bounded context removes it: there is
    -- no backfill or purge path, so this must be declared
    -- before sensitive data starts flowing through the type (see the
    -- protect_sensitive_fields note above the rules).
    sensitive_fields: Set<SensitiveField>

    -- Opt-in, mirrors sensitive_fields in shape and stands beside it
    -- rather than inside it: a plain read-time redaction rule, with no
    -- encryption, no EncryptionKey and no erasure anywhere behind it (see
    -- value PrivateField). The difference from sensitive_fields that
    -- matters most is in which direction each reaches. Marking a field
    -- sensitive protects the future only, never the past, because
    -- protection happens at write time and events are immutable. Marking
    -- one private reaches the past and the future alike, because
    -- protection happens at read time: adding, changing or removing an
    -- entry here changes what every reader sees of this type's whole
    -- history from that moment on. That is the same live-declaration
    -- treatment owner_tag_key above already has, and for the same reason
    -- - nothing about it is snapshotted onto an event when the event is
    -- written, so there is no Event.encryption_keys equivalent to tell
    -- historical events apart, and none is needed.
    private_fields: Set<PrivateField>

    -- Command-triggered origin is always permitted; the other three
    -- origins require explicit opt-in per event type.
    external_creation_allowed: Boolean
    external_creation_tokens: ExternalEventToken with event_type = this
    direct_creation_allowed: Boolean
    direct_creation_tokens: DirectCreationToken with event_type = this
    system_triggered_allowed: Boolean
    system_triggered_schedule: String?     -- cron expression, evaluated by the core's own in-process
                                            -- scheduler; the core cannot rely on an OS-level cron

    -- What this type owes for an occurrence that passed while nothing was
    -- watching for it. Optional as a field because a type that has not
    -- opted into scheduling has no use for one, and mandatory the moment
    -- it has: a registration with system_triggered_allowed = true must
    -- state one, and is rejected rather than defaulted when it does not
    -- (see rule RegisterEventType and invariant
    -- ScheduledTypeIsFullyConfigured). Read back by an admin alongside the
    -- two positions below - see surface TypeRegistration.
    missed_occurrence_policy: MissedOccurrencePolicy?

    -- How far this type's schedule has been accounted for: every
    -- occurrence at or before this instant has either produced an event or
    -- been deliberately passed over, and none of them is considered again.
    -- Not necessarily an occurrence instant itself - closing a gap under
    -- the skip policy moves it to the moment the gap was closed (see rule
    -- SkipMissedOccurrences).
    --
    -- Durable, registered state of the type rather than of a process: one
    -- shared value per event type, exactly as Projection.caught_up_to and
    -- ReadCursor.sequence are, and for the same reason - several instances
    -- of the same service run against one database and must not each hold
    -- their own idea of what has fired. Anchored to the moment scheduling
    -- is opted into, and never reset by a later re-registration, so a
    -- deploy is not a way to re-fire or to skip (see rule
    -- RegisterEventType).
    schedule_position: Timestamp?

    -- The occurrence instant of the most recent occurrence of this
    -- type's schedule that actually produced an event, and the answer to
    -- "when did this last fire" an admin reads on surface TypeRegistration.
    -- Null until the first one fires, and left standing when scheduling is
    -- switched off: what once fired stays a fact.
    --
    -- The occurrence's instant, not the wall clock of the write. The two
    -- differ exactly when a firing is a catch-up: an occurrence due at
    -- 03:00 fired at 09:00 under fire_once records 03:00 here and 09:00 in
    -- its event's Metadata.created_at. Never ahead of schedule_position,
    -- which accounts for every occurrence this one is among (see invariant
    -- FiredOccurrenceIsAccountedFor).
    last_fired_at: Timestamp?

    -- Opt-in in the same shape as the origin permissions above, but for
    -- reading rather than writing: whether events of this type may be
    -- fetched over REST with an EventReadToken.
    event_read_allowed: Boolean
    event_read_tokens: EventReadToken with event_type = this
}

entity AccessToken {
    id: String                             -- non-secret identifier, safe to log and store on events
    secret: String                         -- opaque bearer credential (distinct from id), stored
                                            -- hashed and never compared in plaintext - see the REST
                                            -- token presentation note above the surfaces
    status: active | revoked
    created_at: Timestamp
    revoked_at: Timestamp? when status = revoked
    purpose: ExternalEventToken | DirectCreationToken | CommandToken | EventReadToken

    -- The bearer credential of the REST track. Each variant is scoped to
    -- exactly one registered type - an EventType for the three
    -- event-shaped variants, a CommandType for CommandToken - and holding
    -- one authorises exactly one capability against that single type,
    -- nothing else. The scoping field therefore lives on the variant, not
    -- here: the base only carries what every token has regardless of what
    -- it points at. Tokens are minted and revoked by an admin over
    -- GraphQL; lifecycle is tracked by the admin bounded context via
    -- TokenCreated/TokenRevoked.
    transitions status {
        active -> revoked
        terminal: revoked
    }
}

variant ExternalEventToken : AccessToken {
    -- Held by an out-of-core adapter (e.g. a Kafka client, which needs OS
    -- facilities the core deliberately avoids) to authenticate against
    -- ExternalEventIngestion and fail independently if its own
    -- dependency is unavailable.
    event_type: EventType

    -- The REST track's counterpart to RoleAccessMapping.scope, in the same
    -- register and with the same meaning as EventReadToken.scope below, but
    -- restricting creation rather than reads: where event_type above says
    -- which type this token may submit, this one narrows which of that
    -- type's events it may create. Null - which is what every token holds
    -- unless the minting admin named a value (see rule
    -- CreateExternalEventToken) - means unrestricted within that type,
    -- exactly as every token created before this field existed. A value
    -- means this token may create only those events whose own derived owner
    -- value equals it, and only for an event type that declares an owner
    -- dimension at all; a type declaring none is unaffected by this field
    -- whatever it holds (see EventType.owner_tag_key). The value itself is
    -- an opaque caller-chosen string this library compares for equality and
    -- never interprets, the same treatment RoleAccessMapping.scope gets
    -- (see rule AuthoriseCommandSubmission's guidance on
    -- tag_owner_scope_satisfied for the full contract, and rule
    -- CreateExternalEvent for where it is applied).
    scope: String?
}

variant DirectCreationToken : AccessToken {
    event_type: EventType

    -- Exactly ExternalEventToken.scope above, on the same terms and with
    -- the same null meaning, applied to direct creation instead of external
    -- ingestion - see rule CreateDirectEvent for where it is checked and
    -- rule CreateDirectCreationToken for where it is set.
    scope: String?
}

variant CommandToken : AccessToken {
    -- Held by an AI or otherwise automated caller that triggers a command
    -- over REST rather than over GraphQL. The command itself is processed
    -- exactly as a GraphQL-submitted one (see ProcessCommand); only the
    -- authentication differs.
    command_type: CommandType

    -- ExternalEventToken.scope above, on the command side: it narrows which
    -- of its one command type's commands this token may trigger. Null, as
    -- every token holds unless the minting admin named a value (see rule
    -- CreateCommandToken), means unrestricted within that type. A value
    -- means only commands whose own derived owner value equals it may be
    -- triggered, and only for a command type declaring an owner dimension
    -- at all (see CommandType.owner_tag_key); the value is opaque and
    -- compared for equality alone. See rule AuthoriseCommandTrigger for
    -- where it is checked.
    scope: String?
}

variant EventReadToken : AccessToken {
    -- Held by a remote workflow that fetches events of one type to drive
    -- itself. Read-only: it authorises no writing of any kind.
    event_type: EventType

    -- The REST track's counterpart to RoleAccessMapping.scope, in the
    -- same register and with the same meaning: where event_type above
    -- says which type this token may read, this one narrows which of that
    -- type's events it may read. Null - which is what every token holds
    -- unless the minting admin named a value (see rule
    -- CreateEventReadToken) - means unrestricted within that type,
    -- exactly as every token read before this field existed. A value
    -- means this token may fetch only those events whose own derived
    -- owner value equals it, and only for an event type that declares an
    -- owner dimension at all; a type declaring none is unaffected by this
    -- field whatever it holds (see EventType.owner_tag_key). The value
    -- itself is an opaque caller-chosen string this library compares for
    -- equality and never interprets, the same treatment
    -- RoleAccessMapping.scope gets (see rule QueryEvents' guidance on
    -- event_owner_scope_satisfied for the full contract).
    scope: String?

    -- Where this token's very first ConsumeEvents call begins, and the
    -- only thing this field ever decides. beginning - which is what every
    -- token holds unless the minting admin named another one (see rule
    -- CreateEventReadToken) - starts that first call at position = -1,
    -- before the first sequence of the bounded context, which is exactly
    -- where every token minted before this field existed already starts.
    -- latest starts it past everything already committed instead, so the
    -- token's first call skips straight to what is committed from that
    -- call onwards; see rule ConsumeEvents' own latest_position binding
    -- for the precise position that resolves to. at_sequence and at_time
    -- start it at a place the minting admin named rather than at either
    -- end of history, and each reads that place from one of the two
    -- fields below.
    --
    -- It says where in history a token starts, never what it may read.
    -- event_type and scope above are untouched by it, and so is every
    -- filter, redaction and opt-in either read applies: a latest token
    -- reaches nothing a beginning one could not, it simply does not go
    -- back for what it was minted after, and the same holds of the other
    -- two. Consulted on that first call alone and never again - from the
    -- second call on all four kinds of token are indistinguishable.
    -- FetchEvents never consults it at all, since that read carries the
    -- caller's own cursor rather than a server-side one.
    start_from: EventReadStartPosition

    -- The starting position an at_sequence token reads directly, and the
    -- whole of what at_sequence means: the token's first call continues
    -- strictly after this number, exactly as any later call continues
    -- strictly after the position its cursor already holds.
    --
    -- Opaque and unvalidated, the same treatment scope above gets: it is
    -- a caller-chosen number this library records and later compares
    -- against, with no check that it names a real event, an event of this
    -- token's own type, or an event this token is scoped to. A number
    -- past everything committed simply serves nothing until the stream
    -- reaches it, and a negative one behaves as beginning does.
    start_at_sequence: Integer?

    -- The point in time an at_time token derives its starting position
    -- from - not a position itself, but the cutoff one is computed from:
    -- the highest sequence among this token's own event type whose
    -- metadata.created_at is at or before this value, scoped exactly as
    -- latest's own seed is. See rule ConsumeEvents' at_time_position
    -- binding for that computation in full.
    start_at_time: Timestamp?

    -- Exactly one of the two fields above is populated, and only when
    -- start_from names the value that reads it: beginning and latest
    -- leave both null, at_sequence populates start_at_sequence alone and
    -- at_time populates start_at_time alone. Rule CreateEventReadToken is
    -- what holds that true - a token in any other shape is one this
    -- library never mints - on the same terms the kind-specific fields of
    -- the other AccessToken variants are held true, by what construction
    -- is allowed to produce rather than by an invariant restating it.
}

-- The bootstrap secret: the only credential SkilJ ever generates for
-- itself, and the only way the first superadmin Role comes into being. The
-- mechanism is spelled out above rule CreateSuperadmin; what this
-- declaration answers is why it is a construct of its own rather than a
-- fifth AccessToken variant.
--
-- It is presented the way an AccessToken is - a bearer secret rather than
-- a resolved identity - and that is the whole of the resemblance. Every
-- property that shapes AccessToken is wrong here. An AccessToken is scoped
-- to exactly one registered type inside exactly one bounded context, while
-- superadmin is orthogonal to bounded contexts entirely (see
-- RoleAccessMapping). An AccessToken is minted over GraphQL by an admin
-- who already exists, while this one is generated by SkilJ before any Role
-- exists to mint anything. And an AccessToken is a persisted row with a
-- status, revoked individually through the TokenRevocation surface, while
-- this one is held in memory only, never written to Postgres at all, and
-- ends either at the next restart or at the moment the first superadmin
-- exists - neither of which is a revocation anyone performs. Making it a
-- variant would inherit scoping, persistence and revocation machinery,
-- none of which applies to it.
--
-- There is no id half either. An AccessToken is presented as
-- "<id>.<secret>" so the server can look its row up by primary key before
-- verifying the secret (see the REST token presentation note above the
-- surfaces); there is at most one of these at a time and no row to look
-- up, so the secret is the whole credential.
entity BootstrapSecret {
    secret: String                         -- high-entropy, generated fresh at each process start for
                                            -- as long as no active superadmin Role exists, printed
                                            -- once to SkilJ's own startup log and held nowhere but
                                            -- that process's memory. How it is generated is a black
                                            -- box, the same register as generate_token_secret.
}

-- GraphQL identity resolution. SkilJ does not authenticate GraphQL
-- callers itself; it delegates to a trusted external identity provider
-- and only authorises. On every GraphQL operation:
--   1. The caller presents a JWT the IdP issued. SkilJ verifies that
--      JWT's signature against the IdP's JWKS - a black box here, the
--      same register as protect_sensitive_fields and
--      generate_token_secret.
--   2. SkilJ trusts the subject claim of a verified JWT, and nothing
--      else in it.
--   3. That claim value resolves to the one active Role whose
--      external_subject matches it (see UniqueActiveExternalSubject,
--      which is what makes "the one" well defined).
--   4. That Role's RoleAccessMappings are resolved live, from current
--      state - never from anything carried in the JWT. The JWT
--      establishes identity only; it never carries authorisation.
--
-- This is safe because every rule already re-checks Role.status and
-- RoleAccessMapping.status = active freshly on each operation (it did
-- so before this delegation too). Revoking a Role or one of its
-- RoleAccessMappings therefore takes effect immediately, regardless of
-- how much validity the external JWT itself has left, and no denylist
-- of outstanding JWTs is needed.
--
-- What "the trusted IdP" resolves to concretely is runtime/library
-- configuration, not domain state. A deployment trusts exactly one
-- external IdP, and that IdP's JWKS endpoint, issuer, the audience its
-- tokens for this deployment carry, expected signing algorithm, and
-- which claim carries the subject (defaulting to the
-- standard "sub", but configurable) are all set when the process
-- starts. None of them is a registered entity, a field on anything, or
-- an admin-managed value - the same register as the in-memory event
-- cache's startup warm-up count (see the note above the rules), and for
-- the same reason: nothing in the domain reads or writes them, they
-- only parameterise the black-box verification in step 1 above.
--
-- Trusting exactly one IdP is a deliberate deferral, not a placeholder
-- to revisit blindly. Adding a second (or Nth) trusted IdP later is an
-- additive migration, not a breaking one: an issuer field alongside
-- Role.external_subject, defaulted for existing Roles to the single
-- already-configured issuer, and UniqueActiveExternalSubject widening
-- from keying on external_subject alone to keying on the
-- (issuer, external_subject) pair - two IdPs could plausibly issue the
-- same subject value, and the wider key is what keeps "the one active
-- Role" well defined when they do. Nothing already specified changes
-- shape for that: the resolution steps above, the invariant's
-- existence, CreateRole and the AccessManagement surface all stay as
-- they are, and only the key the invariant uniques on gets wider. That
-- is why deferring is safe rather than merely convenient.

entity Role {
    id: String                             -- non-secret identifier, safe to log and store on events
    external_subject: String               -- the subject claim value, from a JWT the trusted external
                                            -- IdP issued, that this Role is bound to. Deliberately
                                            -- separate from id, which is SkilJ's own internal
                                            -- identifier (and what lands in metadata.client_id on
                                            -- GraphQL-originated commands and events): external_subject
                                            -- may carry PII (an email address, say) depending on the
                                            -- IdP's claim shape, so keeping the two distinct means
                                            -- SkilJ's own event history never embeds it, and the
                                            -- internal id stays stable even if the IdP-side identity
                                            -- is re-provisioned.
    name: String
    superadmin: Boolean                    -- grants AddBoundedContext and all Role/RoleAccessMapping
                                            -- management, independently of any RoleAccessMapping below
    status: active | revoked
    created_at: Timestamp
    revoked_at: Timestamp? when status = revoked

    -- A Role reaches multiple bounded contexts by holding multiple
    -- RoleAccessMappings, one per context - there is no single grant that
    -- spans contexts other than superadmin, which is orthogonal to
    -- context scoping entirely (see RoleAccessMapping).
    access_mappings: RoleAccessMapping with role = this

    transitions status {
        active -> revoked
        terminal: revoked
    }
}

entity RoleAccessMapping {
    role: Role
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    level: AccessLevel
    can_read_sensitive: Boolean             -- orthogonal to level: authorises decrypting
                                             -- sensitive fields within this mapping's
                                             -- bounded context. A write or admin grant
                                             -- does not see decrypted sensitive values
                                             -- unless this is also true.
    scope: String?                          -- orthogonal to level too, and to
                                             -- can_read_sensitive beside it: where those two
                                             -- say what this grant may do within its bounded
                                             -- context, this one narrows which projection
                                             -- instances it may read there. Null - which is
                                             -- what every grant holds unless a superadmin
                                             -- named a value (see rules
                                             -- GrantRoleAccessMapping and
                                             -- CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate, the two
                                             -- places a grant comes into being) - means
                                             -- unrestricted within the bounded context,
                                             -- exactly as every grant read before this field
                                             -- existed. A value
                                             -- means this grant may read only those
                                             -- projection instances whose own derived owner
                                             -- value equals it, and only for a projection
                                             -- that declares an owner dimension at all; a
                                             -- projection that declares none is unaffected
                                             -- by this field whatever it holds. The value
                                             -- itself is an opaque caller-chosen string this
                                             -- library compares for equality and never
                                             -- interprets, the same treatment
                                             -- CommandDecision.rejection_kind gets (see rule
                                             -- QueryProjection's guidance on
                                             -- owner_scope_satisfied for the full contract).
    status: active | revoked
    created_at: Timestamp
    revoked_at: Timestamp? when status = revoked

    -- A grant, not a credential in its own right: a GraphQL caller
    -- presents a JWT from the trusted external IdP, which resolves to a
    -- Role (see the identity resolution note above entity Role), and
    -- this records what that Role may do where. Resolved live on every
    -- operation, never carried in the JWT.
    -- Scoped to exactly one BoundedContext: it
    -- authorises the given level within that context only, nothing else.
    -- Levels accumulate: read authorises querying and subscribing; write
    -- includes read and additionally authorises submitting commands;
    -- admin includes write and additionally authorises the
    -- admin-bounded-context mutations that target this specific context
    -- (archiving it, minting/revoking AccessTokens for its own event and
    -- command types) - but not AddBoundedContext, which is gated by
    -- Role.superadmin instead, since creating a context isn't "for" any
    -- existing one.
    transitions status {
        active -> revoked
        terminal: revoked
    }
}

entity EncryptionKey {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    subject_key: String                    -- e.g. "user" - see SensitiveField.subject_key
    subject_value: String                  -- e.g. "123"
    status: active | destroyed
    created_at: Timestamp
    destroyed_at: Timestamp? when status = destroyed

    -- Crypto-shredding: destroying this key is what "forgets" a subject
    -- (see ForgetSubject). Historical events encrypted under it keep
    -- their ciphertext exactly as stored - event sourcing is
    -- append-only, so forgetting never edits or removes an event - the
    -- ciphertext simply becomes permanently unreadable. A later event
    -- for the same subject provisions a new active key; it does not
    -- revive this one.
    transitions status {
        active -> destroyed
        terminal: destroyed
    }
}

entity Command {
    id: String                             -- SkilJ's own internal identifier, assigned at creation and
                                            -- never derived from the command's content, so a stored
                                            -- Command can be pointed at as itself rather than
                                            -- reconstructed from whatever it happens to contain. That is
                                            -- what makes FetchCommands's triggered_event lookup exact: a
                                            -- CommandTriggered event names the Command behind it, and
                                            -- matching that against a candidate is an identity
                                            -- comparison, so two commands carrying identical payload,
                                            -- metadata, tags and boundary stay distinct.
                                            --
                                            -- An idempotency key does not change any of that. A key is
                                            -- something the caller chooses and this library remembers an
                                            -- outcome against; it is never this id, never derived into
                                            -- it, and never a second way to name a stored Command - see
                                            -- rule ProcessCommand.
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    command_type: CommandType
    payload: String                        -- JSON-encoded command data, conforms to command_type.schema
    metadata: Metadata                     -- client_id identifies the caller that submitted this
                                            -- command: the id of the Role behind the
                                            -- RoleAccessMapping over GraphQL (see CommandSubmission),
                                            -- or the id of the CommandToken over REST (see
                                            -- CommandTrigger)
    encryption_keys: Set<EncryptionKey>    -- captured at creation time, exactly as
                                            -- Event.encryption_keys is: one key per distinct
                                            -- subject a sensitive field referenced at that
                                            -- moment. Empty if command_type declared no
                                            -- sensitive fields when this command was
                                            -- submitted - such a command holds those fields in
                                            -- plaintext, even if sensitive_fields later gains
                                            -- an entry for them (see
                                            -- CommandType.sensitive_fields)

    -- Dynamic consistency boundary: the tags this command's decision was
    -- based on, and the highest Event.sequence observed among events
    -- matching those tags at decision time. consistency_boundary is null
    -- when the command does not use a consistency boundary;
    -- consistency_tags is not, and never is. It is
    -- derive_tags(command_type, payload) on every command this library
    -- stores, whether a boundary was used or not (see rule
    -- ProcessCommand), which is what makes it the exact command-side
    -- counterpart to Event.tags rather than a by-product of using a
    -- boundary - and therefore the field a command's owner value is read
    -- from (see rule FetchCommands' guidance on
    -- command_owner_scope_satisfied).
    consistency_tags: Set<Tag>
    consistency_boundary: Integer?

    -- Events produced by this command, stored in the same transaction.
    triggered_events: CommandTriggered with command = this
}

entity Event {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    event_type: EventType
    payload: String                        -- JSON-encoded event data, conforms to event_type.schema
    metadata: Metadata
    sequence: Integer                      -- append position within bounded_context: monotonically
                                            -- increasing, gapless, starting at 0
    tags: Set<Tag>                         -- derived from event_type.tag_mappings at creation time,
                                            -- not caller-supplied
    encryption_keys: Set<EncryptionKey>    -- captured at creation time: one key per distinct
                                            -- subject a sensitive field referenced at that
                                            -- moment. Empty if event_type declared no
                                            -- sensitive fields when this event was created -
                                            -- such an event holds those fields in plaintext,
                                            -- even if sensitive_fields later gains an entry
                                            -- for them (see EventType.sensitive_fields)
    origin: CommandTriggered | ExternalTriggered | DirectlyCreated | SystemTriggered
}

variant CommandTriggered : Event {
    command: Command
}

variant ExternalTriggered : Event {
    -- Both fields below are fully opaque, adapter-defined blobs. SkilJ
    -- persists verbatim whatever the adapter sends and imposes no schema
    -- of its own on them, validates nothing about them and interprets
    -- nothing inside them - a black box in exactly the same register as
    -- EventType.schema, CommandType.schema and Projection.schema, and as
    -- the encryption scheme and JWT signature verification.
    --
    -- Resolved deliberately, and out of scope by design: SkilJ models no
    -- "source type" concept anywhere - not here, not on
    -- ExternalEventToken, nowhere - so there is no per-source-type shape
    -- variance at this level. An adapter or consuming application that
    -- wants a different shape per source encodes that convention inside
    -- the blob itself (a source_type key within the JSON, say); SkilJ
    -- neither tracks nor enforces it. Note also that these two fields sit
    -- outside payload, so protect_sensitive_fields and derive_tags never
    -- touch them (see CreateExternalEvent).
    --
    -- Adapters must therefore not put sensitive data into either field:
    -- plaintext here survives crypto-shredding, since only the payload
    -- fields named in EventType.sensitive_fields are encrypted under a
    -- subject's key and only those are reachable by ForgetSubject. That is
    -- a documented obligation on the adapter, in the same trust register as
    -- declaring sensitive_fields before sensitive data flows - the adapter
    -- is out-of-core code the same deploying team controls (see
    -- ExternalEventToken), not untrusted end-user input. Opacity is the
    -- point, so RegisterEventType has nothing to inspect here the way it
    -- can rule out a TagMapping/SensitiveField overlap structurally.
    -- Encrypting the whole blob under the event's encryption_keys was
    -- rejected: an un-parsed blob declares no subject for an EncryptionKey
    -- to be scoped to, so ForgetSubject could not shred it either. A
    -- per-EventType opt-out refusing to persist source_content at all was
    -- rejected as machinery that prevents nothing on any type not setting
    -- it.
    source_content: String                 -- source-specific payload, e.g. a raw JSON blob
    source_context: String?                -- source-specific provenance data, e.g. a Kafka
                                            -- topic/partition/offset or a webhook's headers
    -- metadata.client_id holds the id of the ExternalEventToken used to submit this event
}

variant DirectlyCreated : Event {
    -- metadata.client_id holds the id of the DirectCreationToken used to create this event
}

variant SystemTriggered : Event {
    -- No variant-specific fields: the scheduler is the originator, so
    -- there is no external token or command to point back at. See
    -- SystemTriggeredClientIdIsSystem for the client_id constraint.
}

entity Projection {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    name: String
    schema: String              -- JSON Schema describing the queryable fields this projection
                                 -- answers with, in exactly the same register as
                                 -- EventType.schema and CommandType.schema: a black box to this
                                 -- spec, derived from the Rust projection definition. It is what
                                 -- the GraphQL type for this projection is generated from, so a
                                 -- projection is introspectable and a client (a mobile app, say)
                                 -- consumes a real, typed subset of the graph rather than
                                 -- parsing an opaque blob.
    schema_version: Integer     -- increments on each schema change; changes must stay backwards
                                 -- compatible, enforced by schema_is_backwards_compatible at
                                 -- registration exactly as an event or command type's schema is
                                 -- (see the note above RegisterEventType)

    -- Which EventTypes feed this projection. Known at compile time,
    -- derived from the Rust projection definition the same way
    -- EventType/CommandType schemas are, and reconciled here by
    -- RegisterProjection. Knowing it statically is what lets an event
    -- being created inline find exactly the sync projections it must
    -- update, without a runtime discovery step.
    consumed_event_types: Set<EventType>

    -- Opt-in, and deliberate friction: use with care. A sync projection
    -- is updated inline, in the same transaction as the event that feeds
    -- it, so it is never stale - but its update cost is paid on the write
    -- path of every event it consumes, and a failure there fails the
    -- write. The default (false) updates asynchronously instead, off the
    -- write path, at the cost of possible lag (see caught_up_to).
    sync: Boolean

    -- Highest Event.sequence within bounded_context this projection has
    -- processed - whether or not that event's type is one it consumes, so
    -- it tracks position in the stream rather than only the events that
    -- changed something. Null before it has processed anything. A sync
    -- projection is always at the bounded context's latest committed
    -- sequence by construction (see SyncProjectionNeverLags); an async one
    -- may trail, which is exactly what a query's wait_for_sequence waits
    -- past.
    caught_up_to: Integer?

    -- A projection's own instance data may be a single value or many,
    -- each independently addressed by a caller-supplied key (see
    -- QueryProjection): one row per customer for a purchase history, one
    -- row per course for its participants, or exactly one unnamed
    -- instance holding a single shared value - which is what a projection
    -- nobody ever names a key against is. Which key or keys a given event
    -- updates is decided by project() itself, from the event, in exactly
    -- the same black-box register as what it computes: this library owns
    -- when a projection is updated and what a caller may then see, not
    -- how its instances are chosen or folded (see the project() note
    -- above the rules). That multiplicity lives in the instance data the
    -- fold produces, not in the projection's definition, so there is no
    -- key field here and nothing extra for RegisterProjection to
    -- reconcile.

    -- The schema's own contents stay a black box here, the same as
    -- EventType.schema. Sensitivity is declared once, on the source
    -- EventType (see EventType.sensitive_fields) - a projection built
    -- from a sensitive field inherits the same restriction rather than
    -- re-declaring it (see ProjectionQuery).

    -- The rebuilds staged or running against this projection: at most one
    -- pending and one building at a time
    -- (UniqueRebuildPerProjectionAndStatus). Read from TypeRegistration,
    -- which is what makes a waiting rebuild something an admin can look
    -- up rather than something known only to whoever ran the deploy that
    -- staged it.
    rebuilds: ProjectionRebuild with projection = this
}

entity ProjectionRebuild {
    projection: Projection      -- the live Projection this rebuild replaces when it is promoted

    -- The newly declared definition, staged rather than applied: the same
    -- four values RegisterProjection reconciles onto a Projection directly
    -- when a change is trivial, held here instead when it is not (see the
    -- note above that rule). The live Projection keeps serving its own
    -- current definition, untouched, for as long as this row exists.
    schema: String
    schema_version: Integer
    consumed_event_types: Set<EventType>
    sync: Boolean

    -- Highest Event.sequence within the projection's bounded context this
    -- rebuild has processed under the new definition, tracked exactly as
    -- Projection.caught_up_to tracks the live one. Null until an admin
    -- triggers the rebuild, and null is also where a triggered rebuild
    -- starts: a rebuild always replays from the start of the bounded
    -- context's stream, never from wherever the live projection happens
    -- to be.
    caught_up_to: Integer?

    status: pending | building

    -- pending: staged by RegisterProjection and waiting for an admin to
    -- call RebuildProjection - which may be indefinitely, across many
    -- deploys, since re-registering the same non-trivial change restages
    -- this same row rather than adding another. building: an admin has
    -- triggered it and it is replaying its bounded context's stream under
    -- the new definition.
    --
    -- building is terminal because there is no third stored state to
    -- reach: once caught_up_to reaches the bounded context's latest
    -- committed sequence the rebuild is promoted automatically and this
    -- row ceases to exist (see the note above RegisterProjection). A
    -- finished rebuild is not a ProjectionRebuild in some completed
    -- status; it is the live Projection.
    --
    -- A discarded rebuild has the same shape, for the same reason: an
    -- admin who decides a staged replay is not worth paying for calls
    -- DiscardProjectionRebuild and the pending row ceases to exist, with
    -- the live Projection left serving its current definition, untouched.
    -- There is no discarded status either - a rebuild that has ended is
    -- either the live Projection or nothing at all. Only a pending row can
    -- be discarded; a building one runs to promotion.
    transitions status {
        pending -> building
        terminal: building
    }
}

entity Subscription {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    access_mapping: RoleAccessMapping      -- the caller this subscription delivers to. Who may
                                            -- see it, and how, is resolved from this same
                                            -- mapping on every delivery - re-checked live, the
                                            -- same as everywhere else on the GraphQL track (see
                                            -- the note above Actor Declarations) - so a
                                            -- revocation mid-subscription stops delivery
                                            -- immediately rather than at the next reconnect.
                                            -- It does not merely fall silent either: the
                                            -- server closes the connection, with a reason
                                            -- the caller can tell apart from an ordinary
                                            -- transport drop, so a revoked subscription is
                                            -- never mistaken for a quiet bounded context
                                            -- (see EventSubscription's
                                            -- RevocationClosesTheConnection).
    created_at: Timestamp
    from_sequence: Integer                 -- delivery starts strictly after this Event.sequence.
                                            -- Fixed once at creation: the bounded context's
                                            -- latest committed sequence at that moment by
                                            -- default (see CreateAllEventsSubscription/
                                            -- CreateEventTypeSubscription), or an earlier
                                            -- caller-supplied one. Never replayed - a
                                            -- subscription answers with fresh events going
                                            -- forward, not history. A caller wanting current
                                            -- state first queries it separately (FetchEvents or
                                            -- QueryProjection) and supplies the sequence it read
                                            -- up to here for a gap-free handoff into the live feed.
                                            -- Reconnecting after a dropped connection is that
                                            -- same handoff, not a second mechanism: the old
                                            -- subscription is gone with its connection, and the
                                            -- caller creates a fresh one whose from_sequence is
                                            -- the last sequence it actually received (see
                                            -- EventSubscription's DeliveryIsAtMostOnce).
    kind: AllEventsSubscription | EventTypeSubscription
}

variant AllEventsSubscription : Subscription {
    event_types: Set<EventType>    -- restricts delivery to these event types; empty means every
                                    -- event type in the bounded context, the same "empty means
                                    -- unfiltered" convention as EventTypeSubscription.filters.
                                    -- No per-field filtering here, unlike EventTypeSubscription:
                                    -- a Filter's field is only unambiguous within one event
                                    -- type's schema, and this variant deliberately spans every
                                    -- type in the bounded context, each with its own schema. A
                                    -- caller wanting field-level filtering opens an
                                    -- EventTypeSubscription per type of interest instead.
}

variant EventTypeSubscription : Subscription {
    event_type: EventType
    filters: List<Filter>
}

-- The server-side read position of one EventReadToken: the whole of what
-- a cursor is, which is a token, how far it has got, and what moves it.
-- The REST counterpart to Subscription above - both remember where a
-- caller is in a stream, one for a live push over GraphQL, this one for a
-- token-authenticated pull (see the note above rule ConsumeEvents).
--
-- Keyed by the token and nothing finer. A token is already minted
-- individually per reader by an admin (see CreateEventReadToken), so it
-- identifies who is reading; two independent read positions are therefore
-- two tokens, not two names sharing one. See UniqueReadCursorPerToken,
-- which is what makes "the cursor for this token" well defined.
entity ReadCursor {
    token: EventReadToken
    ack_mode: AckMode                      -- chosen when this cursor first appears - on the token's
                                            -- first ConsumeEvents call - and fixed for the token's
                                            -- lifetime. A later call may restate the same mode but
                                            -- never change it (see rule ConsumeEvents); changing
                                            -- mode means a new token.
    sequence: Integer                      -- auto_advance: the highest sequence served so far.
                                            -- ConsumeEvents itself advances it on every call.
                                            -- manual_ack: the highest sequence acknowledged so far.
                                            -- Serving events never moves it - only
                                            -- AcknowledgeEvents does - so consuming again before
                                            -- acknowledging re-serves the same events. That
                                            -- re-service is the mechanism rather than a special
                                            -- case, and it is exactly what makes manual_ack
                                            -- at-least-once (see EventFetch's
                                            -- DeliveryFollowsAckMode). Starts at -1, before the
                                            -- first sequence of a bounded context, the same "from
                                            -- the beginning" position FetchEvents' absent
                                            -- after_sequence resolves to - unless the token was
                                            -- minted to start from latest, in which case it starts
                                            -- past everything committed when it was provisioned
                                            -- (see variant EventReadToken.start_from).
    updated_at: Timestamp                  -- when this cursor last moved

    -- manual_ack only - always null for an auto_advance cursor, which has
    -- no equivalent gap to close (serving already advances it in the same
    -- moment, so there is nothing left unacknowledged for a second caller
    -- to race). Set by ConsumeEvents when it serves a manual_ack cursor
    -- whose own claim was not already held (see that rule's own manual_ack
    -- branch), and cleared by AcknowledgeEvents - so it marks exactly the
    -- span between "served" and "acknowledged", never anything wider.
    -- While set and not yet older than config.read_cursor_checkout_lease,
    -- a second concurrent ConsumeEvents call for the same token is served
    -- nothing rather than the same unacknowledged batch a first caller may
    -- still be acting on - closing the gap where two callers each act on
    -- one served-but-unacknowledged batch before either acknowledges (see
    -- the note above rule ConsumeEvents and EventFetch's own
    -- DeliveryFollowsAckMode, which this narrows but does not weaken: the
    -- batch is still redelivered, to whichever caller next finds the
    -- claim expired or clear, so manual_ack stays at-least-once).
    checked_out_at: Timestamp?
}

-- One Role letting another read the private fields of a record it is
-- itself the default reader of. The only entity the private-field
-- mechanism needs, and only two of the three kinds ever produce one: own
-- and addressed are shareable, team is not, because a team field has
-- nothing to share - a Role either carries the name or it does not, and
-- no grant could change that either way (see enum PrivateFieldKind).
--
-- Self-service, and that is the defining difference from every other
-- grant in this file. A RoleAccessMapping is made for a Role by a
-- superadmin as a separately audited act (see surface AccessManagement's
-- SuperadminOnly), and an AccessToken is minted for a type by an admin.
-- This one is made by the ordinary caller who already holds the thing
-- being shared, needs no admin anywhere, and is revocable only by that
-- same caller (see rule RevokePrivateFieldAccess).
entity PrivateFieldGrant {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext        -- the context the shared record lives in, and the
                                            -- one the grantor's own grant was scoped to when it
                                            -- made this. A grant never spans contexts, the same
                                            -- as everything else here
    grantor: Role                          -- the Role that made this grant, and the only Role
                                            -- that can ever revoke it
    grantee: Role                          -- the Role being let in

    -- At most one of the two below is ever set, and both may be null.
    -- Naming one is a per-record grant: this grantee may read the private
    -- fields of exactly this record and no other. Naming neither is a
    -- blanket grant: every record of any type the grantor is itself the
    -- default private reader of, present and future alike. Naming both is
    -- meaningless and never written - see invariant
    -- PrivateFieldGrantNamesAtMostOneRecord.
    --
    -- A blanket grant is a convenience, not a wider trust boundary. It
    -- cannot be checked against records that do not exist yet, so it is
    -- trusted when it is made and re-derived at every read against the
    -- specific record being read: the grantor must still be that record's
    -- own default private reader for the grant to let anyone through it
    -- (see the render_event note above rule DeliverToSubscriptions). So it
    -- never reaches further than the same grantor could have reached one
    -- record at a time.
    event: Event?
    command: Command?

    status: active | revoked
    created_at: Timestamp
    revoked_at: Timestamp? when status = revoked

    transitions status {
        active -> revoked
        terminal: revoked
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

-- How long a ConsumeEvents claim on a manual_ack ReadCursor holds before
-- it is treated as abandoned and reclaimable - see ReadCursor.checked_out_at
-- and rule ConsumeEvents' own manual_ack branch. Generous by default:
-- long enough that an ordinary caller's own poll-serve-act-acknowledge
-- cycle never trips it, short enough that a caller which crashes after
-- being served (and so never acknowledges) does not leave the stream
-- stuck for long.
--
-- max_events_per_read bounds how many events one FetchEvents,
-- ConsumeEvents or QueryEvents serves: the first that many matching
-- events, in sequence order. Without it one read of a long history -
-- a new consumer starting from the beginning, a fetch with no
-- after_sequence - would serve all of it at once. It bounds a page, not
-- what is readable: each of those rules serves a prefix and continues
-- from exactly where it stopped (FetchEvents' and QueryEvents' callers
-- pass the last served sequence back as after_sequence; ConsumeEvents'
-- cursor advances, or is acknowledged, past the last served event), so
-- nothing is ever skipped. It counts served events, not scanned ones: a
-- page is only short when nothing further matches, so a filtered
-- consumer always makes progress. CountEvents stays unbounded - it is an
-- aggregate, not a page. FetchCommands is bounded by the same value (see
-- first_by_recording), so despite the name it caps commands too.
config {
    read_cursor_checkout_lease: Duration = 5.minutes
    max_events_per_read: Integer = 1000
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Defaults
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The two instances that exist before any caller does, and so are created
-- by no rule.
--
-- admin is the one bounded context AddBoundedContext did not create - it
-- exists unconditionally, before the first superadmin Role does - and
-- cannot create either, since that rule's `not exists
-- BoundedContext{name: name}` guard already sees this one. It is
-- permanent in the other direction too: nothing removes this bounded
-- context. Other contexts can now be removed - DeleteBoundedContext ends
-- an archived one outright - but that rule guards against this one by
-- name, so admin is the single context whose permanence is absolute. See
-- invariant AdminContextIsPermanent.
--
-- Only its name and status are literal above, because only those are
-- spec-time constants. Its created_at and created_by are real recorded
-- values, so a static default declaration is the wrong place for them:
-- both are written by SkilJ itself, at its own first startup. created_at
-- is that first-start moment, and created_by is the skilj marker below.
-- Nothing else could have written either - no caller and no Role existed
-- yet to have created this context.
--
-- That stamping stands on its own. It is not part of, and does not wait
-- for, the arrival of the first superadmin Role: the bootstrap secret is
-- generated at this same first startup, but the Role it authorises is
-- claimed by a separate explicit call that may come minutes later, months
-- later, or - in a deployment nobody ever finishes setting up - never (see
-- the note above rule CreateSuperadmin). This context's provenance is
-- stamped either way. It is also stamped only once: unlike the bootstrap
-- secret, which is regenerated on every startup until it is claimed, this
-- acts only while the values are unset, so a later restart never restamps
-- them.
--
-- skilj is that marker: the single SystemCreator instance, standing for
-- the library itself rather than for any Role.
default BoundedContext admin = { name: "admin", status: active }
default SystemCreator skilj = { }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- next_sequence(bounded_context), used below in CreateExternalEvent,
-- CreateDirectEvent and ProcessCommand, and the consistency-boundary
-- re-check in ProcessCommand, are both enforced at the Postgres level
-- rather than in application code: a single row per bounded context
-- (e.g. in a sequence-counter table) is locked with SELECT ... FOR
-- UPDATE as part of the same transaction that inserts the new
-- Command/Event rows, then incremented and released on commit. This one
-- lock scope gives both guarantees for free - gapless assignment (the
-- counter only advances on a committed transaction, unlike a Postgres
-- SEQUENCE, which is non-transactional and does leave gaps on rollback)
-- and the DCB conflict re-check (no concurrent committer for the same
-- bounded context can be interleaved while the row is locked). Only the
-- final re-check and insert need the lock; reading matching events and
-- running decide() beforehand does not.
--
-- protect_sensitive_fields(type, payload), used in the same three rules
-- and, for the command's own payload, in ProcessCommand, follows a
-- similar pattern: it get-or-creates the EncryptionKey for each subject
-- a sensitive field in type.sensitive_fields references (each key
-- provisioned exactly once - the get-or-create is genuinely idempotent,
-- protected by the schema's own partial unique index over the active
-- key for a bounded_context, subject_key and subject_value, so two
-- concurrent provisionings for one subject can never leave two rows),
-- then returns the payload with those fields' values replaced by
-- ciphertext plus the keys used. That provisioning is resolved before
-- the write's own transaction opens rather than inside it, and
-- deliberately so: a master-key wrap and a database round trip are
-- real work, and doing them while already holding the sequence row
-- lock above would make every other concurrent writer to the same
-- bounded context wait for them, for no correctness benefit. The one
-- consequence is benign: a write that later fails or rolls back for an
-- unrelated reason leaves the key it provisioned behind - real and
-- active, but referenced by nothing, and so readable through by
-- nothing either - until the next write for that same subject finds
-- it rather than provisioning another. A no-op - unchanged payload,
-- empty key set - when sensitive_fields is empty.
--
-- "Those fields" includes a SensitiveField.field written as a two-segment
-- dotted path (see the payload schema shape note above entity
-- CommandType). The value substituted is the leaf's and only the leaf's:
-- the enclosing nested object keeps its other fields, and the payload
-- keeps its structure, with one scalar inside it now holding ciphertext
-- instead of plaintext. That is why leaf-level is the only sound level -
-- a scalar's declared type is the same either way, whereas encrypting the
-- whole object would flip its type between object and string depending on
-- who reads it. subject_field resolves the same way, and is read, never
-- encrypted, exactly as when it names a top-level field. Unlike
-- next_sequence's target, EncryptionKey is a real, rule-governed entity
-- (see its transitions and ForgetSubject below); this call only ever
-- provisions - destruction is always a separate, explicitly authorised
-- action. It may be called more than once for the same type/payload
-- within one rule execution (see ProcessCommand) and is idempotent when
-- it is: repeat calls find the key already provisioned rather than
-- creating another one.
--
-- It takes an EventType and a CommandType interchangeably, and that is
-- load-bearing rather than convenient. Sensitivity is declared
-- identically on both (EventType.sensitive_fields and
-- CommandType.sensitive_fields are the same Set<SensitiveField>, with
-- the same subject_key/subject_field convention), and an EncryptionKey
-- is identified by bounded_context, subject_key and subject_value alone
-- - never by which type declared the field. A command and an event
-- naming the same subject within one bounded context therefore encrypt
-- under the very same key. That is what makes erasure whole with no
-- erasure-side change at all: ForgetSubject destroys one key, and every
-- payload ciphertext written under it - command payloads and event
-- payloads alike - becomes permanently unreadable at once. Unreadable to
-- everyone, including a superadmin and including anyone reading the
-- Postgres rows directly, because the plaintext was never stored in the
-- first place. Neither ForgetSubject nor the SubjectErasure surface needs
-- to know that commands exist. The one thing that could have undercut
-- that wholeness - a TagMapping reading the same payload field a
-- SensitiveField marks, copying its plaintext into Event.tags /
-- Command.consistency_tags where no key destruction reaches it - is ruled
-- out at registration time instead of being lived with: see the overlap
-- constraint in the note above RegisterEventType.
--
-- What erasure cannot reach is data that was already plaintext. A
-- SensitiveField protects only the payloads written after it was
-- declared; commands and events of that type recorded before it was
-- declared were never encrypted, so there is no EncryptionKey to destroy
-- for them and ForgetSubject has nothing to act on. There is no
-- backfill, purge or redaction path for that data, and this spec
-- deliberately does not offer one: the only targeted way to remove it
-- would be to rewrite stored Command/Event rows in place, and immutability is
-- load-bearing everywhere here - a stored command or event is never
-- rewritten, the stream is append-only, and crypto-shredding exists
-- precisely so that forgetting destroys a key rather than touching a
-- row. Archiving is not a partial mitigation either: an archived
-- bounded context stops accepting new commands and events but retains
-- its history exactly as recorded (see BoundedContext.status). Deleting
-- that archived context afterwards does remove the plaintext, but as a
-- way out rather than a redaction path: DeleteBoundedContext is all or
-- nothing and works on a whole bounded context, never on one subject's
-- data within a context anyone still wants. The
-- practical consequence for whoever registers a type: sensitive_fields
-- has to be declared before sensitive data starts flowing through that
-- type. Declaring it later protects the future only, never the past.
--
-- Finding out which historical rows are still in that state - empty
-- Event.encryption_keys/Command.encryption_keys for a type that now
-- declares a sensitive_fields entry - is not something this library
-- offers a query for, and deliberately so. It is an operator/compliance
-- concern, not a security boundary this surface set needs to enforce:
-- unlike the erasure question above, which is about stopping data from
-- being read back through this library's own reads even by an admin,
-- discovering what is still plaintext is something an operator with
-- direct Postgres access - which they already have, to run this library
-- at all - can already answer for themselves, since it is a structural
-- property of the stored rows. Adding a surface for it would be a real
-- but separable feature (e.g. a compliance-reporting read), not
-- something to build ahead of an actual need for it.
--
-- derive_tags(type, payload), used by CreateExternalEvent,
-- CreateDirectEvent and ProcessCommand above and below, is a black box in
-- the same register as the rest: this library owns which tags a payload
-- yields and therefore what a consistency boundary can address, not how a
-- JSON payload is walked to read a field out of it. It takes an EventType
-- and a CommandType interchangeably, for the same reason
-- protect_sensitive_fields does - tag_mappings is the identical
-- Set<TagMapping> on both, and Event.tags and Command.consistency_tags
-- are directly comparable precisely because one function produces them
-- both. Its full contract, per TagMapping in type.tag_mappings, where
-- "the mapped field" means whatever TagMapping.field resolves to - a
-- top-level field named bare, or the one leaf a two-segment dotted path
-- reaches inside a nested shape (see the payload schema shape note above
-- entity CommandType):
--   - The mapped field is present and holds a scalar value: one
--     Tag(key, value: that value). The ordinary case.
--   - The mapped field is absent, because the schema declares it optional
--     and this payload omitted it: one Tag(key, value: null). Not zero
--     tags - see Tag.value for why the "None" state is deliberately
--     addressable rather than invisible.
--   - The mapped field holds a list of scalars: one Tag(key, value: e)
--     per distinct element e, all sharing that mapping's key.
--   - The mapped field holds an empty list, or is a list-typed field that
--     is absent altogether: one Tag(key, value: null), exactly as an
--     absent scalar. An empty list and a missing list are both "no value
--     was provided", and collapsing them onto the same single "None" state
--     keeps that one state rather than inventing a third.
--
-- A dotted path adds no fifth case. It extracts the leaf value exactly as
-- a bare name extracts a top-level one, and produces exactly the same
-- Tag(key, value: that leaf value). A leaf can be missing in two ways -
-- the nested object field is absent from the payload altogether, or the
-- object is present but that particular leaf inside it is absent, since a
-- nested shape's own fields may be optional too - and both are the second
-- bullet above, unchanged: one Tag(key, value: null). There is no
-- "doubly absent" state distinguishable from the ordinary one. That
-- follows the same reasoning that already collapsed an empty list and a
-- missing list onto the single "None" state: absence is absence, and
-- multiplying the ways to be absent would multiply the states a
-- consistency boundary or filter has to address.
--
-- Two consequences of the list case are worth naming rather than leaving
-- to be rediscovered, and both fall out of Event.tags and
-- Command.consistency_tags already being Set<Tag>:
--   - ANY-match comes for free. The consistency-boundary match in
--     ProcessCommand is consistency_tags.any(t => t in tags), i.e. "does
--     any tag in one set appear in the other". A list-valued field
--     contributing one tag per element therefore matches when any element
--     matches, with no rule-level change at all - the semantics people
--     usually want from a multi-valued tag are what the existing set
--     membership already does.
--   - Duplicate elements collapse. Two equal elements produce two equal
--     Tags, and a Set holds one - so a list is effectively deduplicated
--     for tagging purposes, again with nothing extra specified.
--
-- A consistency boundary is therefore always scoped to values, never to
-- a type as a whole, and that is settled rather than an omission. There
-- is no tag meaning "every event of this type, whatever the value", and
-- the shape of the mechanism is what rules it out: matching_events in
-- ProcessCommand is always consistency_tags.any(t => t in tags), and
-- consistency_tags is always derive_tags over the submitting command's
-- own payload. A boundary can only ever name values the submitter
-- itself supplied, and no payload field has a value that could mean
-- "match regardless of value". Nor is there a second read path to
-- compensate: a bounded context's decide() is handed matching_events
-- and nothing else. This library's own rules can check history
-- unscoped - CreateSuperadmin's `not exists Role{superadmin: true,
-- status: active}` is exactly that - but that is a rule in this file,
-- not something a plugged-in decider can reach for.
--
-- The requirements that look like they need a type-wide read generally
-- do not. A balance check spanning every deposit and withdrawal on an
-- account, or a capacity cap on a session's registrations, each have
-- their scoping identifier sitting in the submitting command's own
-- payload. Tag-map account_id or session_id on the command type and on
-- the event types concerned, and the existing mechanism already gathers
-- every relevant event across every type sharing that key, scoped to
-- that one account or session - which is what those requirements
-- actually want. Spanning event types is what a shared key buys (see
-- TagMapping.key); spanning every value of a key is a different thing.
--
-- What is genuinely unavailable is the cross-instance form: "500
-- attendees across every workshop combined" rather than 30 per
-- workshop, or "100 withdrawals a day system-wide" rather than per
-- account. No such requirement has been identified, and it is not being
-- provided speculatively, because the cost would be structural rather
-- than incidental. Tag matching is fast precisely because Event.tags
-- and Command.consistency_tags are stored separately and indexed (see
-- the overlap constraint in the note above RegisterEventType) and
-- because the per-bounded-context event cache primarily serves
-- tag-scoped lookups (see the performance guidance in ProcessCommand).
-- An unscoped read has no tag to narrow by, so neither the index nor
-- the cache can serve it: it would scan a type's whole history on every
-- submission, bounded by neither - the exact cost both exist to avoid.
-- Should such a requirement ever turn up, it wants a mechanism designed
-- for it, not a wildcard value smuggled into Tag.
--
-- Reading events - the Events collections read by FetchEvents and
-- ProcessCommand below - is served by an in-memory cache of the most
-- recent events, scoped per bounded context exactly as sequence and
-- next_sequence already are. On startup each bounded context's cache is
-- warmed by fetching its own events from Postgres in descending
-- sequence order, up to a configurable count that defaults to 1000. That
-- count is a library/runtime configuration knob set when the process
-- starts, not a registered or admin-managed value: it is deliberately
-- not a field on BoundedContext and nothing registers it, for the same
-- reason the next_sequence row lock isn't modelled as an entity. Events
-- are appended to their bounded context's cache as they are committed,
-- so the latest events are always available without a database round
-- trip.
--
-- Postgres remains the source of truth throughout; the cache is purely a
-- read-path optimisation and never a limit on what is readable. Any read
-- the cache cannot satisfy - events older than the warm-up window, or
-- any read on an instance that has cold-started and not yet warmed -
-- resolves against Postgres and returns exactly the same events. The
-- cache changes how fast a read is answered, never which events a caller
-- can see, so no rule below is conditioned on it.
--
-- project(projection, event) - the plugged-in, projection-specific fold
-- that applies one event to one projection's stored state - is a black
-- box in the same register as decide(): this library owns when a
-- projection is updated and what a caller may then see, not what the
-- projection computes. It runs in one of two places, chosen per
-- projection by Projection.sync:
--   - Asynchronously (the default). A background consumer per bounded
--     context walks that context's committed events in sequence order
--     and, for each projection, calls project() when the event's type is
--     in the projection's consumed_event_types, then advances that
--     projection's own caught_up_to to the event's sequence either way.
--     This runs outside any request's transaction, so a projection can
--     trail the write that fed it.
--   - Synchronously, when Projection.sync is true. The update happens
--     inline, in the same transaction that creates the event - which for
--     a CommandTriggered event is the same transaction as the command
--     that produced it - so it commits or fails with the write. Called
--     from CreateExternalEvent, CreateDirectEvent and ProcessCommand
--     alike, for every sync projection in the bounded context whose
--     consumed_event_types include the type of event just created;
--     caught_up_to advances for every sync projection in that context
--     regardless. Because consumed_event_types is known at compile time
--     (see Projection), the set to update is resolved statically, with no
--     runtime discovery. This is deliberate friction, not a fast path:
--     see Projection.sync.
--
-- Either way, one call to project() may update more than one of that
-- projection's instances: a transfer event updating both the giver's own
-- row and the receiver's own row is one event touching two keys, not two
-- events. Which keys those are, and how many, is project()'s own
-- decision from the event it is handed - this spec neither enumerates
-- them nor bounds them, exactly as it never says what the fold computes
-- (see the instance-data note on entity Projection).
--
-- await_projection_caught_up(projection, sequence), used by
-- QueryProjection below, is the reader-side counterpart: it blocks until
-- projection.caught_up_to reaches sequence and reports whether it got
-- there, up to a wait bound. Resolved: that bound is a process-start
-- runtime/library configuration knob, the same register as the event
-- cache's warm-up count and the external IdP's trust configuration (see
-- the scope header) - not a per-query argument (that would grow the
-- wire contract this spec deliberately leaves deferred) and not a fixed
-- value baked into the library (an operator can tune how long a caller
-- waits without a code change). A sync projection satisfies it
-- immediately and always, by construction.
--
-- Correlation and causation, stamped by all four of the rules below and
-- specified once here rather than four times. Metadata.correlation_id
-- names the whole business transaction a command or event belongs to;
-- Metadata.causation_id names the one thing that directly produced it (see
-- value Metadata, which also says why neither is distributed tracing).
-- Both are durable, stored and queryable, and neither is ever interpreted
-- by this library - opaque caller-chosen strings compared for equality,
-- the same treatment an idempotency key and a RoleAccessMapping.scope
-- already get.
--
-- valid_correlation_id(id) is the one gate either value passes, applied
-- separately to each: it holds when id is null or empty, and otherwise
-- when id is at most 200 characters long. It is a black box for the same
-- reason valid_bounded_context_name is - a character-level check is below
-- the level this file works at - but a deliberately looser one, and the
-- difference matters. A bounded context name is carried through verbatim
-- as an identifier by the layers underneath this spec, which is why that
-- check restricts the character set at all. These two are nothing of the
-- kind: they are arbitrary trace data a caller or a message-broker bridge
-- hands over, and a bridge has to be able to forward what its upstream
-- actually gave it - a UUID, a W3C traceparent-style string, a broker's
-- own message id, none of which would survive a Postgres-identifier
-- charset. So no charset is imposed, and the only thing refused is a value
-- long enough to be a payload rather than an id. A caller supplying
-- neither value passes vacuously and is never rejected on this ground.
--
-- Who supplies what, and what is generated:
--   - correlation_id is used verbatim when the caller names one, and
--     generated by generate_id() when it does not, so every stored Command
--     and every stored Event carries one. generate_id() is a black box in
--     the same register as generate_token_id and generate_token_secret:
--     the spec owns that it yields a fresh, unique value, not which
--     primitive produces it.
--   - causation_id is never generated. It is stored exactly as supplied
--     and left null when nothing upstream was named, which is a root of a
--     chain rather than a gap in one. A plain submission a caller
--     originated itself, a direct creation and a scheduler occurrence are
--     all roots.
--   - An event a command triggers inherits the command's own
--     correlation_id and takes the command's id as its causation_id (see
--     rule ProcessCommand). That is the one place this library establishes
--     a causal link itself rather than recording one it was told about,
--     and it is what makes a chain reassemblable from the store: every
--     event a command produced shares the command's correlation_id, and
--     points back at the command by id.
--
-- What is deliberately not here is any enforcement that a chain is
-- well-formed. Nothing checks that a supplied causation_id names a record
-- that exists, that a correlation_id a caller reuses belongs to a chain it
-- has any part in, or that the two agree with each other. All three would
-- require this library to have an opinion about causality it does not have
-- - a caller's chain routinely starts outside SkilJ entirely, in a broker
-- or a service this spec knows nothing about - and a dangling reference is
-- exactly what a chain crossing a system boundary looks like from inside
-- one of the systems. These are provenance a caller asserts, not a claim
-- this library verifies, which is the same register client_id already sits
-- in.

rule CreateExternalEvent {
    when:
        SubmitExternalEvent(
            adapter, event_type, payload, source_content, source_context?,
            dedupe_partition_key?, dedupe_sequence?,
            correlation_id?, causation_id?)
    requires: event_type.external_creation_allowed = true
    requires: adapter.event_type = event_type
    requires: adapter.status = active
    requires: event_type.bounded_context.status = active
    requires: valid_payload(event_type.schema, payload)

    let protected = protect_sensitive_fields(event_type, payload)
    let tags = derive_tags(event_type, payload)

    -- The ownership check, and the write-side counterpart to EventFetch's
    -- own EventsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared: a token that names a scope (see
    -- variant ExternalEventToken.scope) may create events only for the
    -- owner it names, checked against the very same tags the event is about
    -- to be written with. Single-record, so it rejects rather than filters,
    -- for the reason rule AuthoriseCommandSubmission's own note gives in
    -- full rather than a second copy of it here. Vacuously true for a token
    -- that names no scope, which is every token unless the minting admin
    -- named one, and for an event type that declares no owner dimension.
    requires: tag_owner_scope_satisfied(tags, event_type.owner_tag_key, adapter.scope)

    -- This is the submission that most often has a chain to join already:
    -- an adapter sitting in front of a broker is forwarding a message that
    -- was itself caused by something upstream, and correlation_id and
    -- causation_id are where it says so. Both are the adapter's own opaque
    -- values, checked separately by the one predicate that governs them -
    -- see the correlation note above the rules, including why the check
    -- imposes no charset on either, which is what lets a bridge forward an
    -- upstream id verbatim rather than reshaping it. Supplying neither is
    -- the ordinary case for an adapter whose source carries no such id, and
    -- passes vacuously.
    requires: valid_correlation_id(correlation_id)
    requires: valid_correlation_id(causation_id)

    -- Redelivery. dedupe_partition_key and dedupe_sequence are the caller's
    -- own values, carried here untouched from surface
    -- ExternalEventIngestion and null whenever the adapter supplied
    -- neither. They exist because the message sources adapters sit in front
    -- of - Kafka, Kinesis, Pulsar and their kin - number their messages
    -- within a partition and deliver at least once: a consumer that crashes
    -- before committing its own read position is handed the same messages
    -- again on restart, and without something to recognise them by, every
    -- one of them becomes a second event standing for the same fact.
    --
    -- The pair is supplied together or not at all, which is what the guard
    -- below says: dedupe_partition_key names the partition, shard or stream
    -- the message came from, dedupe_sequence is that message's own position
    -- within it, and neither means anything without the other. Supplying
    -- exactly one is a malformed submission and is rejected as such - it is
    -- the caller being told it asked for something incoherent, and is not
    -- to be confused with the duplicate case below, which is not an error
    -- at all.
    requires:
        (dedupe_partition_key = null implies dedupe_sequence = null)
        and (dedupe_sequence = null implies dedupe_partition_key = null)

    -- highest_dedupe_sequence(adapter, dedupe_partition_key) is the single
    -- number this library remembers for that pair: the highest
    -- dedupe_sequence any submission bearing that partition key from that
    -- adapter has ever had an event written for, or null when the partition
    -- key is null or when nothing has been recorded for it yet. It is a
    -- black box in the same register as next_sequence and
    -- recorded_acceptance - library-owned durable state whose storage shape
    -- this spec does not reach into - rather than a plugged-in decision
    -- like decide().
    --
    -- Three cases, and they are the whole of the behaviour:
    --
    --   Pair absent. Nothing is looked up and nothing is recorded. The
    --   submission creates its event exactly as every submission did before
    --   this pair existed at all. This is a guarantee rather than merely the
    --   common case - see ExternalEventIngestion's
    --   OmittingTheDedupePairChangesNothing.
    --
    --   Sequence above the watermark, or nothing recorded for the pair yet.
    --   The event is created exactly as it always was, and this sequence
    --   becomes the new watermark for (adapter, dedupe_partition_key) as
    --   part of the same transaction the event is written in - so a
    --   watermark advances exactly when the event it stands for is durable,
    --   never before and never without it. A submission this rule rejects
    --   for any other reason advances nothing.
    --
    --   Sequence at or below the watermark. This is a redelivery of a
    --   message already accounted for, and no event is created: no
    --   ExternalTriggered, no sequence consumed, no projection updated,
    --   nothing written. It is not an error - the submission is accepted and
    --   acknowledged, and an adapter replaying a partition after a crash is
    --   doing exactly what it is supposed to do, not making a bad request.
    --
    -- Deliberately unlike an idempotency key hit on a command, a duplicate
    -- here is answered with nothing describing what happened last time. A
    -- watermark remembers one number, not which event any particular past
    -- message produced, so there is no original outcome to return and none
    -- is invented; see ExternalEventIngestion's
    -- ARedeliveryProducesNoEventAndNoOutcome.
    --
    -- One number per pair, not a row per message, and that compactness is
    -- the whole design rather than an optimisation of it. It is sound
    -- because the caller's own source already guarantees strictly
    -- increasing delivery order within one partition, which is exactly the
    -- property that makes everything at or below the highest seen
    -- redundant. What it buys in cheapness it gives up in generality: this
    -- deduplicates ordered message streams and nothing else. An adapter
    -- with arbitrary unordered identifiers to deduplicate by has no
    -- mechanism here, and should not reach for this one by inventing an
    -- ordering its source does not have.
    --
    -- The cost of a watermark, stated plainly rather than left to be
    -- discovered: a message arriving out of order within its own partition
    -- is dropped, silently and permanently, because it is indistinguishable
    -- from a redelivery. That is the trade an adapter makes by supplying
    -- the pair at all, and it is why the pair is optional - an adapter
    -- whose source cannot promise in-partition ordering supplies neither
    -- value and is never deduplicated.
    --
    -- Scoped to the adapter and the partition key, and to nothing else.
    -- Deliberately not per event type: an ExternalEventToken is already
    -- issued for exactly one EventType (see variant ExternalEventToken), so
    -- scoping by adapter is at least as narrow, and scoping by type instead
    -- would let two unrelated adapters for the same type collide on a
    -- partition key string they each chose independently. The obligation
    -- this puts on an adapter is to keep its own partition keys stable
    -- across restarts, since a fresh key starts a fresh watermark.
    --
    -- Nothing expires, the same treatment a recorded idempotency key gets
    -- and for the same reason (see rule ProcessCommand). A watermark is
    -- also not the opaque source_context blob and never parsed out of it:
    -- an adapter that puts its Kafka topic/partition/offset in there for
    -- provenance has said nothing to this mechanism, which acts only on
    -- values named separately and explicitly (see variant
    -- ExternalTriggered).
    let watermark = highest_dedupe_sequence(adapter, dedupe_partition_key)
    let is_redelivery = watermark != null and dedupe_sequence <= watermark

    -- Every sync projection consuming this event type is updated inline,
    -- in this same transaction - see the project() note above the rules.
    ensures:
        if not is_redelivery:
            ExternalTriggered.created(
                bounded_context: event_type.bounded_context,
                event_type: event_type,
                payload: protected.payload,
                metadata: {
                    type: event_type.name,
                    version: event_type.schema_version,
                    client_id: adapter.id,
                    created_at: now,
                    correlation_id: correlation_id ?? generate_id(),
                    causation_id: causation_id
                },
                sequence: next_sequence(event_type.bounded_context),
                tags: tags,
                encryption_keys: protected.encryption_keys,
                source_content: source_content,
                source_context: source_context
            )
}

rule CreateDirectEvent {
    when: SubmitDirectEvent(adapter, event_type, payload, correlation_id?, causation_id?)
    requires: event_type.direct_creation_allowed = true
    requires: adapter.event_type = event_type
    requires: adapter.status = active
    requires: event_type.bounded_context.status = active
    requires: valid_payload(event_type.schema, payload)

    let protected = protect_sensitive_fields(event_type, payload)
    let tags = derive_tags(event_type, payload)

    -- The same ownership check CreateExternalEvent applies just above, on
    -- the same terms and against the same declaration, differing only in
    -- which token variant carries the scope - see variant
    -- DirectCreationToken.scope. Its note above is not restated here.
    requires: tag_owner_scope_satisfied(tags, event_type.owner_tag_key, adapter.scope)

    -- The same two values CreateExternalEvent takes, on the same terms and
    -- checked by the same predicate - see the correlation note above the
    -- rules. A direct creation more often has nothing upstream to name than
    -- an ingested message does, which is why causation_id stays null here
    -- in the ordinary case rather than being invented.
    requires: valid_correlation_id(correlation_id)
    requires: valid_correlation_id(causation_id)

    -- Sync projection updates happen inline here too - see the project()
    -- note above the rules.
    ensures:
        DirectlyCreated.created(
            bounded_context: event_type.bounded_context,
            event_type: event_type,
            payload: protected.payload,
            metadata: {
                type: event_type.name,
                version: event_type.schema_version,
                client_id: adapter.id,
                created_at: now,
                correlation_id: correlation_id ?? generate_id(),
                causation_id: causation_id
            },
            sequence: next_sequence(event_type.bounded_context),
            tags: tags,
            encryption_keys: protected.encryption_keys
        )
}

-- The third and last of the origins that create an Event with no command
-- behind it, and the only origin anywhere with no caller at all: the two
-- rules above are each driven by an adapter presenting a token, while this
-- one is driven by SkilJ's own in-process scheduler against
-- EventType.system_triggered_schedule. Everything downstream is identical -
-- the same sequence assignment, the same tag derivation, the same
-- sensitive-field protection, the same inline update of every sync
-- projection consuming the type - because none of that ever depended on who
-- asked.
--
-- What has no natural source, and is settled here rather than left
-- implicit, is the payload. An external or direct submission carries one
-- from its caller; a command-triggered event gets one from decide()'s own
-- EventSpec. An occurrence of a schedule has neither, so
-- scheduled_payload(event_type) supplies it: the plugged-in,
-- event-type-specific producer of the payload one occurrence of that type's
-- schedule emits, a black box in exactly the register decide() and
-- project() are in. This library owns that an occurrence produces a
-- genuine, correctly stamped SystemTriggered event of that type in that
-- bounded context; the bounded context owns what the event says. It is
-- registered the same way the rest of a bounded context's plugged-in code
-- is - compiled into the running binary alongside the schedule that fires
-- it (see the note above RegisterEventType) - so nothing new carries it in.
-- Like next_sequence and generate_token_secret, and unlike valid_filters,
-- it is not expected to return the same thing on every call: the
-- determinism a black box has here is within one rule execution, not across
-- occurrences. Its one obligation is that what it returns conforms to
-- event_type.schema - the same obligation a caller-supplied payload already
-- carries, and the same predicate states it: valid_payload(schema, payload)
-- (see the payload schema shape note above entity CommandType). What
-- differs is where it lands. A caller's payload is checked at the boundary
-- it crosses, because it arrives from outside; this one crosses no
-- boundary, so the obligation rests on the bounded context's own
-- compiled-in code rather than on a guard in this rule - exactly as it does
-- for the EventSpec payloads decide() returns, which ProcessCommand does
-- not re-check either.
--
-- One occurrence produces exactly one event, and that is a resolution
-- rather than an omission. A schedule names one EventType, so there is no
-- second type for an occurrence to fan out into, and "emit only when there
-- is something to emit" is a decision - which is what a CommandType and
-- decide() are for. A bounded context wanting either registers a scheduled
-- event type whose event its own code reacts to by submitting a command,
-- and gets fan-out and conditionality from the mechanism that already has
-- them, rather than from a second decider reachable only from a timer.
--
-- When an occurrence is due is deliberately not modelled here. The schedule
-- naming which moments are due is registered domain state
-- (EventType.system_triggered_schedule, reconciled by RegisterEventType and
-- readable by an admin); evaluating a cron expression against a clock is
-- the scheduler's own mechanism, the same register as the async projection
-- consumer's polling loop or next_sequence's row lock, and nothing about
-- the resulting event depends on it. Which occurrence is being raised is a
-- different matter and is modelled: SystemTriggerDue carries the
-- occurrence's own instant, so what an event stands for is stated by the
-- trigger rather than inferred from whenever the rule happened to run. That
-- distinction is the whole of the missed-occurrence answer below - a
-- catch-up firing at 09:00 for an occurrence due at 03:00 records 03:00 as
-- the occurrence it stood for and 09:00 as its Metadata.created_at, which
-- is the only honest pair of values it has.
--
-- What the scheduler owes for an occurrence that passed while nothing was
-- watching for it was the last open question in this file. It is answered
-- here by declining to answer it on a bounded context's behalf:
-- EventType.missed_occurrence_policy is chosen per event type at
-- registration, with no default anywhere and no fallback - a registration
-- that opts into scheduling without choosing is rejected outright (see the
-- note above RegisterEventType). The three choices are guards on this rule,
-- not scheduler trivia, and they are evaluated against durable state rather
-- than against anything a process remembers.
--
-- That state is EventType.schedule_position: every occurrence at or before
-- that instant has been accounted for - fired, or deliberately passed over
-- - and none of them is considered again. It is a field on the registered
-- type, so it is one shared value per event type rather than one per
-- replica or per deploy, exactly as Projection.caught_up_to and
-- ReadCursor.sequence are, and for the same reason: several instances of
-- the same service run against one database and must not each hold their
-- own idea of what has fired. A second instance raising an occurrence the
-- first has already fired finds the position past it and produces nothing,
-- which is what makes the duplicate raise harmless rather than a duplicate
-- event.
--
-- The scheduler's own side of that contract is uniform across all three
-- policies and stated once, here: raise every occurrence beyond the
-- position that has come due, in ascending order, and nothing else.
-- Interleaving two instances doing that against one position is the same
-- concurrency problem next_sequence already has, and is answered the same
-- way - in the database, in the transaction that advances the position and
-- writes the event - rather than modelled as an entity here. Ascending
-- order matters for replay_backlog alone, which is also the only policy
-- that fires more than one occurrence of a backlog; raising a backlog out
-- of order under it would leave the events it does write in an order the
-- schedule never had.
--
-- Each policy is then a guard on whether one raised occurrence produces an
-- event:
--   - replay_backlog fires every occurrence beyond the position, so an
--     outage is paid off one event per missed occurrence, in occurrence
--     order. schedule_position and last_fired_at never diverge under it.
--   - fire_once fires an occurrence only when no later one has come due
--     yet, so a backlog collapses into its own last occurrence: one event,
--     stamped with the occurrence it stood for, however long the outage.
--     The occurrences it passed over are accounted for all the same, since
--     the position jumps to the one that fired.
--   - skip fires an occurrence only when the occurrence immediately before
--     it is already accounted for and no later one has come due - that is,
--     only when there is no gap. A gap is closed without firing, by
--     SkipMissedOccurrences below, which is what keeps skip from being a
--     policy that silently stops firing forever after its first outage.
--
-- next_occurrence_after(schedule, instant) is the single cron primitive
-- those guards need: the first occurrence of that schedule strictly after
-- that instant. A black box in the same register as valid_filters - the
-- expression grammar is mechanism, the guard is behaviour - and, unlike
-- scheduled_payload, deterministic in the ordinary way: the same schedule
-- and instant always resolve to the same occurrence, which is exactly why
-- two instances evaluating these guards reach the same verdict.
--
-- SystemTriggerDue, and SystemScheduleResumed alongside it, are therefore
-- the triggers in this file that no surface provides, and that is correct
-- rather than a missing surface. A surface is a boundary between two
-- parties; the scheduler raising either of them is SkilJ's own in-process
-- loop, on the same side of every boundary here as the code it hands the
-- occurrence to. It presents no credential, faces no actor and is
-- reachable by nobody - which is precisely why the guards below are the
-- whole of the authorisation story for this origin, where the other three
-- each have a token or a grant in front of them. A future reader should not
-- close the checker's unreachable-trigger note by inventing a surface for
-- either. What an admin does get is a read: surface TypeRegistration shows
-- every scheduled type's policy and both of its positions, which is
-- visibility into what the scheduler has done, not a way to drive it.
--
-- Unlike CreateExternalEvent and CreateDirectEvent, this does require an
-- active bounded context, and the difference is deliberate rather than
-- incidental. Those two have an admin lever of their own: revoking the
-- token stops the writes, whatever the context's status. A scheduler
-- presents no credential and there is none to revoke, so archiving is the
-- only lever there is - and BoundedContext.status already says an archived
-- context accepts no new events. Without this guard an archived context
-- would go on minting events on a timer with nothing short of deleting it
-- able to stop that.

rule CreateSystemEvent {
    when: SystemTriggerDue(event_type, occurrence_at)
    requires: event_type.system_triggered_allowed = true
    requires: event_type.system_triggered_schedule != null
    requires: event_type.missed_occurrence_policy != null
    requires: event_type.bounded_context.status = active

    -- An occurrence is never fired before its own instant, and never
    -- twice: schedule_position is the durable, shared record of how far
    -- this type's schedule has been accounted for, so an occurrence at or
    -- behind it has already been settled - by an earlier firing, by a
    -- policy that passed it over, or by another instance a moment ago.
    requires: occurrence_at <= now
    requires: occurrence_at > event_type.schedule_position

    -- The policy, applied. no_gap says this occurrence is the first one
    -- the position has not accounted for; nothing_later_is_due says no
    -- occurrence after it has come due yet, so it is the whole of the
    -- backlog's tail. See the three bullets in the note above.
    let no_gap =
        next_occurrence_after(event_type.system_triggered_schedule,
            event_type.schedule_position) = occurrence_at
    let nothing_later_is_due =
        next_occurrence_after(event_type.system_triggered_schedule,
            occurrence_at) > now

    requires:
        event_type.missed_occurrence_policy = replay_backlog
        or (event_type.missed_occurrence_policy = fire_once
            and nothing_later_is_due)
        or (event_type.missed_occurrence_policy = skip
            and no_gap and nothing_later_is_due)

    let payload = scheduled_payload(event_type)
    let protected = protect_sensitive_fields(event_type, payload)

    -- Sync projection updates happen inline here too - see the project()
    -- note above the rules. client_id is the literal "system": there is no
    -- token or Role behind this event to name - see invariant
    -- SystemTriggeredClientIdIsSystem.
    --
    -- This origin is always a root of its chain, and unlike the other three
    -- it cannot be anything else: no caller reaches this rule at all, so
    -- there is nobody to hand it a correlation_id to join or a causation_id
    -- to point back at. It therefore always generates a fresh
    -- correlation_id and always leaves causation_id null - the same "no
    -- identity to borrow" reasoning that makes client_id the literal
    -- "system" here. An occurrence of a schedule is something that
    -- happened because the clock reached it, not because anything caused
    -- it. See the correlation note above the rules.
    --
    -- Both positions move in the same transaction as the event itself, and
    -- both to the occurrence's instant rather than to now: what this event
    -- stands for is the occurrence, and the wall clock of the write is
    -- already recorded in its metadata.
    ensures:
        event_type.schedule_position = occurrence_at
        event_type.last_fired_at = occurrence_at
        SystemTriggered.created(
            bounded_context: event_type.bounded_context,
            event_type: event_type,
            payload: protected.payload,
            metadata: {
                type: event_type.name,
                version: event_type.schema_version,
                client_id: "system",
                created_at: now,
                correlation_id: generate_id(),
                causation_id: null
            },
            sequence: next_sequence(event_type.bounded_context),
            tags: derive_tags(event_type, payload),
            encryption_keys: protected.encryption_keys
        )
}

-- The other half of the skip policy, and the only rule in this file that
-- advances a position without producing anything. Skip is the one choice
-- of the three whose meaning depends on nobody having been watching: an
-- occurrence fired two seconds late by a busy instance and an occurrence
-- fired six hours late by a process that was down leave identical durable
-- state behind, so no guard on CreateSystemEvent can tell them apart on
-- its own. A scheduler becoming responsible for a type knows the
-- difference, and this is where it says so: everything up to this moment
-- is accounted for, none of it fired.
--
-- That is what "resume at the next occurrence in the future, exactly as an
-- OS-level cron does for a machine that was off" means once it has to be
-- durable and shared. The position moves to now rather than to the last
-- occurrence before now, which is the same statement one instant tighter
-- and needs no second cron primitive to compute; occurrences are compared
-- against it, never assumed to be it.
--
-- It is a no-op in steady state, which is the sign it is the right shape:
-- a live cluster's position is already at or past its last occurrence, so
-- a second instance starting alongside the first skips nothing that the
-- first was about to fire. It never runs for the other two policies, whose
-- entire point is that a gap is paid off rather than closed - under those
-- a position only ever moves by firing.
--
-- Monotonic, like every other advance of this position: it moves the
-- position to the present, which no committed occurrence can be ahead of.
-- Two instances resuming at once is therefore an interleaving with one
-- outcome, and needs no more than the same transaction next_sequence
-- already takes.

rule SkipMissedOccurrences {
    when: SystemScheduleResumed(event_type)
    requires: event_type.system_triggered_allowed = true
    requires: event_type.missed_occurrence_policy = skip
    requires: event_type.bounded_context.status = active

    -- Prevents re-firing, and makes a resume against an already-current
    -- position the no-op it should be.
    requires: event_type.schedule_position < now

    ensures: event_type.schedule_position = now
}

-- The REST track's reason for existing beyond writing, and the raw half
-- of the two reads specified here (QueryProjection below is the derived,
-- GraphQL half): a remote workflow drives itself off one
-- event type's stream without holding a GraphQL Role. after_sequence is
-- the caller's own cursor - absent means "from the beginning", and
-- sequences start at 0 per bounded context. The filters reuse the same
-- Filter shape an EventTypeSubscription declares, so narrowing a fetch
-- and narrowing a subscription look the same to a caller. The detailed
-- query/filter wire contract, including paging, is still open (see the
-- wire-contract exclusion in the scope header).
--
-- valid_filters(event_type, filters) is a black box in the same register
-- as matches_filters itself: this library owns that a filter is rejected
-- outright rather than silently matching nothing (or everything) when it
-- doesn't make sense, not the exact matrix of which FilterOperator
-- applies to which JSON Schema type. It checks two things per filter -
-- that field names a field event_type.schema actually declares, and that
-- operator is one that field's declared type supports (e.g. greater_than
-- against a string-typed field is invalid, not merely always-false).
-- A Filter.field written as a two-segment dotted path is checked the same
-- way, one level deeper: the path is resolved by walking into the named
-- nested shape's own declared fields, and the leaf's declared type is
-- matched against the operator by the same matrix. No new operator
-- semantics - a filter on address.country compares a string exactly as a
-- filter on a top-level string field does (see the payload schema shape
-- note above entity CommandType).
-- Checked once, at the point filters are supplied: registration time for
-- an EventTypeSubscription (see CreateEventTypeSubscription), call time
-- for FetchEvents, since it has no persisted filter to register ahead of
-- one. A subscription or fetch with an invalid filter is rejected rather
-- than silently created or answered with results nobody asked for.
--
-- The exact type-to-operator matrix - which FilterOperator values a
-- string field supports, which an integer field does, and so on - is
-- deliberately not enumerated here, and not left open either: it is
-- exactly the kind of mechanism this spec consistently hands to a black
-- box rather than specifying itself, the same register as how decide()
-- reaches its verdict, how protect_sensitive_fields encrypts, or how
-- next_sequence takes its Postgres lock. This library's job is that an
-- invalid filter is rejected outright rather than silently mismatched
-- (stated above); which matrix valid_filters uses to decide "invalid" is
-- the black box's job, not a gap in this one.
--
-- Four of the operators narrow more specifically than the general five,
-- and are named here so that a reader meeting them in the enum is not
-- left guessing what they are for. Which fields they fit stays inside
-- the matrix above. near, similar_color and in_subnet each apply only
-- to a field whose schema says what kind of value it carries - a
-- geographic coordinate, a colour, an IP address respectively - and each
-- narrows to events whose value sits within a caller-supplied bound of a
-- caller-supplied reference: near to those within a distance of a point,
-- similar_color to those within a similarity threshold of a target
-- colour, in_subnet to those falling inside a network (CIDR) range.
-- Gating on what a field's schema declares it to carry, rather than on
-- its bare JSON type alone, is the same matrix doing the same job one
-- notch more finely - not a second mechanism, and not a new kind of
-- payload field either: each of the three still reads an ordinary scalar
-- leaf, one whose schema happens to say what that scalar holds (see the
-- payload schema shape note above entity CommandType). in is the
-- exception among the four: it is tied to no particular kind of value
-- and fits any plain scalar field, narrowing to events whose value
-- equals any one of several caller-supplied candidates. How a caller
-- expresses a reference, a bound or a candidate list - distance units,
-- colour space, threshold encoding - belongs to the same query/filter
-- wire contract that is still open (see the scope header's exclusions),
-- not here.
--
-- All four are additive: equals, contains, is_like, greater_than and
-- less_than are unchanged, and one of the four used against a field its
-- schema does not fit is rejected by valid_filters exactly as any other
-- ill-fitting operator is.
--
-- Unlike the write rules, this deliberately does not require the bounded
-- context to be active: archiving stops new commands and events, but
-- keeps the history, and reading that history is the point.
--
-- This is the read the in-memory event cache primarily serves (see the
-- note above the rules): a workflow polling from a recent cursor is
-- answered from its bounded context's cache without a Postgres round
-- trip. candidates below is still the full set of matching events
-- regardless - a cursor reaching further back than the cache holds is
-- answered from Postgres and delivers the same events, just less
-- quickly.

rule FetchEvents {
    when: FetchEvents(token, read_type, filters, after_sequence?)
    requires: token.status = active
    requires: token.event_type = read_type
    requires: read_type.event_read_allowed = true
    requires: valid_filters(read_type, filters)

    let candidates =
        Events where bounded_context = read_type.bounded_context
            and event_type = read_type
            and sequence > (after_sequence ?? -1)

    -- The token's own ownership check, composed into the filter beside the
    -- caller's rather than standing apart from it: an event this token is
    -- not scoped to is simply not delivered, and the fetch itself still
    -- succeeds. Checked against token.scope rather than any grant's - the
    -- REST track has no Role behind it, so the token is the whole of the
    -- permission model here (see EventFetch's TokenScopedToEventType).
    -- Vacuously true for a token that names no scope, which is every token
    -- unless the minting admin named one, and for an event type that
    -- declares no owner dimension. See rule QueryEvents' guidance on
    -- event_owner_scope_satisfied.
    let served = first_by_sequence(
        filter(candidates, e =>
            matches_filters(e, filters) and event_owner_scope_satisfied(e, token.scope)),
        config.max_events_per_read)

    ensures:
        EventsDelivered(
            token: token,
            event_type: read_type,
            events: map(served, e => redact_private_fields(e))
        )

    @guidance
        -- redact_private_fields(record) is the REST track's counterpart to
        -- render_event, and its signature is the whole of its contract: it
        -- takes the record and nothing else. There is no access_mapping
        -- argument because there is no caller identity to pass one from,
        -- and that absence is what makes the result unconditional rather
        -- than merely strict. It returns the payload with every leaf named
        -- by the record's type's private_fields set to null, for every
        -- caller, on every call.
        --
        -- Every one of the three private-field kinds fails closed here
        -- structurally rather than by omission, which is why nothing needs
        -- to be decided per call. An EventReadToken is scoped to an
        -- EventType and tied to no Role: it is nobody's metadata.client_id,
        -- so no own-kind field can match; it carries no Role.name, so no
        -- team-kind field can match; and it carries no external_subject, so
        -- no addressed-kind field can match. No PrivateFieldGrant can reach
        -- it either, since a grant names a Role as its grantee and there is
        -- no Role here to be named. So evaluating the three tests per field
        -- would redact every one of them anyway, and the black box states
        -- that outcome directly instead of computing a foregone conclusion.
        --
        -- This is the same structural argument EventFetch's
        -- SensitiveFieldsStayEncrypted already makes about decryption,
        -- reaching the same place by the opposite route. A sensitive field
        -- needs no redaction step on this track because it was protected at
        -- write time and is simply delivered as stored; a private field is
        -- stored in plaintext and so needs this step to reach the same
        -- outcome. A caller needing either reads it over GraphQL. See
        -- EventFetch's PrivateFieldsStayRedacted.
        --
        -- It is a black box in the same register as render_event and
        -- render_command: this library owns that an unentitled caller never
        -- receives a private field's value, not how a JSON leaf is nulled
        -- out on the way to the wire.
}

-- Server-side read cursors: the second and third way to read the very
-- stream FetchEvents above pulls, under an identical permission model -
-- the same EventReadToken, the same event_read_allowed opt-in, the same
-- single EventType, the same filters. What differs is only who remembers
-- where the caller got to. FetchEvents remembers nothing: after_sequence
-- is the caller's own cursor, carried on every call. ConsumeEvents and
-- AcknowledgeEvents keep that position on the server instead, so a caller
-- with nowhere durable to write a cursor of its own - a stateless worker,
-- a shell script, one step of a remote workflow - can still poll a stream
-- without re-reading it from the beginning.
--
-- A cursor is keyed by the token and by nothing else. There is
-- deliberately no consumer-name concept alongside it: an EventReadToken
-- is already minted individually per reader (see CreateEventReadToken),
-- so the token is the reader's identity, and two independent read
-- positions are two tokens - an existing, lightweight mechanism - rather
-- than two names sharing one token.
--
-- The cursor is get-or-created on first use, in the same register as
-- protect_sensitive_fields' EncryptionKey lookup (see the note above the
-- rules): the first ConsumeEvents call for a token provisions the row, in
-- the same transaction that answers that call, and every later call finds
-- it. That first call is also the only one that decides ack_mode. A later
-- call may omit it and inherit what is stored, or restate the same mode,
-- but naming the other one is rejected rather than applied - a consumer's
-- delivery guarantee never changes underneath it.
--
-- The two modes differ in exactly one thing, which is what moves the
-- cursor:
--   - auto_advance: serving moves it. ConsumeEvents advances the cursor
--     to the highest sequence it just served, so the next call continues
--     strictly after that. An event served to a caller that then dies
--     before processing it is never served again - at most once, the same
--     guarantee EventSubscription makes under DeliveryIsAtMostOnce.
--   - manual_ack: only AcknowledgeEvents moves it. Serving leaves the
--     cursor where it was, so consuming again before acknowledging
--     re-serves the same events - at least once, with duplicates on
--     redelivery for the caller to handle (idempotent processing, say)
--     and never de-duplicated here (see EventFetch's
--     DeliveryFollowsAckMode).
--
-- manual_ack alone has one more moving part: ReadCursor.checked_out_at.
-- A caller that consumes a batch, then acts on it somewhere outside this
-- specification (publishing it to an external system, say) before
-- acknowledging, has a real gap between "served" and "acknowledged" - and
-- without anything to close it, a second caller presenting the same token
-- during that gap would be served the identical unacknowledged batch and
-- might act on it too, doubling whatever the first caller's own action
-- was. checked_out_at closes that specific gap, and only that gap: it is
-- claimed by ConsumeEvents when it serves a manual_ack cursor not already
-- claimed, cleared by AcknowledgeEvents, and treated as abandoned - so
-- reclaimable, serving the same batch again - once older than
-- config.read_cursor_checkout_lease. It narrows DeliveryFollowsAckMode's
-- at-least-once guarantee, never contradicts it: a served-but-unacknowledged
-- batch is still always eventually redelivered, to whichever caller next
-- finds the claim expired or clear, exactly as before this existed - the
-- only change is that "next" no longer means "possibly also, right now,
-- to a second caller racing the first."
--
-- Neither rule requires the bounded context to be active, for the same
-- reason FetchEvents does not: archiving stops writes, and reading the
-- retained history is the point. Both are served by the same
-- per-bounded-context event cache FetchEvents is (see the note above the
-- rules), and both leave sensitive fields as stored ciphertext for the
-- same structural reason (see EventFetch's SensitiveFieldsStayEncrypted).
-- Both redact every private field unconditionally for that same reason
-- read the other way round, since a private field is stored in plaintext
-- and so needs a step to reach the outcome a sensitive field reaches by
-- being stored encrypted (see EventFetch's PrivateFieldsStayRedacted and
-- the guidance above rule FetchEvents).
--
-- A revoked token's cursor needs no disposal path and gets none. Both
-- rules require token.status = active and revocation is terminal, so the
-- row simply becomes permanently unreachable - the same end every other
-- row in this file reaches, since nothing here is deleted one row at a
-- time. The one rule that removes anything, DeleteBoundedContext, works
-- at whole-bounded-context granularity: it takes such a cursor with it,
-- along with everything else scoped to the context, and is no more a
-- disposal path for one stale cursor than it is for one stale event.
--
-- highest_sequence(events) is a black box in the same trivial register as
-- the filter(events, ...) beside it: the greatest Event.sequence among a
-- set of events, or null when that set is empty.
--
-- first_by_sequence(events, n) is another: the n events with the lowest
-- Event.sequence among a set, or all of them when there are n or fewer.
-- It is how config.max_events_per_read bounds a read.
--
-- Commands have no sequence, but this library records them in an order:
-- recorded_after(a, b) is whether a was recorded after b, and
-- first_by_recording(commands, n) the n recorded earliest - how
-- FetchCommands bounds its page by the same config.max_events_per_read.

rule ConsumeEvents {
    when: ConsumeEvents(token, ack_mode?, filters)
    requires: token.status = active
    requires: token.event_type.event_read_allowed = true
    requires: valid_filters(token.event_type, filters)

    -- Null when this token has never consumed before; the ensures below
    -- is what provisions it in that case. That first call is also the
    -- only one token.start_from is ever consulted on, and a token minted
    -- latest therefore serves nothing at all from before that call: its
    -- cursor is provisioned already past everything committed by then.
    -- That is the whole point of the choice - a consumer newly attached
    -- to a long-lived stream can start on what happens from now on
    -- rather than grinding through every historical event to reach the
    -- present, which is otherwise the only way it can get there. From
    -- the second call on the two kinds of token are indistinguishable:
    -- both continue strictly after their own stored position by the
    -- ordinary path below, and neither looks at start_from again.
    let cursor = ReadCursor{token: token}
    let is_new = not exists cursor

    -- A brand new cursor must state its mode; an existing one may omit
    -- it, but a supplied mode has to match the stored one.
    requires: not is_new or ack_mode != null
    requires: is_new or ack_mode = null or ack_mode = cursor.ack_mode

    let mode = if is_new: ack_mode else: cursor.ack_mode

    -- The highest sequence this token could ever have read of its own
    -- type, and the seed a latest token's first cursor is provisioned
    -- at. Scoped exactly as a served event is - an event outside this
    -- token's own scope was never visible to it, so it cannot count as
    -- one this token has already seen either - but deliberately blind to
    -- this call's own filters argument, which varies from call to call.
    -- The seed is decided once and stands for the life of the cursor, so
    -- it must not depend on which filter the caller happened to supply
    -- on the single call that provisioned it.
    let history =
        Events where bounded_context = token.event_type.bounded_context
            and event_type = token.event_type
    let latest_position =
        highest_sequence(filter(history, e =>
            event_owner_scope_satisfied(e, token.scope))) ?? -1

    -- The at_time counterpart of the seed above, and the same computation
    -- with one extra condition: the highest sequence this token could
    -- have read of its own type as of token.start_at_time, scoped the
    -- same way and blind to this call's filters for the same reason.
    -- Empty means nothing of this type was committed by then, so the
    -- token starts at -1 and gets the whole stream, which is what
    -- beginning would have given it.
    let at_time_position =
        highest_sequence(filter(history, e =>
            event_owner_scope_satisfied(e, token.scope)
                and e.metadata.created_at <= token.start_at_time)) ?? -1

    -- at_sequence and at_time are the same idea as latest with a cutoff
    -- the minting admin chose rather than "now": at_sequence gives that
    -- cutoff directly as a sequence number and is used as it stands,
    -- at_time gives a point in time and has the cutoff derived from it
    -- above.
    --
    -- at_time reads only what is already committed at the moment this
    -- first call actually happens, and is not a scheduling mechanism. A
    -- start_at_time in the future is not an instruction to wait for it -
    -- it behaves exactly as latest would at that moment, since nothing
    -- committed by then has a later created_at to be excluded by it.
    let position =
        if not is_new: cursor.sequence
        else if token.start_from = latest: latest_position
        else if token.start_from = at_sequence: token.start_at_sequence
        else if token.start_from = at_time: at_time_position
        else: -1

    let candidates =
        Events where bounded_context = token.event_type.bounded_context
            and event_type = token.event_type
            and sequence > position

    -- manual_ack only, and false whenever is_new (a cursor just being
    -- provisioned was never claimed by anyone) - see ReadCursor.checked_out_at
    -- and the note above this rule. A claim older than
    -- config.read_cursor_checkout_lease is treated as abandoned rather
    -- than live, the same "self-healing, not a hard failure" register
    -- ScheduleDeadline's own missed-occurrence handling already uses
    -- elsewhere in this specification.
    let is_leased =
        mode = manual_ack and not is_new and cursor.checked_out_at != null
            and cursor.checked_out_at + config.read_cursor_checkout_lease > now

    -- The same two-part filter FetchEvents applies, on the same candidate
    -- population under the same token, and deliberately identical: these
    -- are two ways to read one stream under one permission model, so what
    -- either serves is the same set (see the note above). An event this
    -- token is not scoped to is not served here either - see rule
    -- QueryEvents' guidance on event_owner_scope_satisfied. What that
    -- composition does not change is the cursor: an event excluded because
    -- of scope is treated exactly as one excluded by a filter already is,
    -- passed over without being served and without moving the position
    -- past it. is_leased overrides all of that to the empty set - a
    -- second caller racing a live claim sees no candidates at all, not a
    -- filtered or scope-narrowed view of them.
    let served =
        if is_leased: []
        else: first_by_sequence(
            filter(candidates, e =>
                matches_filters(e, filters) and event_owner_scope_satisfied(e, token.scope)),
            config.max_events_per_read)

    -- Unchanged when nothing matched, in either mode: an auto_advance
    -- cursor has nothing new to advance past, and a manual_ack one never
    -- moves here at all.
    let next_position =
        if mode = auto_advance: highest_sequence(served) ?? position
        else: position

    -- Redacted on the way out, unconditionally and by the very same black
    -- box FetchEvents applies - two ways to read one stream under one
    -- permission model serve the same set, so they redact it identically
    -- (see the guidance above rule FetchEvents). Deliberately applied at
    -- the delivery site alone and not to served above: what a caller is
    -- allowed to see must never move a cursor. next_position is derived
    -- from the unredacted set, so a private field nobody may read still
    -- counts as an event that was served, and consuming never stalls on
    -- one.
    ensures:
        EventsConsumed(
            token: token,
            event_type: token.event_type,
            events: map(served, e => redact_private_fields(e))
        )
        if is_new:
            ReadCursor.created(
                token: token,
                ack_mode: mode,
                sequence: next_position,
                checked_out_at: if mode = manual_ack: now else: null,
                updated_at: now
            )
        else if mode = auto_advance:
            cursor.sequence = next_position
            cursor.updated_at = now
        else if not is_leased:
            -- manual_ack, and either never claimed or a stale claim just
            -- reclaimed - either way this call's own served batch (the
            -- same batch a stale claim's own caller was already served,
            -- when reclaiming) is now this call's claim to hold until it
            -- acknowledges or its own lease lapses in turn.
            cursor.checked_out_at = now
}

rule AcknowledgeEvents {
    when: AcknowledgeEvents(token, sequence)
    requires: token.status = active

    let cursor = ReadCursor{token: token}
    requires: exists cursor
    requires: cursor.ack_mode = manual_ack

    -- Forward only, the same monotonicity Event.sequence itself has: an
    -- acknowledgement may confirm ground already covered, never hand it
    -- back. A caller that wants to re-read what it already acknowledged
    -- reads it over FetchEvents, which takes its own cursor.
    requires: sequence >= cursor.sequence

    ensures:
        cursor.sequence = sequence
        cursor.updated_at = now
        -- Cleared unconditionally, whether or not this call's own caller
        -- is the one still holding a live claim - see
        -- ReadCursor.checked_out_at and the note above rule ConsumeEvents.
        -- An acknowledgement is exactly what a claim exists to wait for,
        -- so once one arrives the claim has done its job; there is no
        -- scenario where leaving it set would still be protecting
        -- anything.
        cursor.checked_out_at = null
}

-- The GraphQL read, and the counterpart to FetchEvents above: where a
-- REST caller pulls raw events, a Role queries a projection built from
-- them. Read-only - it delivers a result rather than changing stored
-- state - so it follows FetchEvents' shape rather than the write rules'.
--
-- wait_for_sequence is the caller's read-your-writes lever, and is the
-- only thing that distinguishes an async projection from a sync one from
-- the outside: pass the highest Event.sequence already known (typically
-- from the triggered events of a command just submitted) and the query
-- waits for the projection to reach it before answering. Omit it and the
-- projection answers from wherever it currently is. A sync projection is
-- already there, always.
--
-- Like FetchEvents, this deliberately does not require the bounded
-- context to be active: archiving stops writes, and reading what was
-- built before that is the point.

rule QueryProjection {
    when: QueryProjection(access_mapping, projection, key?, wait_for_sequence?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = projection.bounded_context

    -- Which of the projection's instances to answer from. A projection
    -- holding one shared value is queried without a key, which is exactly
    -- what the default stands for: the same single instance every time
    -- (see the instance-data note on entity Projection).
    let instance_key = key ?? ""

    -- The ownership check, decided against the one instance just named
    -- rather than against the bounded context alone - see the
    -- owner_scope_satisfied paragraphs in the guidance below.
    -- Vacuously true for a grant that names no scope, which is every
    -- grant unless a superadmin named one, and for any projection that
    -- declares no owner dimension.
    requires: owner_scope_satisfied(projection, instance_key, access_mapping)

    -- The membership check, decided against the querying grant's own Role
    -- name and covering the projection whole rather than any one instance
    -- of it - see the team_only_satisfied paragraphs in the guidance
    -- below. Independent of the ownership check above and failing
    -- separately from it: a projection may declare both, and a query must
    -- satisfy both. Vacuously true for any projection that declares no
    -- required team, which is every projection unless one names one.
    requires: team_only_satisfied(projection, access_mapping)

    -- Blocks until the projection has caught up, and fails the query if
    -- it does not within the configured wait - see the
    -- await_projection_caught_up note above the rules. Vacuously true
    -- when the caller asked for no particular position. Resolved: this
    -- rejection is reported as a timeout specifically, distinguishable
    -- from one caused by e.g. a revoked grant or the wrong bounded
    -- context (see ReadYourWritesWhenRequested), the same way
    -- CommandDecision.rejection_reason is surfaced verbatim rather than
    -- left generic - so a caller can tell "retry" from "permanently
    -- invalid".
    requires:
        wait_for_sequence = null
        or await_projection_caught_up(projection, wait_for_sequence)

    ensures:
        ProjectionDelivered(
            access_mapping: access_mapping,
            projection: projection,
            key: instance_key,
            result: read_projection(projection, instance_key, access_mapping)
        )

    @guidance
        -- read_projection(projection, key, access_mapping) is a black box
        -- in the same register as decide() and project(): this library
        -- owns who may read a projection, which instance of it and at
        -- what position, not how the projection's own stored state
        -- answers a query. The key names the instance to answer from -
        -- one row of many, or the single shared instance a projection
        -- that is never keyed holds - and the result delivered is that
        -- instance's, never the whole projection's. It takes the
        -- whole grant rather than a single boolean because entitlement
        -- is still decided by two independent grants -
        -- access_mapping.can_read_sensitive = true, or
        -- access_mapping.role.external_subject matching the subject the
        -- content is about - but here that test is applied once, up
        -- front, to the queried instance's own key: a projection's key
        -- is itself a real subject value in practice (a customer id, a
        -- user id), so the key is the subject checked. If the grant
        -- holds, every active EncryptionKey for that one subject is
        -- resolved and the instance's stored state is decrypted with
        -- them, at any depth; if it does not hold, nothing is looked up
        -- at all. There is deliberately no per-field distinction at read
        -- time here, and no per-projection declaration of which fields
        -- are sensitive: the instance is either checked against its own
        -- subject's keys as one unit, or not - so nothing can leak
        -- through a declaration someone forgot to write. That is the
        -- contrast with render_event, which genuinely does decide per
        -- field, each against that field's own
        -- EncryptionKey.subject_value; see the note above
        -- DeliverToSubscriptions for why the self-subject half fails
        -- closed there. Deciding inside the black box rather than
        -- post-filtering the result is deliberate - a caller with
        -- neither grant is never handed a decrypted value to redact
        -- afterwards.
        --
        -- owner_scope_satisfied(projection, key, access_mapping) is a
        -- black box in that same register, and is what gives
        -- RoleAccessMapping.scope its meaning at read time. A projection
        -- may optionally declare which tag key names its "owner"
        -- dimension - "customer", say, or "tenant". That declaration is
        -- deliberately not a field on entity Projection and has no
        -- registration or admin-visible surface anywhere: it is compiled,
        -- deployed configuration only, the same register the summary
        -- behind surface SnapshotInspection is drawn from (see the
        -- guidance above ProcessCommand's decide() call) and the same
        -- register in which this spec never says what project() folds or
        -- how a key is chosen. An instance's own owner value is derived,
        -- not supplied: as events are folded into that instance, the tag
        -- whose key matches the declared owner tag key carries the value,
        -- read from the tags of whichever events touched it - derived
        -- exactly as Event.tags is itself derived from tag_mappings and
        -- never caller-supplied (see the derive_tags note above the
        -- rules). Nothing a querying caller sends decides it.
        --
        -- The predicate holds, and the query proceeds, when any of three
        -- things is true: access_mapping.scope is null, so the grant is
        -- unrestricted within its bounded context and reads exactly as
        -- every grant read before scope existed; or the projection
        -- declares no owner tag key at all, so there is no owner
        -- dimension for a scope to select within; or the queried
        -- instance's own derived owner value equals access_mapping.scope.
        -- It does not hold, and the query is rejected, when the grant
        -- names a scope, the projection does declare an owner tag key,
        -- and that instance's owner value is either a different value or
        -- not yet established - an instance nothing has touched yet, a
        -- key with no owner behind it, fails closed exactly as a
        -- mismatched one does. Ownership must be affirmatively provable,
        -- not merely un-contradicted, which is deliberately stricter than
        -- the "cold instance, not a failure" treatment an untouched key
        -- otherwise gets here (see SnapshotInspection's
        -- UnknownNameIsAnError): an owner nothing has established is the
        -- case an attacker reaches for. What a rejected query leaves the
        -- caller with is the same framing another context's projections
        -- already get - invisible, not merely unreadable - see
        -- GrantScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared on surface ProjectionQuery.
        --
        -- team_only_satisfied(projection, access_mapping) is a black box
        -- in that same register, and gates the projection whole rather
        -- than one instance of it. A projection may optionally declare
        -- which Role name is required to query it at all - "support", say,
        -- or "operations". That declaration gets identical treatment to
        -- the owner tag key described just above: deliberately not a field
        -- on entity Projection, with no registration and no admin-visible
        -- surface anywhere, compiled and deployed configuration only. It
        -- reuses a team-kind private field's own semantics exactly (see
        -- enum PrivateFieldKind) - membership is the grant, decided by
        -- Role.name equality, with nothing to share, nothing to revoke,
        -- and entitlement ending the moment the Role is no longer named
        -- that. What differs is only what it covers: one projection
        -- entire, rather than one field of one record.
        --
        -- The predicate holds, and the query proceeds, when either of two
        -- things is true: the projection declares no required team at all,
        -- so there is no membership to hold and it reads exactly as every
        -- projection read before this existed; or it does declare one and
        -- access_mapping.role.name equals it exactly. It does not hold,
        -- and the query is rejected, when the projection declares a
        -- required team and the querying grant's own Role carries a
        -- different name. This is a separate test from the ownership one
        -- above rather than a refinement of it - a projection declaring
        -- both is answered only when both hold - and what a rejected query
        -- leaves the caller with is the same framing the owner mismatch
        -- gets: invisible, not merely unreadable - see
        -- TeamGatedWhenDeclared on surface ProjectionQuery, which states
        -- it in the same terms GrantScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared does.
}

-- CreateAllEventsSubscription and CreateEventTypeSubscription register a
-- standing Subscription; DeliverToSubscriptions below is what actually
-- pushes events to it. Both creation rules resolve the same default: when
-- the caller omits from_sequence, delivery starts from the bounded
-- context's current latest committed sequence, so a freshly created
-- subscription sees only what happens next. A caller who instead supplies
-- from_sequence - typically the sequence it last read via FetchEvents or
-- QueryProjection, or the point it last disconnected from a prior
-- subscription - resumes from there with no gap and no replay of
-- anything earlier. Neither rule requires the bounded context to be
-- active: unlike EventFetch and QueryProjection, this is deliberate the
-- other way - an archived context accepts no new events, so a
-- subscription against one would be created only to sit forever unfed.
-- CreateEventTypeSubscription rejects an invalid filter up front via
-- valid_filters, the same check and the same reasoning as FetchEvents -
-- see the note above it.

rule CreateAllEventsSubscription {
    when: CreateAllEventsSubscription(access_mapping, event_types, from_sequence?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context.status = active

    -- Every named event type must belong to the same bounded context as
    -- the grant - the same check and the same reasoning as
    -- RegisterProjection's consumed_event_types. An empty set is not a
    -- vacuous case of this: it is the "no restriction" state itself.
    requires: event_types.all(et => et.bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context)

    let starting_point =
        from_sequence
        ?? (Events where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context).count - 1

    ensures:
        AllEventsSubscription.created(
            bounded_context: access_mapping.bounded_context,
            access_mapping: access_mapping,
            created_at: now,
            from_sequence: starting_point,
            event_types: event_types
        )
}

rule CreateEventTypeSubscription {
    when: CreateEventTypeSubscription(access_mapping, event_type, filters, from_sequence?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = event_type.bounded_context
    requires: event_type.bounded_context.status = active
    requires: valid_filters(event_type, filters)

    let starting_point =
        from_sequence
        ?? (Events where bounded_context = event_type.bounded_context).count - 1

    ensures:
        EventTypeSubscription.created(
            bounded_context: event_type.bounded_context,
            access_mapping: access_mapping,
            created_at: now,
            from_sequence: starting_point,
            event_type: event_type,
            filters: filters
        )
}

-- render_event(event, access_mapping) is a black box in the same register
-- as read_projection(projection, key, access_mapping): this library owns
-- who a subscription delivers to and which of its fields arrive decrypted,
-- not how the payload is rendered into a GraphQL push. A sensitive field
-- decrypts under either of two independent grants, checked per field
-- against that field's own EncryptionKey.subject_value:
--   - access_mapping.can_read_sensitive = true - the same
--     bounded-context-wide grant read_projection applies too, or
--   - access_mapping.role.external_subject = the field's subject_value -
--     the caller's own verified IdP identity matches the subject the
--     field is about, so a caller reading their own data needs no
--     separate grant. This only fires when a bounded context tags a
--     sensitive field's subject using the same identifier space as
--     external_subject in the first place; where that alignment doesn't
--     hold, this path simply never matches and can_read_sensitive remains
--     the only way in - fails closed, not open.
-- Every other field is left as stored ciphertext, exactly as
-- read_projection and FetchEvents leave an unauthorised field - never
-- decrypted and then redacted afterwards.
--
-- Both grants are the GraphQL track's, and the self-subject one is so
-- structurally: FetchEvents authenticates an EventReadToken, a bearer
-- credential scoped to an EventType and tied to no Role, so there is no
-- verified caller identity to compare a field's subject_value against.
-- REST reading only ciphertext is a consequence of that token model
-- rather than a gap left open - see EventFetch's
-- SensitiveFieldsStayEncrypted.
--
-- A third redaction rule runs alongside that one, per entry in the
-- record's type's private_fields, and it is a plain visibility rule
-- rather than a cryptographic one. Nothing in it involves an
-- EncryptionKey, resolve_data_key or ForgetSubject anywhere - there is no
-- ciphertext to decrypt, because a private field is stored in plaintext
-- exactly as written - and that absence is the whole point of the
-- mechanism rather than a shortcut taken within it (see value
-- PrivateField). It decides one thing per field: whether this caller is
-- entitled to see the leaf.
--
-- For an own-kind or addressed-kind field, the caller is entitled when
-- any of three things is true:
--   - is_default_private_reader(record, access_mapping) holds - the
--     caller is the record's own creator, or the party its payload
--     addressed (see the guidance on rule
--     GrantPrivateFieldAccessForEvent for that predicate in full), or
--   - an active PrivateFieldGrant names this exact record and this
--     caller's Role as grantee, or
--   - an active blanket PrivateFieldGrant - one naming neither an event
--     nor a command - names this caller's Role as grantee, and its own
--     grantor independently satisfies is_default_private_reader for this
--     same record. The grantor's standing is re-derived here, against the
--     record actually being read, rather than trusted from when the grant
--     was made. That is what keeps a blanket grant a convenience instead
--     of a wider trust boundary: it can never let a grantee see something
--     its grantor could not have handed over one record at a time.
-- For a team-kind field, the caller is entitled when
-- access_mapping.role.name equals that field's own declared team, and
-- there is no grant to look for - membership is the grant (see enum
-- PrivateFieldKind).
--
-- A caller with none of those gets the record, with that one JSON leaf
-- set to null. Not the enclosing object, not the whole record withheld,
-- and not ciphertext either, since none was ever produced - exactly the
-- shape and exactly the place an unentitled caller already finds a
-- sensitive field's leaf left as stored ciphertext, one rule up.
--
-- The REST track reaches the same outcome by a different route, and the
-- difference is in the mechanism rather than in what a caller ends up
-- holding. A sensitive field is protected at rest, so FetchEvents and
-- ConsumeEvents are safe by handing back what is stored and doing nothing
-- else. A private field is stored in plaintext, so handing it back as
-- stored would disclose it - and those two rules therefore apply
-- redact_private_fields instead, which nulls every private leaf
-- unconditionally, for every caller, with no access_mapping argument to
-- condition it on (see the guidance above rule FetchEvents and
-- EventFetch's PrivateFieldsStayRedacted).
--
-- That is not a stricter policy bolted onto that track but the same three
-- tests reaching their only possible answer there. Each is stated in
-- terms of a Role, and an EventReadToken is tied to none: it is nobody's
-- client_id, carries no Role.name and carries no external_subject, and no
-- PrivateFieldGrant can name it as a grantee. So all three fail closed
-- structurally, and the black box states that outcome directly rather
-- than computing a foregone conclusion per field. Declaring
-- private_fields on a REST-readable event type is therefore safe rather
-- than merely permitted: the declaration holds against every reader on
-- both tracks, and event_read_allowed does not weaken it.
--
-- DeliverToSubscriptions runs outside the transaction that committed the
-- triggering event, the same register as an async projection's update:
-- best-effort, after the fact, for every still-active subscription whose
-- from_sequence the new event is past. "Best-effort" means at most once
-- and no more: while a subscription's connection is live it receives
-- every matching event once, in sequence order, and there is nothing to
-- acknowledge back. Nothing is held for a connection that is not live -
-- a subscription lasts exactly as long as its connection, so an event
-- committed while that connection is down is delivered to no one and
-- kept for no one. A caller that wants what it missed reads it back
-- (FetchEvents or QueryProjection) and starts a fresh subscription from
-- the last sequence it actually received; see Subscription.from_sequence
-- and EventSubscription's DeliveryIsAtMostOnce.
--
-- The still-active check on the grant below is a live re-check on every
-- delivery rather than a snapshot taken at creation (see the note on
-- Subscription.access_mapping), and dropping out of this loop is not all
-- that happens to a revoked caller: the same revocation closes its
-- connection, with a reason it can tell apart from an ordinary transport
-- drop, so it learns its subscription is over instead of merely ceasing
-- to hear from one - see EventSubscription's
-- RevocationClosesTheConnection.

rule DeliverToSubscriptions {
    when: event: Event.created
    for subscription in Subscriptions
            where bounded_context = event.bounded_context
                and from_sequence < event.sequence
                and access_mapping.status = active:
        -- The ownership check is an additional condition on matches
        -- itself rather than a branch of its own: whichever kind of
        -- subscription this is, and whichever narrowing that kind already
        -- applies, an event the receiving grant is not scoped to is not
        -- delivered. Checked against the subscription's own grant, which
        -- is re-read live on every delivery exactly as its status is (see
        -- the note above). Vacuously true for a grant that names no scope
        -- and for an event whose type declares no owner dimension. See
        -- rule QueryEvents' guidance on event_owner_scope_satisfied.
        let kind_matches =
            if subscription.kind = AllEventsSubscription:
                subscription.event_types.count = 0
                    or event.event_type in subscription.event_types
            else:
                subscription.event_type = event.event_type
                    and matches_filters(event, subscription.filters)

        let matches =
            kind_matches
            and event_owner_scope_satisfied(event, subscription.access_mapping.scope)
        ensures:
            if matches:
                EventDelivered(
                    subscription: subscription,
                    event: event,
                    rendered_payload: render_event(event, subscription.access_mapping)
                )
}

-- A command reaches ProcessCommand from exactly one of two callers, each
-- authenticated its own way, and the two rules below are the only
-- difference between them: a Role acting over GraphQL through its
-- per-context grant, or an automated caller acting over REST with a
-- CommandToken scoped to this one command type. Everything after
-- authentication - the consistency boundary, decide(), the events
-- written - is identical, so it is specified once, in ProcessCommand,
-- rather than mirrored per caller. CommandAuthorised is an internal
-- hand-off within the same transaction, not an asynchronous boundary: a
-- decision to reject is still reported to the original caller.

rule AuthoriseCommandSubmission {
    when:
        SubmitCommand(
            access_mapping, command_type, payload, idempotency_key?,
            correlation_id?, causation_id?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level in {write, admin}
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = command_type.bounded_context
    requires: command_type.bounded_context.status = active
    requires: valid_payload(command_type.schema, payload)

    -- The very same derivation ProcessCommand performs downstream, over
    -- the very same payload - see the derive_tags note above the rules.
    let consistency_tags = derive_tags(command_type, payload)

    -- The ownership check, and the write-side counterpart to CommandQuery's
    -- own CommandsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared: a grant that names a scope may
    -- submit commands only for the owner it names. It is checked against
    -- the very same CommandType.owner_tag_key and the very same derived
    -- tags the read side already reads back with
    -- command_owner_scope_satisfied (see rule FetchCommands' guidance) -
    -- just before the record exists rather than after it does, which is the
    -- whole reason it takes the tags and the key directly instead of a
    -- Command to read them off.
    --
    -- Single-record, so it rejects outright rather than filtering: there is
    -- exactly one command being authorised here, not a range, and nothing
    -- to filter it out of - the same treatment InspectEvent and
    -- InspectSnapshot give their own single reads. Vacuously true, the same
    -- as always, for a grant that names no scope, which is every grant
    -- unless a superadmin named one, and for a command type that declares
    -- no owner dimension.
    requires:
        tag_owner_scope_satisfied(
            consistency_tags, command_type.owner_tag_key, access_mapping.scope)

    -- idempotency_key is carried through untouched and unvalidated: it is
    -- the caller's own opaque value, not part of the command's payload and
    -- so no business of valid_payload's, and null whenever the caller
    -- supplied none. What it means is ProcessCommand's, since both tracks
    -- mean the same thing by it.
    --
    -- correlation_id and causation_id are carried through on exactly the
    -- same footing, and for the same reason: both are the caller's own
    -- opaque values, outside the payload and so outside valid_payload's
    -- reach, and null whenever the caller supplied neither. Their meaning,
    -- their one validity check and the generation of a correlation_id when
    -- none was named are all ProcessCommand's, since both tracks mean the
    -- same thing by them too.
    ensures:
        CommandAuthorised(
            command_type: command_type,
            payload: payload,
            client_id: access_mapping.role.id,
            idempotency_key: idempotency_key,
            correlation_id: correlation_id,
            causation_id: causation_id
        )

    @guidance
        -- tag_owner_scope_satisfied(tags, owner_tag_key, scope) is a black
        -- box in the same register as event_owner_scope_satisfied and
        -- command_owner_scope_satisfied, and carries the identical
        -- three-holds/fails-closed contract those two do. It is documented
        -- here once, because this is the first rule that reaches for it;
        -- AuthoriseCommandTrigger, CreateExternalEvent and CreateDirectEvent
        -- refer back to this paragraph rather than repeating it, and so do
        -- the surface guarantees over those.
        --
        -- What differs from its two read-side siblings is only what it is
        -- handed, never what it decides. Each of those takes a stored record
        -- and reads both halves off it - the owner dimension from the
        -- record's own type, the owner value from Event.tags or
        -- Command.consistency_tags. At every call site of this one there is
        -- no such record yet: the check is what decides whether the record
        -- comes into being at all, so the tags and the owner tag key are
        -- passed in directly. The tags passed are always derive_tags over
        -- the very payload about to be written, so the value tested is
        -- exactly the value the read side will later read back - one
        -- derive_tags produces both (see the derive_tags note above the
        -- rules), which is what makes a write refused here and a read
        -- refused there agree about who owns what.
        --
        -- The predicate holds, and the write proceeds, when any of three
        -- things is true: scope is null, so the caller is unrestricted
        -- within whatever it may already write and writes exactly as it did
        -- before scope existed; or owner_tag_key is null, so the type
        -- declares no owner dimension for a scope to select within; or the
        -- derived tags contain a Tag whose key equals owner_tag_key and
        -- whose value equals scope. It does not hold, and the write is
        -- rejected, when scope is non-null, the type does declare an owner
        -- tag key, and no derived tag carries that key with a matching
        -- value - covering alike a tag under that key holding a different
        -- value, a tag under that key holding null (the never-set state a
        -- Tag can legitimately be in - see value Tag), and no tag under that
        -- key at all, which is what a payload that maps nothing to it
        -- yields. Ownership must be affirmatively provable, not merely
        -- un-contradicted, the same framing owner_scope_satisfied and
        -- event_owner_scope_satisfied already use and for the same reason:
        -- an owner nothing established is the case an attacker reaches for,
        -- so it fails closed. A scoped caller therefore cannot write a
        -- record it would then be unable to read.
        --
        -- Which caller's scope is checked depends on the track, and the
        -- predicate itself neither knows nor cares - exactly as on the read
        -- side. On the GraphQL track it is the grant's,
        -- RoleAccessMapping.scope, here. On the REST track it is the
        -- token's own - CommandToken.scope in AuthoriseCommandTrigger,
        -- ExternalEventToken.scope and DirectCreationToken.scope in the two
        -- event-creating rules. Both are opaque caller-chosen strings
        -- compared for equality and never interpreted.
        --
        -- Decided live against the type's current declaration, never
        -- snapshotted: changing or clearing owner_tag_key at a later
        -- registration changes who may write the type from that point on,
        -- the same way it already changes who may read its whole history
        -- (see rule QueryEvents' guidance).
}

rule AuthoriseCommandTrigger {
    when:
        TriggerCommand(
            token, command_type, payload, idempotency_key?,
            correlation_id?, causation_id?)
    requires: token.status = active
    requires: token.command_type = command_type
    requires: command_type.rest_trigger_allowed = true
    requires: command_type.bounded_context.status = active
    requires: valid_payload(command_type.schema, payload)

    let consistency_tags = derive_tags(command_type, payload)

    -- The ownership check, on exactly the terms and for exactly the reasons
    -- rule AuthoriseCommandSubmission's own note above gives in full -
    -- write-side counterpart to CommandQuery's
    -- CommandsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared, single-record so rejecting rather
    -- than filtering, vacuously true for a caller naming no scope or a
    -- command type declaring no owner dimension. None of that is restated
    -- here.
    --
    -- The one real difference is whose scope is checked: the token's own,
    -- not a RoleAccessMapping's. That is the identical "the token's own
    -- scope, not the minting admin's" independence rule CreateEventReadToken's
    -- own note already establishes for reading, applied here to triggering -
    -- see variant CommandToken.scope.
    requires:
        tag_owner_scope_satisfied(
            consistency_tags, command_type.owner_tag_key, token.scope)

    -- idempotency_key, correlation_id and causation_id are all carried
    -- through untouched and unvalidated here, exactly as
    -- AuthoriseCommandSubmission's own note above says of them; the meaning
    -- and the checks are ProcessCommand's on both tracks alike.
    ensures:
        CommandAuthorised(
            command_type: command_type,
            payload: payload,
            client_id: token.id,
            idempotency_key: idempotency_key,
            correlation_id: correlation_id,
            causation_id: causation_id
        )
}

rule ProcessCommand {
    when:
        CommandAuthorised(
            command_type, payload, client_id, idempotency_key?,
            correlation_id?, causation_id?)

    -- Idempotency. idempotency_key is a value the caller chooses and
    -- supplies itself, carried here untouched from whichever surface
    -- authorised the command (CommandSubmission over GraphQL,
    -- CommandTrigger over REST) and null whenever the caller supplied
    -- none. It exists because a caller whose request times out cannot tell
    -- whether the command landed, and resubmitting blind applies it twice -
    -- two Commands, two sets of events - out of what the caller meant as
    -- one action.
    --
    -- recorded_acceptance(command_type, idempotency_key) is the outcome
    -- this library remembers against that key: the events an earlier
    -- submission bearing it actually triggered, or null when the key is
    -- null or when nothing has been recorded against it yet. It is a black
    -- box in the same register as next_sequence - library-owned durable
    -- state whose storage shape this spec does not reach into - rather
    -- than a plugged-in decision like decide().
    --
    -- Three cases, and they are the whole of the behaviour:
    --
    --   Key absent. Nothing is looked up and nothing is recorded. The
    --   submission is processed as new, exactly as every submission was
    --   before keys existed at all. This is a guarantee rather than merely
    --   the common case - see CommandSubmission's
    --   OmittingTheKeyChangesNothing.
    --
    --   Key not seen before. Processed as new, and then, if and only if
    --   the decision is an acceptance, its outcome is recorded against the
    --   key as part of the same transaction the command and its events are
    --   written in - so a key is remembered exactly when the events it
    --   stands for are durable, never before and never without them.
    --
    --   Key already accepted. This rule does not fire: decide() is not
    --   called again, no second Command is created and no second event is
    --   appended. The caller is answered with the recorded outcome - the
    --   very events the original submission triggered - and can tell that
    --   answer apart from a fresh acceptance rather than being left to
    --   guess. Deliberately the original answer rather than a fresh one
    --   that would usually agree: events committed in between are exactly
    --   what can make a fresh decide() decide differently, and a caller
    --   retrying a request it never heard back from is asking what
    --   happened, not asking again. This is where an idempotency key
    --   genuinely changes what is observable, and it is the point of
    --   having one.
    --
    -- Only acceptances are remembered. A rejection is decided fresh every
    -- time, however often the same key arrives with it, and that is
    -- deliberate rather than an omission: this rule does not fire on a
    -- rejection at all (see the requires on decision.accepted below), so a
    -- rejection appends no event, creates no Command and leaves nothing
    -- durable behind. With no side effect to double-apply there is nothing
    -- to protect, and re-deciding is both always safe and more truthful
    -- than replaying a stale "no" at a caller whose command might now
    -- succeed.
    --
    -- A key is scoped to one bounded context and one command type, and to
    -- nothing else. It is not global, and deliberately not scoped to the
    -- calling identity either: two callers presenting the same key for the
    -- same command type are answered as one. Resolved that way for
    -- simplicity - it also makes the two tracks share one record rather
    -- than keep two, which they could not do anyway, since one
    -- authenticates a Role and the other a CommandToken and they have no
    -- identity in common to scope by (see the note above
    -- AuthoriseCommandSubmission). The obligation this puts on a caller is
    -- to choose keys that are unique to an attempt - a fresh UUID per
    -- submission, not a name like "retry-1" another caller could pick too.
    --
    -- Nothing expires. Once recorded, a key's association with its outcome
    -- is permanent, the same treatment every command and event here
    -- already gets: nothing in this library is ever deleted except by
    -- ForgetSubject, which shreds a subject's ciphertext without removing
    -- anything, and by deleting the bounded context itself.
    let prior_outcome = recorded_acceptance(command_type, idempotency_key)
    requires: prior_outcome = null

    -- Both carried here untouched from whichever surface authorised the
    -- command, and both checked separately by the same predicate - see the
    -- correlation note above the rules for what each means, why the check
    -- imposes no charset, and why a caller supplying neither passes.
    requires: valid_correlation_id(correlation_id)
    requires: valid_correlation_id(causation_id)

    let consistency_tags = derive_tags(command_type, payload)
    let boundary = highest_sequence(command_type.bounded_context, consistency_tags)
    let matching_events =
        Events where bounded_context = command_type.bounded_context
            and sequence <= boundary
            and consistency_tags.any(t => t in tags)

    -- decide() is the plugged-in, bounded-context-specific decider (e.g.
    -- "is there enough balance to withdraw"). This library only owns the
    -- read/decide/commit shape, not the business logic itself. boundary
    -- and matching_events are evaluated fresh at commit time (see the
    -- Postgres locking note above the rules), so decide() always sees
    -- complete, current data for its tags - there is no separate staler
    -- state to reconcile against.
    --
    -- Its contract is the whole of CommandDecision: on acceptance, the
    -- events to append; on rejection, both rejection_reason (the
    -- human-readable explanation) and rejection_kind (the machine-readable
    -- label a caller branches on), never one without the other. See the
    -- shape note on CommandDecision for exactly which fields a decider
    -- owes in each case, and why that pairing is an obligation on decide()
    -- rather than something the value type itself can enforce.
    --
    -- Resolved: that plugin boundary stays a prose contract here, not a
    -- formally specified interface. Whether the implementation expresses
    -- decide() as a Rust trait each bounded context implements, as a
    -- closure registered per command type, or as something else again, is
    -- a language and tooling concern this spec deliberately does not reach
    -- into - the same register in which it never says how
    -- protect_sensitive_fields encrypts, or how next_sequence takes its
    -- Postgres lock. Every other black box in this file (project(),
    -- read_projection, render_event, render_command, derive_tags, valid_filters,
    -- valid_tag_mappings, valid_sensitive_fields, valid_payload, next_sequence,
    -- scheduled_payload, next_occurrence_after, generate_token_secret,
    -- tag_owner_scope_satisfied, valid_correlation_id, generate_id) is
    -- documented exactly this way; decide() is not special enough to earn a different treatment.
    --
    -- decide() always observes matching_events exactly as computed above -
    -- complete, fresh, unabridged - and every guarantee here holds
    -- regardless of how that computation happens to be carried out
    -- underneath. For an entity with a long history, computing it from
    -- scratch on every command is one legitimate way; another, behaviourally
    -- identical, way is to combine a periodically-updated, versioned
    -- summary of everything up to some earlier point with only the events
    -- committed since - the same final matching_events either way, just
    -- cheaper once that summary covers a long prefix. This summary, when
    -- one exists, is scoped to a single one of consistency_tags' own keys
    -- (never their union - a command deriving more than one tag simply has
    -- no such summary to draw on, and computes matching_events the first
    -- way instead) and is versioned: a summary computed under a
    -- superseded definition is never trusted as current, treated instead
    -- as if none exists yet. See surface SnapshotInspection for the one
    -- place this summary is itself observable - nowhere else does its
    -- existence, absence or content change what a caller can see.
    let decision = decide(command_type, payload, matching_events)
    requires: decision.accepted = true

    -- The command's own payload is protected on the way into storage,
    -- exactly as an event's is, and under the same per-subject keys - see
    -- the protect_sensitive_fields note above the rules. Deliberately
    -- after decide(): the decider is handed the payload as submitted,
    -- since it needs the plaintext to decide with; only what is stored is
    -- ciphertext.
    let protected = protect_sensitive_fields(command_type, payload)

    -- Every sync projection consuming one of the event types created
    -- below is updated in this same transaction, alongside the command
    -- and its events - see the project() note above the rules.
    --
    -- This is also where a chain is established rather than merely
    -- recorded, and the two halves of that are visible below: the command
    -- itself gets the caller's correlation_id or a generated one, and its
    -- causation_id exactly as supplied; every event it triggers then
    -- inherits that correlation_id - never null by this point, whatever the
    -- caller sent - and names the command as its own immediate cause. See
    -- the correlation note above the rules.
    ensures:
        let command = Command.created(
            id: generate_token_id(),
            bounded_context: command_type.bounded_context,
            command_type: command_type,
            payload: protected.payload,
            metadata: {
                type: command_type.name,
                version: command_type.schema_version,
                client_id: client_id,
                created_at: now,
                correlation_id: correlation_id ?? generate_id(),
                causation_id: causation_id
            },
            encryption_keys: protected.encryption_keys,
            consistency_tags: consistency_tags,
            consistency_boundary: boundary
        )
        for spec in decision.events:
            CommandTriggered.created(
                bounded_context: command_type.bounded_context,
                event_type: spec.event_type,
                payload: protect_sensitive_fields(spec.event_type, spec.payload).payload,
                metadata: {
                    type: spec.event_type.name,
                    version: spec.event_type.schema_version,
                    client_id: client_id,
                    created_at: now,
                    correlation_id: command.metadata.correlation_id,
                    causation_id: command.id
                },
                sequence: next_sequence(command_type.bounded_context),
                tags: derive_tags(spec.event_type, spec.payload),
                encryption_keys: protect_sensitive_fields(spec.event_type, spec.payload).encryption_keys,
                command: command
            )

    @guidance
        -- Performance note, not a change in behaviour: decide() may be
        -- called an extra time in practice. An implementation can read
        -- matching_events and call decide() once optimistically, without
        -- holding the per-bounded-context lock, then take the lock only
        -- to fetch whatever events arrived after boundary (not a full
        -- re-scan) and call decide() again immediately with the
        -- complete set if anything new showed up. A conflict is a
        -- staleness signal, not an automatic rejection - only the final,
        -- fresh decision determines accepted/rejected. Either way the
        -- rule's own requires/ensures above is what must hold at commit.
        --
        -- The in-memory event cache helps here too, since a consistency
        -- boundary typically spans recent events for a tag: matching_events
        -- can often be assembled without a Postgres round trip, and the
        -- under-lock step reads only what arrived after boundary. This
        -- changes nothing about what matching_events contains - a boundary
        -- reaching past the cached window is completed from Postgres.
}

-- The command-side read, and the only one there is: CommandSubmission
-- and CommandTrigger only submit, and a Projection is built from
-- EventTypes alone, so before this nothing anywhere read a stored
-- Command.payload back. Admin-level rather than read-level deliberately.
-- A command payload is one caller's own input as it was submitted, not a
-- curated view of it the way a projection is, so reading other callers'
-- commands is an operator and audit capability rather than part of
-- ordinary reading - which is why this faces AdminAccess, the same actor
-- SubjectErasure and TypeRegistration face.
--
-- It answers two shapes of question with one rule: "which commands of
-- this type were submitted in this window" (command_types, plus the
-- optional after/before bounds on metadata.created_at - both inclusive,
-- and each omittable independently of the other), and "which command
-- produced this event" (triggered_event, the reverse of
-- CommandTriggered.command). triggered_event is a CommandTriggered
-- specifically rather than any Event: a command-triggered event is the
-- only kind that has a command behind it at all, and belonging to some
-- command's triggered_events is exactly what makes it one.
--
-- Every filter narrows and none overrides another. Supplying
-- triggered_event alongside a command_types or time filter its command
-- does not satisfy yields nothing, rather than the command anyway: a
-- caller stating two conditions gets what meets both, and is never
-- handed a result contradicting a filter it supplied itself. That
-- uniform conjunction is also what keeps an empty command_types set
-- meaning "no restriction" rather than "no results" - not a vacuous case
-- but the unrestricted state itself, exactly as
-- AllEventsSubscription.event_types is.
--
-- Like FetchEvents and QueryProjection, this deliberately does not
-- require the bounded context to be active: archiving stops new commands,
-- and reading the ones submitted before that is the point.

rule FetchCommands {
    when: FetchCommands(access_mapping, command_types, after?, before?, triggered_event?, after_command?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin

    -- Every named command type must belong to the same bounded context as
    -- the grant - the same check and the same reasoning as
    -- CreateAllEventsSubscription's event_types.
    requires: command_types.all(ct => ct.bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context)

    -- And the same scoping for the reverse lookup: an event from another
    -- bounded context is not a way into that context's commands.
    requires:
        triggered_event = null
        or triggered_event?.bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context

    -- after_command is where the previous page stopped - the last command
    -- it served - and the same scoping applies to it.
    requires:
        after_command = null
        or after_command?.bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context

    let matched =
        Commands where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context
            and (command_types.count = 0 or command_type in command_types)
            and (after = null or metadata.created_at >= after)
            and (before = null or metadata.created_at <= before)
            and (triggered_event = null or triggered_event in triggered_events)

    -- The ownership check narrows what matched rather than standing beside
    -- it as a requires, which is what makes it a filter - the same shape
    -- QueryEvents uses and for the same reason: this ranges over many
    -- commands, so one the grant does not own is simply not among them and
    -- the fetch itself still succeeds. It is composed here rather than
    -- folded into the narrowing above because the predicate needs the
    -- command bound to ask anything of it, exactly as QueryEvents composes
    -- its own. It is one more narrowing among the four, and overrides none
    -- of them: the reverse lookup gets it too, so naming a triggered_event
    -- whose command the grant does not own yields nothing rather than that
    -- command anyway - the same uniform conjunction stated above. Vacuously
    -- true for a grant that names no scope, which is every grant unless a
    -- superadmin named one, and for any command whose type declares no
    -- owner dimension.
    -- Bounded like the event reads (see config.max_events_per_read): the
    -- first that many in the order they were recorded, continuing after
    -- after_command when given. Recording order, not created_at, since two
    -- commands can share a timestamp; a page boundary between them would
    -- otherwise skip or repeat one.
    let candidates = first_by_recording(
        filter(matched, c =>
            command_owner_scope_satisfied(c, access_mapping.scope)
                and (after_command = null or recorded_after(c, after_command))),
        config.max_events_per_read)

    ensures:
        CommandsDelivered(
            access_mapping: access_mapping,
            commands: map(candidates, c => render_command(c, access_mapping))
        )

    @guidance
        -- render_command(command, access_mapping) is a black box in the
        -- same register as read_projection and render_event, and applies
        -- the identical two-grant test per sensitive field - see
        -- render_event's note above DeliverToSubscriptions for the full
        -- explanation rather than a second copy of it here. In short: a
        -- field decrypts when access_mapping.can_read_sensitive is true,
        -- or when access_mapping.role.external_subject matches that
        -- field's own EncryptionKey.subject_value; every other field is
        -- left as stored ciphertext, never decrypted and then redacted
        -- afterwards. An admin grant on its own is not a sensitive-data
        -- grant here any more than it is on a projection:
        -- can_read_sensitive is orthogonal to level (see
        -- RoleAccessMapping).
        --
        -- It applies that note's third redaction rule too, per entry in
        -- CommandType.private_fields, and on the same terms: a leaf this
        -- caller is not entitled to comes back as null rather than as
        -- ciphertext, because a private field was never encrypted in the
        -- first place. Which callers are entitled - the record's own
        -- creator or addressee, a Role a grant names, a Role carrying a
        -- team field's declared name - is stated there in full and is not
        -- restated here. An admin grant is not one of those entitlements
        -- either: this is the same orthogonality can_read_sensitive has to
        -- level, applied to a mechanism that has no level of its own at
        -- all.
        --
        -- map(candidates, ...) is the same collection-combinator register
        -- as filter(candidates, ...) in FetchEvents: the library owns
        -- which commands are delivered and which of their fields arrive
        -- decrypted, not how a stored command is rendered onto the wire.
        --
        -- command_owner_scope_satisfied(command, scope) is a black box in
        -- the same register as event_owner_scope_satisfied, and is that
        -- predicate's command-side sibling: the identical contract - the
        -- three ways it holds, and the fail-closed treatment of an owner
        -- nothing established - stated once in full on rule QueryEvents'
        -- guidance rather than a third time here. Only the two places it
        -- reads differ. The owner dimension is CommandType.owner_tag_key
        -- rather than EventType.owner_tag_key, validated at registration by
        -- the very same valid_owner_tag_key (see rule RegisterCommandType).
        -- And the owner value is read from Command.consistency_tags rather
        -- than Event.tags.
        --
        -- consistency_tags is the right field to read precisely because it
        -- is derive_tags(command_type, payload) unconditionally, on every
        -- command this library stores (see rule ProcessCommand). It is not
        -- a by-product of using a consistency boundary and does not go
        -- missing when a command uses none - only consistency_boundary
        -- itself is absent in that case. So a command's derived owner value
        -- is exactly as reliably present as an event's, and comparable with
        -- it for the reason every tag on either side is: one derive_tags
        -- produces both (see the derive_tags note above the rules).
        --
        -- Whose scope is checked never varies here, unlike on the event
        -- side where it is the grant's or the token's depending on the
        -- track. It is always the grant's, RoleAccessMapping.scope: there
        -- is no command-read token to be the other half, and none is
        -- intended (see surface CommandQuery's guidance).
}

-- The event-side counterpart to FetchCommands, and the gap it closes is a
-- real one: before these three, no GraphQL caller could read raw
-- historical events at all. EventSubscription pushes and never replays
-- (see its FreshEventsOnly), and FetchEvents pulls but only over REST,
-- for a single EventType, authenticated by a bearer token with no Role
-- behind it. A Role holding an admin grant had no way to look at the
-- stream its own bounded context had accumulated.
--
-- Admin-level rather than read-level, for exactly the reason spelled out
-- above FetchCommands: a raw event is one writer's own record as it was
-- appended, not a curated view of it the way a projection is, so reading
-- other callers' events is an operator and audit capability rather than
-- part of ordinary reading. Read-level callers query projections.
--
-- The three narrow the same population three ways. event_types restricts
-- by type, empty meaning no restriction - the unrestricted state itself
-- rather than a vacuous case, the same convention as
-- AllEventsSubscription.event_types and FetchCommands.command_types, and
-- checked the same way: every named type must belong to the grant's own
-- bounded context. tags matches an event's own derived tags with the
-- ANY-match semantics the consistency boundary in ProcessCommand already
-- uses (see the derive_tags note above the rules), and reuses Tag
-- unchanged: a tag key is shared across event types by design, which is
-- what makes it the right narrowing here where event_types may name
-- several types at once. The per-field Filter mechanism FetchEvents uses
-- cannot do that job - a Filter.field is only unambiguous within one
-- event type's schema, the same reason AllEventsSubscription carries no
-- filters. after_sequence is the caller's own cursor, absent meaning from
-- the beginning, exactly as in FetchEvents.
--
-- Like FetchEvents, QueryProjection and FetchCommands, none of the three
-- requires the bounded context to be active: archiving stops new events,
-- and reading the ones appended before that is the point.
--
-- Served by the same in-memory per-bounded-context event cache FetchEvents
-- reads through (see the note above the rules), so the recent end of
-- history is answered without a Postgres round trip and older events
-- simply more slowly - the cache bounds how fast a read is answered,
-- never how far back it reaches.

rule QueryEvents {
    when: QueryEvents(access_mapping, event_types, tags?, after_sequence?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin

    -- Every named event type must belong to the same bounded context as
    -- the grant - the same check and the same reasoning as
    -- CreateAllEventsSubscription's event_types.
    requires: event_types.all(et => et.bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context)

    -- Bound before the candidate set below so the caller's tag set stays
    -- nameable inside it, where tags is each event's own field.
    let wanted_tags = tags

    let matched =
        Events where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context
            and (event_types.count = 0 or event_type in event_types)
            and (wanted_tags = null or wanted_tags.any(t => t in tags))
            and sequence > (after_sequence ?? -1)

    -- The ownership check narrows what matched rather than standing beside
    -- it as a requires, which is what makes it a filter: this is a browse
    -- over many events, so an event the grant does not own is simply not
    -- among them and the query itself still succeeds. Composed the same
    -- way FetchEvents composes matches_filters, and for the same reason -
    -- the predicate needs the event bound to ask anything of it. Vacuously
    -- true for a grant that names no scope, which is every grant unless a
    -- superadmin named one, and for any event whose type declares no owner
    -- dimension - see the event_owner_scope_satisfied paragraphs in the
    -- guidance below.
    let candidates = first_by_sequence(
        filter(matched, e => event_owner_scope_satisfied(e, access_mapping.scope)),
        config.max_events_per_read)

    ensures:
        EventsQueried(
            access_mapping: access_mapping,
            events: map(candidates, e => render_event(e, access_mapping))
        )

    @guidance
        -- render_event(event, access_mapping) is the same black box
        -- DeliverToSubscriptions pushes through, applying the identical
        -- two-grant test per sensitive field - see the note above that
        -- rule rather than a second copy of it here. In short: a field
        -- decrypts when access_mapping.can_read_sensitive is true, or when
        -- access_mapping.role.external_subject matches that field's own
        -- EncryptionKey.subject_value; every other field arrives as stored
        -- ciphertext, never decrypted and then redacted afterwards. An
        -- admin grant is not a sensitive-data grant here any more than it
        -- is in FetchCommands - can_read_sensitive is orthogonal to level.
        --
        -- The delivery is named apart from FetchEvents' EventsDelivered
        -- because it carries a different thing: a grant and events drawn
        -- from a whole bounded context, rather than a token and events of
        -- the one type it was issued for.
        --
        -- event_owner_scope_satisfied(event, scope) is a black box in the
        -- same register as owner_scope_satisfied (see rule
        -- QueryProjection's guidance), and is the sibling mechanism to it:
        -- the same idea applied per raw event instead of per projection
        -- instance. It is documented here once, because this is the first
        -- rule that reaches for it; QueryProjection's own is the
        -- projection-side half, and neither restates the other. Everywhere
        -- else it appears - CountEvents, InspectEvent, FetchEvents,
        -- ConsumeEvents, DeliverToSubscriptions, and the surface
        -- guarantees over those - refers back to this paragraph rather
        -- than repeating it.
        --
        -- What differs from the projection half is where the owner
        -- dimension is declared and where the owner value is read. A
        -- projection's owner tag key is compiled, deployed configuration
        -- with no registration surface at all; an event type's is
        -- registered state an admin submits and this library validates -
        -- EventType.owner_tag_key, checked against the type's own
        -- tag_mappings at registration (see rule RegisterEventType). And a
        -- projection instance's owner value is folded up over the events
        -- that built it, whereas an event's is simply on the event: it is
        -- one of its own derived Tags, derived from its payload by
        -- derive_tags exactly as every other tag is and never supplied by
        -- the caller reading it (see the derive_tags note above the
        -- rules).
        --
        -- The predicate holds, and the event is in scope, when any of
        -- three things is true: scope is null, so the caller is
        -- unrestricted within whatever it may already read and reads
        -- exactly as it did before scope existed; or
        -- event.event_type.owner_tag_key is null, so the type declares no
        -- owner dimension for a scope to select within; or the event
        -- carries a tag whose key equals event.event_type.owner_tag_key
        -- and whose value equals scope. It does not hold - and the event
        -- is excluded, or the read of it rejected, depending on which
        -- caller asked - when scope is non-null, the type does declare an
        -- owner tag key, and no tag on the event carries that key with a
        -- matching value. That covers three cases and treats them alike: a
        -- tag under that key holding a different value, a tag under that
        -- key holding null (the never-set state a Tag can legitimately be
        -- in - see value Tag), and no tag under that key at all, which is
        -- what an event written before the key was declared holds.
        -- Ownership must be affirmatively provable, not merely
        -- un-contradicted, the same framing owner_scope_satisfied already
        -- uses and for the same reason: an owner nothing established is
        -- the case an attacker reaches for, so it fails closed.
        --
        -- Which caller's scope is checked depends on the track, and the
        -- predicate itself neither knows nor cares. On the GraphQL track
        -- it is the grant's - RoleAccessMapping.scope, here and in
        -- CountEvents, InspectEvent and DeliverToSubscriptions. On the
        -- REST track it is the token's - EventReadToken.scope, in
        -- FetchEvents and ConsumeEvents. Both are opaque caller-chosen
        -- strings compared for equality and never interpreted.
        --
        -- Decided live against the event type's current declaration, never
        -- snapshotted onto an event when it was written: changing or
        -- clearing owner_tag_key at a later registration therefore changes
        -- who may read the type's whole history from that point on, not
        -- merely what is written next (see RegisterEventType's note on why
        -- additive-only does not extend to this field).
}

rule CountEvents {
    when: CountEvents(access_mapping, event_types, tags?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: event_types.all(et => et.bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context)

    let wanted_tags = tags

    -- The same narrowing as QueryEvents minus the cursor: a count is a
    -- live aggregate over everything matching, not a page of it, so a
    -- sequence window would only describe how far a caller happened to
    -- have read. It answers the same question the browse answers, which
    -- is why it takes the same filters rather than an unfiltered total.
    let matched =
        Events where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context
            and (event_types.count = 0 or event_type in event_types)
            and (wanted_tags = null or wanted_tags.any(t => t in tags))

    -- And the same ownership filter, composed the same way, for the same
    -- reason it is a filter there: an event the grant does not own is not
    -- counted, rather than the count being refused. A count is the browse's
    -- own aggregate, so counting what the browse would not show would be
    -- the one place a scoped caller could still infer another owner's
    -- volume. See rule QueryEvents' guidance on
    -- event_owner_scope_satisfied.
    let candidates = filter(matched, e => event_owner_scope_satisfied(e, access_mapping.scope))

    ensures:
        EventsCounted(
            access_mapping: access_mapping,
            count: candidates.count
        )
}

rule InspectEvent {
    when: InspectEvent(access_mapping, event)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = event.bounded_context

    -- The ownership check, and the one place on this surface it rejects
    -- rather than filters. QueryEvents and CountEvents range over many
    -- events, so an event the grant does not own drops out of a result;
    -- this names exactly one, so there is nothing to drop it out of and
    -- the read of it is refused outright - the same shape
    -- QueryProjection's own owner_scope_satisfied requires clause has, for
    -- the same reason. Vacuously true for a grant that names no scope and
    -- for an event whose type declares no owner dimension. See rule
    -- QueryEvents' guidance on event_owner_scope_satisfied, and rule
    -- QueryProjection's on the projection-side sibling.
    requires: event_owner_scope_satisfied(event, access_mapping.scope)

    ensures:
        EventInspected(
            access_mapping: access_mapping,
            event: event,
            rendered_payload: render_event(event, access_mapping)
        )

    @guidance
        -- Single-event detail, and what it adds over picking the same
        -- event out of a QueryEvents result is provenance: when it was
        -- created (metadata.created_at) and how (origin). Each origin
        -- variant already carries its own answer to "how" - the Command
        -- behind a CommandTriggered event, the adapter's source blob and
        -- context on an ExternalTriggered one, the token-derived client_id
        -- on a DirectlyCreated one, and the scheduler itself on a
        -- SystemTriggered one (see SystemTriggeredClientIdIsSystem) - so
        -- nothing new is recorded for this rule to read.
        --
        -- Sensitive fields are rendered under the same two-grant test
        -- QueryEvents applies; see its guidance.
}

-- The ten rules below are the admin bounded context's operations. In
-- principle each should itself be a Command producing a CommandTriggered
-- Event (BoundedContextAdded, BoundedContextArchived, BoundedContextDeleted,
-- EventTypeRegistered, CommandTypeRegistered, TokenCreated, TokenRevoked)
-- within the admin bounded context, the same as any other bounded
-- context's commands - full dogfooding. That wiring depends on the still-open general
-- command-processing mechanism, so for now these are modelled as direct
-- effects, consistent with CreateExternalEvent and CreateDirectEvent
-- above.

-- Creating a bounded context grants nobody access to it. AddBoundedContext
-- writes the BoundedContext and nothing else: no RoleAccessMapping comes
-- with it, not even one for the superadmin who made the call. The only
-- thing that ever brings a RoleAccessMapping into being is an explicit
-- GrantRoleAccessMapping, so a freshly created context is inert the moment
-- it exists. Every per-context surface faces an actor identified by an
-- active RoleAccessMapping - ReadAccess, WriteAccess, AdminAccess - and
-- the AccessTokens the REST track presents are minted only by an
-- admin-level one, so until a grant is made the context has no reader, no
-- writer, no admin and no token. Not even ArchiveBoundedContext reaches
-- it, being admin-gated itself. A superadmin can see it in
-- BoundedContextDirectory and do nothing else with it.
--
-- The startup reconciliation loop is the first thing this stops.
-- RegisterEventType, RegisterCommandType and RegisterProjection each take
-- an admin-level access_mapping scoped to the target context (see the note
-- above them and surface TypeRegistration), so a deploy carrying a
-- brand-new context's types registers none of them. The order is fixed and
-- manual at its first step: AddBoundedContext, then GrantRoleAccessMapping
-- giving some Role admin on that context, and only then the loop.
--
-- That is a consequence of a decision already made rather than an
-- oversight. Auto-granting the creating superadmin admin access as a side
-- effect of creation is exactly the self-service path surface
-- AccessManagement's SuperadminOnly guarantee rules out: a grant is made
-- on a Role's behalf by a superadmin as its own separately audited act,
-- never as a by-product of some other operation. Creating a context and
-- granting access into what was created are two actions because they are
-- two decisions.
--
-- So a bounded context created and never granted to anyone is a valid
-- state, if rarely an intended one, and a permanent one: nothing can ever
-- happen inside it - no type registered, no event, no command, no read -
-- until a superadmin grants some Role access to it. Nothing expires,
-- reminds or repairs it in the meantime.
--
-- Everything above describes this rule, and this rule is now one of two
-- ways a bounded context comes into being. The other is
-- CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate below, which does grant access in the
-- same call - a stated exception, argued where it is made rather than
-- here, and one that leaves this rule exactly as it was. The name checks
-- are the part the two genuinely share: that rule applies the same
-- uniqueness and validity guards to the name it is given, so "a name is
-- chosen once and passes this check when it is" still holds of every
-- bounded context, whichever of the two made it.

rule AddBoundedContext {
    when: AddBoundedContext(caller, name)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true
    requires: not exists BoundedContext{name: name}

    -- A name is not only a label. It is carried through verbatim as an
    -- identifier by the layers underneath this spec, which accept a
    -- restricted character set and a bounded length, so a name that would
    -- not survive that trip is refused here rather than failing somewhere
    -- later and less legibly. valid_bounded_context_name(name) holds when
    -- the name is made only of lowercase letters, digits and underscores,
    -- starts with a letter, and is at most 40 characters long. It is a
    -- black box for the same reason valid_filters and valid_tag_mappings
    -- are: a character-level check is below the level this file works at.
    --
    -- This is the only gate a name ever passes. There is no rename, so a
    -- name is chosen once, at the moment of creation, and every later use
    -- of it - including a reuse after DeleteBoundedContext frees it - comes
    -- back through this same rule and this same check.
    requires: valid_bounded_context_name(name)

    ensures:
        let creator = SuperadminCreator.created(role: caller)
        BoundedContext.created(
            name: name,
            status: active,
            created_at: now,
            created_by: creator
        )
}

-- The read counterpart to AddBoundedContext, and superadmin-gated for the
-- same reason: a bounded context directory is not "for" any one context,
-- so no RoleAccessMapping is the right key to it. It answers the question
-- an admin frontend has before it can ask anything else - which bounded
-- contexts exist, when each appeared and who created it - without relying
-- on GraphQL introspection to discover them.
--
-- It is not one of the ten the note above counts: those are the admin
-- bounded context's mutations, each of which should one day be a Command
-- producing an Event. This one writes nothing, so there is nothing for it
-- to dogfood into - it follows FetchCommands' and QueryProjection's shape
-- rather than the rules around it.
--
-- Unrestricted across contexts and statuses alike: a superadmin already
-- reaches every context by construction, and an archived context is
-- exactly what a directory should keep showing, since its history stays
-- readable (the same reasoning FetchEvents, QueryProjection and
-- FetchCommands apply to their own reads).

rule ListBoundedContexts {
    when: ListBoundedContexts(caller)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true

    ensures:
        BoundedContextsListed(
            caller: caller,
            bounded_contexts: BoundedContexts
        )
}

rule ArchiveBoundedContext {
    when: ArchiveBoundedContext(access_mapping, bounded_context)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = bounded_context
    requires: bounded_context.status = active

    -- The admin context is permanent. The note above the Defaults section
    -- already says nothing removes it - DeleteBoundedContext below guards
    -- against it by name for exactly this reason; this guard is what makes
    -- the same hold for archiving, the only other way a context stops
    -- accepting work. It also keeps admin out of reach of deletion twice
    -- over, since only an archived context can be deleted.
    -- Archiving admin would strand the system: the grants, tokens and
    -- Roles that gate every other context's administration all live in it,
    -- so a superadmin holding an admin-level grant on admin could
    -- otherwise lock everyone, themselves included, out of every context
    -- at once. See invariant AdminContextIsPermanent.
    requires: bounded_context != admin

    ensures:
        bounded_context.status = archived
}

-- The one rule in this file that removes something rather than
-- deactivating it, and deliberately the hardest one to reach.
--
-- Two steps, never one. The context has to be archived already, by a
-- separate earlier call: archiving stops new commands and events while
-- leaving everything recorded still readable, and deleting is the second,
-- deliberate act that ends that too. So no single call turns a live
-- bounded context into nothing, and whoever only meant to stop a context
-- already has what they wanted after step one.
--
-- Superadmin-gated rather than grant-gated, unlike ArchiveBoundedContext
-- directly above, and that difference is the point rather than an
-- oversight. An admin-level RoleAccessMapping authorises everything that
-- happens inside its context, up to and including stopping it - but not
-- ending it. Deleting a context is not an operation within that context;
-- it is an operation on the set of contexts a deployment has, the same
-- kind of thing AddBoundedContext and ListBoundedContexts are, and gated
-- the same way for the same reason.
--
-- Everything scoped to the deleted context ceases to exist with it: its
-- EventTypes and CommandTypes, its Commands and Events, its Projections
-- and ProjectionRebuilds, its Subscriptions and ReadCursors, the
-- AccessTokens minted over its types, the EncryptionKeys its ciphertext
-- was written under, the SuperadminCreator AddBoundedContext minted for
-- it, and every RoleAccessMapping granting a Role access to it. The
-- ensures below says only that the BoundedContext itself no longer
-- exists; the cascade is left to the implementation rather than itemised
-- entity by entity here, the same treatment next_sequence's row lock and
-- the warm event cache already get. What this file does state is the
-- observable end state: afterwards nothing anywhere answers a question
-- about that context.
--
-- One effect does not fit that shape and so is stated outright below: a
-- context deleted while other contexts were templated from it leaves
-- those contexts standing. They are not scoped to it - each is a bounded
-- context in its own right, with its own types, history and grants, all
-- of which became its own at the moment they were registered (see the
-- note above rule ApplyTemplateRegistrations) - so what the deletion
-- takes from them is the link and nothing else. Each becomes an ordinary
-- untemplated context: unchanged in what it holds and what it answers,
-- and with nothing left to resync from. Being templated from is
-- therefore not a claim on the template's continued existence, and no
-- tenant makes its template undeletable. Deletion stays what it already
-- was, an unconditional act depending on nothing outside the context
-- being ended.
--
-- The name comes free again. AddBoundedContext's `not exists
-- BoundedContext{name: name}` guard is true once more, so a later call
-- may take the name back - and what it gets is a new bounded context with
-- its own created_at and created_by, not the old one revived. That is
-- exactly why this is modelled as the context ceasing to exist rather
-- than as a third BoundedContext.status value: a deleted-but-present
-- context would still carry a creation stamp describing nothing, and a
-- reused name would inherit it.
--
-- What does not go away is the fact that any of it happened. Each of the
-- ten admin operations - adding, archiving and now deleting among them -
-- is meant in the end to be a Command against the admin bounded
-- context producing an Event there (see the note above them; none has
-- that wiring yet, and this rule does not start it). Once they do, a
-- context's whole created/archived/deleted history stays permanently
-- readable in admin, which nothing can delete - hence the
-- `bounded_context != admin` guard below and invariant
-- AdminContextIsPermanent. Only the deleted context's own operational
-- data is genuinely gone; the record that it once existed was never its
-- data to take.
--
-- One consequence of requiring archived first is worth saying plainly.
-- ArchiveBoundedContext needs an admin-level grant on its target, so a
-- context created and never granted to anyone - the permanently inert
-- state the note above AddBoundedContext describes - cannot be archived,
-- and therefore cannot be deleted either. Getting rid of a mistake still
-- starts with GrantRoleAccessMapping.

rule DeleteBoundedContext {
    when: DeleteBoundedContext(caller, bounded_context)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true
    requires: bounded_context.status = archived
    requires: bounded_context != admin

    ensures:
        -- Any context templated from this one loses the link and nothing
        -- else, becoming an ordinary untemplated bounded context. Stated
        -- here rather than left to the cascade above because it is the one
        -- effect that lands outside the deleted context: everything else
        -- ceases to exist with it, while these contexts survive and are
        -- merely amended. No new precondition comes with it - a tenant
        -- does not make its template undeletable, and nothing is confirmed
        -- or refused - since this is the data cleanup deletion implies, not
        -- a decision of its own. See the note above.
        for bc in BoundedContexts where template = bounded_context:
            bc.template = null
        not exists bounded_context
}

-- The three rules below are one feature: a bounded context stamped out
-- from another one's declared types, so that a deployment giving every
-- tenant its own bounded context does not register the same
-- EventTypes, CommandTypes and Projections by hand once per tenant. They
-- belong to the same admin-operations group the note above
-- AddBoundedContext describes, and carry the same eventual-dogfooding
-- obligation as the rest of it.
--
-- The bar they are built to is an operational one: adding a tenant is an
-- ops action, not a code change and not a redeploy. Everything a tenant
-- context needs already exists as registered state on some other context,
-- so what is missing is not a new kind of declaration but a way to say
-- "that one, again, over here".
--
-- One level, deliberately. A context named as a template must have no
-- template of its own (see invariant TemplateIsNeverItselfTemplated), so
-- the picture is one edge deep: a template with tenants hanging off it,
-- and nothing hanging off a tenant. What that buys is not tidiness but
-- two questions never having to be answered.
--
-- The first is precedence. Registration rejects an incompatible schema
-- outright rather than merging or preferring one (see
-- schema_is_backwards_compatible in the note below), so a chain in which
-- two levels both declare an event type of the same name under
-- incompatible schemas has no resolution this file could state - the
-- application would succeed or fail depending on which ancestor was
-- walked first, which is not a contract. With one level there is exactly
-- one source for any given name, and "what does this tenant get" is a
-- lookup rather than a walk.
--
-- The second is cycles. Chained templates make A templating B templating
-- A expressible, and ruling it out needs a transitive-closure check -
-- something no invariant in this file can state, since an invariant reads
-- current field values and navigates current relationships and nothing
-- more. Capping the depth at one makes the cycle unconstructible instead
-- of forbidden-and-hopefully-checked, which is the shape this file
-- prefers wherever it can have it.
--
-- The cost is a deployment wanting a hierarchy of templates, which it
-- does not get. That is accepted rather than overlooked: a second level
-- can be added later without breaking anything written here, and nothing
-- below assumes tenants are leaves other than the guard that makes them
-- so.
--
-- Two departures from principles stated elsewhere in this file are
-- deliberate, and both are stated rather than left to be noticed.
--
-- First, CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate creates a context and grants
-- access into it in one call. The note above AddBoundedContext says
-- plainly that those are two acts because they are two decisions, and
-- that auto-granting on creation is the self-service path surface
-- AccessManagement's SuperadminOnly guarantee rules out. That reasoning
-- still holds for AddBoundedContext, which is untouched. It does not hold
-- here, and the difference is which Role gets the grant: nothing is
-- granted to the caller, so this is not a superadmin quietly acquiring
-- access to what it just made. It is a superadmin naming, in one audited
-- act, both the tenant context and the Role that operates it - which is a
-- single decision, not two, and taking two calls to express it is the
-- friction this whole feature exists to remove. The grant is still made
-- on a Role's behalf by a superadmin, still explicitly, still at a level
-- and sensitivity the superadmin chose; it is not made by, for, or as a
-- convenience to whoever will use it.
--
-- Second, the type registrations these rules perform are not gated by an
-- admin-level RoleAccessMapping the way RegisterEventType,
-- RegisterCommandType and RegisterProjection are. They run on the
-- superadmin's own authority, which is the authority that already creates
-- and deletes bounded contexts outright. This is forced rather than
-- chosen: the grant this rule makes is at whatever level the superadmin
-- named, so it is not necessarily an admin one and cannot be what
-- authorises anything; and ResyncBoundedContextFromTemplate names no
-- mapping at all. A grant gate keeps a grant holder inside its own
-- context, which is a bound a superadmin was never under.
--
-- What is not here is as decided as what is. Nothing propagates a
-- template's later changes to its tenants on its own: a template that
-- registers a new event type today reaches its tenants when, and only
-- when, someone calls ResyncBoundedContextFromTemplate for each of them.
-- Automatic propagation would make a deploy against one context an
-- unbounded write across every other, which is exactly the kind of thing
-- RegistrationNeverRebuilds exists to prevent. There is also no
-- tenant-facing path: both rules face a superadmin, and a tenant cannot
-- create itself, resync itself, or decline a resync.

rule CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate {
    when: CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate(caller, template, name, role, level,
        can_read_sensitive, scope?)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true

    -- The same two name guards AddBoundedContext applies, and the same
    -- reasoning: this is the second and only other way a bounded context
    -- name is chosen, so the gate that rule calls "the only gate a name
    -- ever passes" has to be on both ways in or it is on neither.
    requires: not exists BoundedContext{name: name}
    requires: valid_bounded_context_name(name)

    -- The context being stamped from has to exist and still be accepting
    -- work. An archived one accepts no registration of its own, so its
    -- declared types have stopped being a moving statement of what a
    -- tenant should have and became a frozen record of what one once
    -- did - and a tenant stamped out of it would be live from the moment
    -- it was made, out of something that is not.
    requires: template.status = active

    -- One level. See the note above.
    requires: template.template = null

    -- Guards the grant below the same way GrantRoleAccessMapping does.
    -- Its other guard - no active mapping for this pair already - is
    -- vacuous here, since the context this grants on does not exist yet.
    requires: role.status = active

    -- scope is carried through unvalidated, the identical treatment
    -- GrantRoleAccessMapping gives the scope it records: an opaque
    -- caller-chosen string compared for equality at read time and never
    -- interpreted here (see entity RoleAccessMapping, and rule
    -- QueryProjection's guidance on owner_scope_satisfied). Omitted, the
    -- grant below is created unrestricted within the tenant context -
    -- which is what every grant this rule made before this argument
    -- existed is.
    --
    -- It is named here rather than left to a GrantRoleAccessMapping call
    -- afterwards because that call could not do the same thing. This rule
    -- is one of exactly two places a RoleAccessMapping comes into being,
    -- and invariant UniqueActiveAccessPerRoleAndContext refuses a second
    -- active mapping for a pair that already has one - so a later grant
    -- cannot rescope the one made here, and nothing else amends a mapping
    -- in place either. Revoking it and granting again is the only path
    -- that would, and skipping that pair of calls for the ordinary case is
    -- what this argument is for: a templated tenant is exactly the
    -- deployment where an owner-scoped grant is wanted from the first
    -- moment the tenant exists.
    ensures:
        let creator = SuperadminCreator.created(role: caller)
        let tenant = BoundedContext.created(
            name: name,
            status: active,
            created_at: now,
            created_by: creator,
            template: template
        )
        RoleAccessMapping.created(
            role: role,
            bounded_context: tenant,
            level: level,
            can_read_sensitive: can_read_sensitive,
            scope: scope,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
        TemplateApplicationDue(bounded_context: tenant, template: template)
}

-- How a template's later schema evolution reaches a tenant that already
-- exists, and the only way it does. Safe to call as often as anyone
-- likes: what it triggers is a resubmission of registrations, and a
-- registration of an unchanged shape is already a no-op by construction
-- (see the note below rule ApplyTemplateRegistrations and the
-- reconciliation-loop note further down). Calling it on a tenant that is
-- already in step therefore changes nothing at all.
--
-- The tenant has to be active, which the design of this rule inherits
-- rather than invents: every registration it leads to requires an active
-- bounded context, so a resync against an archived tenant would be a call
-- that reports success and applies nothing. Refusing it outright is the
-- same treatment RebuildProjection gives a projection with nothing
-- staged.
--
-- The template has to be active too, for the reason
-- CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate gives. A tenant whose template has
-- been archived is not broken and is not degraded: it keeps serving
-- everything it was already registered with, since what it inherited
-- became its own at the moment it was registered. It simply has nothing
-- new to be told, and says so rather than pretending to look.

rule ResyncBoundedContextFromTemplate {
    when: ResyncBoundedContextFromTemplate(caller, bounded_context)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true
    requires: bounded_context.status = active
    requires: bounded_context.template != null
    requires: bounded_context.template.status = active

    ensures:
        TemplateApplicationDue(
            bounded_context: bounded_context,
            template: bounded_context.template
        )
}

-- What "applying a template" means, defined once and reached from both
-- entry points above rather than written twice. Creation and resync
-- differ in everything around this step - one makes a context and a
-- grant, the other makes nothing - and in this step they are identical,
-- which is the whole reason resync is safe to describe as "the same thing
-- again".
--
-- Each of the three emissions below is a resubmission, not a copy. What
-- it stands for is the template's current registration for that one type,
-- submitted against bounded_context: the same schema, tag mappings,
-- sensitive fields, permissions, consumed event types and sync flag the
-- template declares at this moment, run through the same guards and the
-- same outcomes RegisterEventType, RegisterCommandType and
-- RegisterProjection apply to any other registration. That distinction is
-- load-bearing in four places:
--   - valid_schema, valid_tag_mappings, valid_sensitive_fields,
--     valid_owner_tag_key and the tag/sensitive-field overlap check are
--     re-run against the tenant's own registration rather than assumed
--     from the template's.
--   - schema_version is the tenant's own. A first application registers
--     at version 1 however far the template has evolved, and a later
--     resync advances it only when the schema text actually changed for
--     this tenant.
--   - schema_is_backwards_compatible governs a resync exactly as it
--     governs a deploy. A template whose schema evolved incompatibly
--     cannot push that break into a tenant; the resubmission is rejected,
--     and the tenant carries on with what it has.
--   - a projection is staged, not rebuilt. A resync that changes a
--     tenant's projection definition non-trivially leaves the live
--     projection answering exactly what it answered before and waits for
--     an explicit RebuildProjection, precisely as a deploy does (see
--     RegisterProjection and TypeRegistration's RegistrationNeverRebuilds).
--     A resync never replays a stream.
-- Copying rows would have all four of those wrong, so the file says
-- resubmission and means it. The mechanism by which the resubmission
-- happens is left to the implementation, in the same register as
-- DeleteBoundedContext's cascade: what is stated here is which
-- registrations are owed and what each one is equivalent to.
--
-- Nothing is ever removed by this. A tenant that has an event type the
-- template does not is left holding it: the template names what a tenant
-- must have, never what it may not, and un-registering is not an
-- operation this file has at all. A resync is additive in exactly the way
-- every registration is.
--
-- The order of the three loops is the reconciliation loop's order, and
-- for the reason stated there: event types before command types, so that
-- a CommandType consistency-checking on a tag key becomes submittable
-- only after the EventType declaring that key has landed
-- (ConsistencyTagKeysAreDeclared), and projections last, which
-- RegisterProjection's Set<EventType> argument already forces. An
-- application is the same pass a deploy makes, so it is made in the same
-- order.
--
-- The guards are the ones the entry points already checked, restated here
-- because this rule is reached by a chained trigger and a guard that only
-- exists at the emitting rule is a guard on one path rather than on the
-- act. The template link in particular is checked from the tenant's own
-- field, so no caller and no chain can apply an arbitrary context's
-- registrations to a context that is not its tenant.

rule ApplyTemplateRegistrations {
    when: TemplateApplicationDue(bounded_context, template)
    requires: bounded_context.template = template
    requires: bounded_context.status = active
    requires: template.status = active

    ensures:
        for source in template.event_types:
            TemplateEventTypeApplied(bounded_context: bounded_context, source: source)
        for source in template.command_types:
            TemplateCommandTypeApplied(bounded_context: bounded_context, source: source)
        for source in template.projections:
            TemplateProjectionApplied(bounded_context: bounded_context, source: source)
}

-- RegisterEventType, RegisterCommandType and RegisterProjection below are
-- idempotent upserts, meant to be driven by a startup reconciliation
-- loop: the running instance discovers the EventType/CommandType/
-- Projection definitions compiled into its own binary (derived from the
-- Rust type and projection definitions themselves - a black box here, the
-- same as decide() and protect_sensitive_fields) and registers each one
-- against its bounded context. Re-registering an unchanged shape is a
-- no-op, so the loop can run on every startup, and on every instance:
-- what one instance registers is registered for all of them, so the
-- others' passes find the shape already there and change nothing (see
-- TypeRegistration's RegistrationReachesEveryInstance).
--
-- The loop registers a bounded context's EventTypes before its
-- CommandTypes (and, structurally already guaranteed by RegisterProjection
-- taking consumed_event_types as a Set<EventType> of already-existing
-- entities rather than names, Projections after both). This is what keeps
-- ConsistencyTagKeysAreDeclared (see the invariant) satisfied at the
-- moment a CommandType goes live within one reconciliation pass: any
-- EventType that pass also registers has already landed by the time a
-- CommandType consistency-checking on one of its tag keys becomes
-- submittable. It does not reach across separate deploys - a CommandType
-- shipped now that anticipates an EventType arriving in a later release
-- is still a real gap between the two releases, accepted rather than
-- structurally prevented, since closing that fully would mean rejecting a
-- CommandType registration for referencing a key no EventType declares
-- yet, which forecloses exactly this kind of staged rollout.
--
-- schema_version tracks payload-shape compatibility specifically (see
-- EventType.schema_version), so it only advances when schema itself
-- changed. tag_mappings, sensitive_fields, the origin permissions and a
-- projection's consumed_event_types and sync flag are always reconciled
-- to the given values without a version bump - they don't affect how a
-- stored payload is interpreted.
--
-- valid_tag_mappings(schema, tag_mappings) and
-- valid_sensitive_fields(schema, sensitive_fields) are black boxes in the
-- same register as valid_filters (see the note above rule FetchEvents):
-- this library owns that a mapping naming a field that doesn't exist is
-- rejected outright, rather than silently deriving no tag or protecting
-- nothing, not how a JSON Schema is walked to answer the question. They
-- check field existence and nothing else - valid_tag_mappings that every
-- TagMapping.field is a field schema declares, valid_sensitive_fields the
-- same for every SensitiveField.field and SensitiveField.subject_field.
-- For a two-segment dotted path (see the payload schema shape note above
-- entity CommandType) "exists" means the whole path resolves: the outer
-- field is declared, its declared type is the named nested shape, and
-- that shape declares the named leaf. Same check, one level deeper.
-- There is no FilterOperator-style type-matching concern here: a tag
-- mapping or a sensitive field reads a value, it never compares one, so
-- the field's declared type has no bearing on whether the mapping makes
-- sense.
--
-- Both validate against the schema submitted in the same call, not the
-- registered type's current one, so a registration that changes schema
-- and tag_mappings together is checked against the shape it is moving to
-- rather than the one it is leaving. Unlike filters, these are only ever
-- checked at registration time: tag_mappings and sensitive_fields exist
-- solely as registered state on an EventType or CommandType and are never
-- supplied ad hoc by a caller, so there is no call-time counterpart. A
-- registration whose tag_mappings or sensitive_fields fail the check is
-- rejected outright, exactly as an invalid filter is.
--
-- valid_schema(schema) is the unconditional member of that family, and
-- the reason it is needed is that neither of the two above is. Each is
-- satisfied vacuously by an empty tag_mappings or sensitive_fields, so
-- each only ever reads schema as a side effect of checking something
-- declared against it. A registration declaring neither therefore reached
-- no schema check at all, and could register a string that is not
-- well-formed JSON, let alone a usable JSON Schema. Such a registration
-- was still rejected eventually - by valid_payload, on the first write of
-- that type - but only later, and at a different call from the one that
-- made the mistake.
--
-- valid_schema asks the one question about a schema that has an answer
-- with no payload to hand: schema reads as well-formed JSON, and is
-- itself a valid JSON Schema. It is the same check valid_payload already
-- performs on schema before validating anything against it, minus the
-- payload - so a schema valid_schema accepts is exactly a schema
-- valid_payload can use, and the rejection lands at the registration its
-- author can still see. It says nothing about any payload; conformance
-- remains valid_payload's job. A black box in the same register as the
-- rest of the family: this library owns that an unusable schema is
-- rejected at registration time, not how a JSON Schema document is parsed
-- and compiled.
--
-- RegisterProjection is the third rule that asks it, and the one that
-- needed it most. A projection declares no tag_mappings and no
-- sensitive_fields at all, so it never had even the vacuous pair to read
-- its schema as a side effect - it reached no schema check whatsoever.
-- Nor is there a later call to catch the mistake: a projection's schema
-- is consumed by the generated GraphQL type, not by any payload write, so
-- valid_payload never sees it and the "rejected eventually" consolation
-- above does not apply. An unusable schema registered there would surface
-- as a broken generated type, at no call in particular. Registration is
-- the only place the question can be asked, which is where it now is.
--
-- It is checked before valid_tag_mappings and valid_sensitive_fields on
-- RegisterEventType and RegisterCommandType, because whether a named
-- field exists in a schema is only a meaningful question once the schema
-- is one. On RegisterProjection, which declares neither, it is simply the
-- first schema-related guard the rule reaches.
--
-- schema_is_backwards_compatible(existing_schema, schema) is the third
-- black box of that family, and the one that makes schema evolution a
-- rule rather than an intention. All three registration rules carried
-- "changes must stay backwards compatible" on their schema_version field
-- already; this is what enforces it, uniformly across EventType,
-- CommandType and Projection - enforcing it on some but not others would
-- be arbitrary, since the same sentence was written on all three. It
-- applies only when a registration is updating something: a first-time
-- registration has no prior schema to be compatible with, so the guard is
-- reached only when the type or projection already exists. An
-- incompatible change is rejected outright, exactly as an invalid tag
-- mapping is - there is no "accept it and bump the version harder" path.
--
-- The contract it computes, stated here in prose because computing it is
-- the black box's job and enumerating JSON Schema mechanics is not this
-- spec's:
--   - Every field present in existing_schema is still present in schema.
--     A declared field is permanent.
--   - A field may move from required to optional, but never from optional
--     to required. Loosening is allowed; tightening is not.
--   - A newly added field must be optional, so that payloads written
--     against the previous schema remain valid against this one.
--   - A field's declared type never changes. What was declared an integer
--     stays an integer for that field's whole lifetime, exactly as its
--     name does. No widening, no narrowing, no lateral change.
-- In short: fields may be added or loosened, never removed, tightened or
-- retyped. Phasing a field out of use is done by declaring it optional
-- and simply ceasing to populate it - the declaration itself stays
-- forever, and readers of old payloads keep finding what they were
-- written with.
--
-- All four bullets apply to the outer, top-level field only. A payload
-- schema may declare a field whose type is a nested object shape (one
-- level, reuse only - see the payload schema shape note above entity
-- CommandType), and that shape's own internal evolution - fields added,
-- removed or retyped inside it - is not tracked, validated or enforced
-- here at all. It is entirely the bounded context's own responsibility,
-- in the same "not something this spec enforces" register the top-level
-- JSON Schema type matrix is already left in. Nesting introduces no new
-- failure mode: if a nested shape's definition changes at all, the schema
-- text of every event or command type embedding it differs, so
-- schema_changed is true and schema_version bumps for each of them -
-- exactly the mechanism that already fires when any scalar field changes.
-- That is expected, ordinary versioning behaviour, not a nested-specific
-- complication. The one part of a nested shape that does get pinned is
-- pinned by a different mechanism, not by these four bullets: a leaf some
-- TagMapping, SensitiveField or Filter actually addresses - see the
-- extension below.
--
-- The type bullet is deliberately the blunt answer rather than the
-- permissive one. "Widening is allowed" would be defensible in a world
-- with a canonical subtype order - Avro and Protobuf both have one - but
-- JSON Schema does not, so the widening matrix itself would have to be
-- delegated to the black box, and that would be a genuinely fuzzier
-- black box than the FilterOperator-to-field-type matrix this one
-- already resembles. Immutability is also just what the first bullet
-- already says about a field's name, applied to what the field is: a
-- declared field is permanent, and so is what it was declared as. A type
-- that needs a differently typed value declares a new optional field for
-- it and leaves the old one standing.
--
-- That is also what resolves what used to be an open question about
-- retiring a tagged field. The worry was a dead end: valid_tag_mappings
-- requires every TagMapping.field to exist in the schema being
-- registered, and the additive-only key guard below requires every
-- previously declared key to survive re-registration, so a schema
-- revision dropping the payload field a live key reads from could satisfy
-- neither. With this check in place that revision is simply not a legal
-- registration: a field a TagMapping points at cannot be removed by any
-- compliant schema evolution, so the mapping can never lose its target
-- and the dead end cannot be reached. A tagged field that has fallen out
-- of use goes optional and stops being populated, which derive_tags
-- already has a defined answer for - Tag(key, value: null), see its note
-- above the rules. This deliberately rests on an enforced check rather
-- than on an honour system: the reason no path is specified for retiring
-- a key is that nothing can create the situation that would need one.
--
-- That protection extends to a leaf reached by a two-segment dotted path,
-- symmetrically and with no new machinery - it is the same existence
-- check, resolved one level deeper. If a TagMapping.field is
-- address.country, valid_tag_mappings is satisfied only when the whole
-- path resolves in the schema being registered, so country must still be
-- declared inside Address, not merely address still declared at the outer
-- level. A schema revision that keeps address but drops country from
-- Address is therefore not a legal registration for that type, exactly as
-- dropping a tagged top-level field is not. The same holds wherever the
-- equivalent existence check applies: valid_sensitive_fields for
-- SensitiveField.field and subject_field, and valid_filters for
-- Filter.field at subscription registration and fetch time.
--
-- This is the one thing that pins any part of a nested shape's interior,
-- and it pins exactly as much as is being used: an addressed leaf, and
-- nothing else in the shape. The bullets above still govern the top level
-- only, so the rest of a nested shape's internal evolution stays the
-- bounded context's own affair. What cannot happen is a live tag,
-- sensitive field or filter silently losing the leaf it reads, which is
-- the property that mattered at the top level and matters identically one
-- level in.
--
-- The same contract settles a second question, this one about the past
-- rather than the future: why no historical schema text is kept
-- anywhere. Only the current schema and a current schema_version exist
-- per type; the text that was in force at version 3 is simply gone once
-- version 4 is registered. That is sufficient, and it is sufficient
-- because of the shape above rather than by luck. The four bullets
-- together mean every field any prior version ever declared is still
-- declared now, at a requiredness no tighter than it then had and at the
-- same type it then had. So a type's current schema is a superset of
-- every schema that type has ever had: a payload written under any
-- earlier version is still valid against the current one, and every
-- field it carries is still described there, correctly typed. A consumer
-- reading an old event never needs the historical text, because the
-- single schema it can already reach describes what it is holding,
-- completely and correctly.
--
-- That is what Metadata.version is for, and what it is not for. It
-- records the schema_version a message was written under - audit and
-- provenance, "this was produced against v3" - not a key a reader
-- resolves to something before it can interpret the payload. Nothing in
-- this spec reads it back to validate against, and nothing needs to.
--
-- That argument needs all three of presence, requiredness and type to be
-- pinned down, and all three are: presence and requiredness by the first
-- three bullets, type by the fourth. Nothing a legal registration can do
-- leaves a field of an old payload described by a type it was not
-- written against, so the superset holds outright rather than only for
-- the parts of a payload whose types happen not to have moved.
--
-- One further constraint sits alongside those two, but deliberately not
-- inside either black box: it compares two sets the registration already
-- carries and needs no schema knowledge at all, so it is stated directly.
-- A registration is rejected when any TagMapping.field names the same
-- payload field as a SensitiveField.field on that same type. derive_tags
-- reads the plaintext payload before protect_sensitive_fields encrypts
-- it, so such a mapping would smuggle the very value being encrypted into
-- Event.tags / Command.consistency_tags - stored separately, indexed, and
-- never touched by ForgetSubject. The tag copy would survive
-- crypto-shredding even though the payload ciphertext itself became
-- permanently unreadable. Forbidding the overlap closes that at
-- registration time, structurally, rather than leaving it as a documented
-- risk.
--
-- The constraint is on SensitiveField.field alone - the field that
-- actually gets encrypted (e.g. "ssn", "email"). It says nothing about
-- SensitiveField.subject_field, which is a different field (e.g.
-- "user_id"): the plaintext identifier the EncryptionKey is looked up or
-- derived by, never encrypted in the first place precisely because it has
-- to stay readable to find the key. Tagging on a subject_field is
-- unaffected and fully allowed - it is the normal way to build a
-- per-subject consistency boundary, e.g. mapping account_id to the key
-- "account" while also naming account_id as the subject_field for some
-- encrypted field. This is not a blanket "don't tag identifying fields"
-- rule; only overlap with SensitiveField.field is forbidden.
--
-- Two more overlap constraints stand alongside that one, arriving with
-- private_fields and resting on the very same argument rather than a new
-- one. A registration is rejected when any PrivateField.field names the
-- same payload field as a TagMapping.field on that same type, and
-- rejected again when it names the same payload field as a
-- SensitiveField.field. The tag half is the identical leak rather than a
-- comparable one: derive_tags reads the plaintext payload, and Event.tags
-- / Command.consistency_tags carry no per-field redaction at all, so a
-- field that was both tagged and private would hand its plaintext value
-- to every reader through the tag copy while the payload leaf beside it
-- was dutifully being nulled out. The sensitive half closes the other
-- side of the same shape: a field already stored as ciphertext has
-- nothing left for a plaintext redaction rule to protect, and declaring
-- both would only make it ambiguous which of two different mechanisms a
-- given reader's leaf came back from - one that leaves ciphertext
-- standing, one that replaces the leaf with null. So a payload field is
-- at most one of the three: tagged, sensitive or private.
--
-- Each constraint is on PrivateField.field alone, exactly as the original
-- is on SensitiveField.field alone. PrivateField.addressee_field is a
-- different field and is unconstrained, for the identical reason
-- subject_field is: it is the plaintext identifier the visibility test is
-- resolved by, so it has to stay readable to be compared at all, and
-- tagging on it - or marking it sensitive - is a separate and perfectly
-- ordinary choice.
--
-- valid_private_fields(schema, private_fields) is the fourth member of
-- the valid_schema / valid_tag_mappings / valid_sensitive_fields family,
-- and a black box in exactly that register. It asks the same existence
-- question those two ask - every PrivateField.field, and every
-- PrivateField.addressee_field where the kind declares one, must resolve
-- in the schema being registered, with a two-segment dotted path
-- resolving whole one level deeper the same way SensitiveField.field and
-- subject_field do - and it asks one further question they have no
-- counterpart for, because PrivateField is the first of these value types
-- whose own fields are conditional on a kind. That further question needs
-- no schema knowledge at all, only the declaration's own shape: team is
-- set and addressee_field is null exactly when kind = team,
-- addressee_field is set and team is null exactly when kind = addressed,
-- and both are null when kind = own. Anything else is rejected outright,
-- the same as an invalid tag mapping is, rather than registered as a rule
-- no reader could evaluate - a team field naming no team, or an addressed
-- field naming no addressee field, is one nobody could ever be entitled
-- to, so it would silently redact itself from everybody forever.
--
-- private_fields is reconciled across re-registrations exactly the way
-- sensitive_fields is, and deliberately so rather than by inheriting the
-- shape by accident: assigned to whatever the call carries, with no
-- additive-only guard over it and no schema_version bump. The reason is
-- the identical one. Additive-only exists to protect what history already
-- carries - tag keys accumulate because stored events and commands
-- already carry tags under them and an invariant checks those against the
-- type's current mappings - and neither sensitive_fields nor
-- private_fields has any such stored counterpart to strand. Where the two
-- do differ is which way a change reaches, and that is a consequence of
-- when each mechanism runs rather than of how either is reconciled: a
-- sensitive_fields change affects writes from that point forward only,
-- because encryption happens at write time and stored payloads are never
-- rewritten, while a private_fields change affects every read from that
-- point forward across the type's whole history, because redaction
-- happens at read time. That is the same live-declaration behaviour
-- owner_tag_key already has, argued in full on EventType.private_fields
-- and not restated in the rule below.
--
-- All three are gated by an admin-level RoleAccessMapping for the target
-- bounded context, the same as the rules above. A RoleAccessMapping scoped
-- to the admin bounded context itself deliberately gains no new meaning
-- here: a grant on the admin context must not become a way to register
-- types into arbitrary other contexts.
--
-- Registration is also where the two REST opt-ins are set:
-- EventType.event_read_allowed and CommandType.rest_trigger_allowed. Both
-- are reconciled the same way as the origin permissions - a type is
-- reachable over REST only because its own registration says so, never
-- because a token happens to exist for it.
--
-- Scheduling is the one opt-in that is not a single flag, and it is opted
-- into whole or not at all. A registration saying system_triggered_allowed
-- = true must also say when the type fires and what a missed occurrence
-- means; one that omits either is rejected here rather than completed with
-- a value this library picked. That is deliberate and is the answer to what
-- used to be the last open question in this file: both missed-occurrence
-- answers can lose or duplicate real work, so choosing between them is the
-- bounded context author's call, made once, in the same reconciliation loop
-- that declares everything else about the type (see enum
-- MissedOccurrencePolicy and the note above rule CreateSystemEvent).
-- Symmetrically, a registration that does not opt in is never asked for
-- either value - they have no meaning for a type nothing schedules, and
-- whatever it last supplied is simply reconciled onto the type like every
-- other declared field.
--
-- Opting in also anchors the schedule position, and only opting in does.
-- EventType.schedule_position starts at the moment scheduling is first
-- switched on, so a backlog can only ever reach back to that moment rather
-- than to the beginning of time - without which registering a per-minute
-- schedule under replay_backlog would owe an unbounded replay before its
-- first real occurrence. Re-registration deliberately leaves the position
-- alone: it is the one piece of an EventType's state a deploy must not
-- reset, since resetting it forward would skip an outage's backlog and
-- resetting it back would re-fire history, and a reconciliation loop
-- submitting the same shape on every deploy would do whichever of those it
-- was written to do, every time. Switching scheduling off and on again
-- re-anchors, because that is the same act as opting in for the first time
-- - the off period is not a backlog anybody is owed. last_fired_at is
-- never reset by any of this: what once fired stays a fact, whether or not
-- the type still fires.
--
-- Changing the cron expression on a scheduled type needs nothing extra, and
-- that is a property of what the position is rather than luck. It is an
-- instant, not an occurrence index, so "everything up to here is accounted
-- for" survives the schedule that produced those occurrences being
-- replaced: the new expression's occurrences after that instant are exactly
-- the ones still owed.

rule RegisterEventType {
    when: RegisterEventType(access_mapping, bounded_context, name, schema, tag_mappings,
        sensitive_fields, private_fields, external_creation_allowed, direct_creation_allowed,
        system_triggered_allowed, event_read_allowed, system_triggered_schedule?,
        missed_occurrence_policy?, owner_tag_key?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = bounded_context
    requires: bounded_context.status = active
    requires: valid_schema(schema)
    requires: valid_tag_mappings(schema, tag_mappings)
    requires: valid_sensitive_fields(schema, sensitive_fields)

    -- Every named field must exist in the schema being registered, and
    -- each entry's kind-conditional fields must be the ones its kind
    -- calls for - see the valid_private_fields paragraph in the note
    -- above. Required rather than optional as an argument, exactly as
    -- sensitive_fields is: a type declaring no private fields passes an
    -- empty set and is checked vacuously, rather than leaving "declared
    -- nothing" and "said nothing" as two different registrations.
    requires: valid_private_fields(schema, private_fields)

    -- valid_owner_tag_key(tag_mappings, owner_tag_key) holds when
    -- owner_tag_key is null - the type declares no owner dimension, which
    -- is every registration that omits it - or when it names a key one of
    -- the tag_mappings submitted in this same call actually produces. A
    -- registration naming a key nothing derives is rejected outright,
    -- exactly as an invalid tag mapping is, rather than registering an
    -- owner dimension no event can ever satisfy: an owner that cannot be
    -- established is one no scoped caller can ever read past (see rule
    -- QueryEvents' guidance on event_owner_scope_satisfied), so the
    -- mistake is caught at the registration its author can still see. It
    -- is checked against the mappings in this call rather than the
    -- registered type's current ones, the same way valid_tag_mappings and
    -- valid_sensitive_fields are checked against the schema in this call.
    requires: valid_owner_tag_key(tag_mappings, owner_tag_key)

    -- No TagMapping may read a field sensitive_fields encrypts: the tag
    -- copy is plaintext and would outlive crypto-shredding. Only
    -- SensitiveField.field is off limits, never subject_field - see the
    -- note above.
    requires: not tag_mappings.any(m => sensitive_fields.any(s => s.field = m.field))

    -- And no TagMapping may read a field private_fields redacts, for the
    -- identical reason: the tag copy carries no per-field redaction, so
    -- the plaintext would reach every reader through Event.tags while the
    -- payload leaf was being nulled out beside it.
    requires: not tag_mappings.any(m => private_fields.any(p => p.field = m.field))

    -- And a field is at most one of the three: what is already stored as
    -- ciphertext is not also redacted from plaintext. Only
    -- PrivateField.field is off limits here, never addressee_field - see
    -- the note above.
    requires: not sensitive_fields.any(s => private_fields.any(p => p.field = s.field))

    -- Scheduling is opted into whole: a type that says it fires on a
    -- schedule has to say when, and what a missed occurrence means. No
    -- default, so a registration missing either is rejected rather than
    -- completed on its author's behalf - see the note above.
    requires:
        system_triggered_allowed = true implies
            (system_triggered_schedule != null and missed_occurrence_policy != null)

    let existing = EventType{bounded_context: bounded_context, name: name}

    -- The one act that anchors the schedule position: opting in, whether
    -- for a brand new type or for one that had scheduling switched off.
    -- Every other registration leaves the position where it is.
    let scheduling_newly_enabled =
        system_triggered_allowed = true
        and (not exists existing or existing.system_triggered_allowed = false)

    -- Schema evolution is additive and loosening only, and a first
    -- registration has nothing to be compatible with - see the note above.
    requires:
        not exists existing
        or schema_is_backwards_compatible(existing.schema, schema)

    -- Tag mapping keys are additive-only on re-registration: events
    -- already carry tags under the keys declared so far, and
    -- EventTagsMatchDeclaredMappings checks a tag's key against the
    -- EventType's current mappings. Dropping a key would strand that
    -- history, so registration is rejected outright rather than silently
    -- losing it. The field a key reads from may change freely.
    --
    -- Additive-only deliberately does not extend to owner_tag_key. It is
    -- a pointer into tag_mappings rather than a member of it, re-validated
    -- fresh on every registration by valid_owner_tag_key above rather than
    -- accumulated across them, so a later registration may freely change
    -- which key it names or clear it back to null - as long as whatever it
    -- names is still a key those mappings produce. Nothing is stranded by
    -- that: the tags themselves are what history carries, and the keys
    -- carrying them stay additive-only regardless of which one is
    -- currently pointed at as the owner. What changing it does change is
    -- who may read the type's events from that point on, immediately and
    -- for its whole history alike, since ownership is decided live at read
    -- time against the type's current declaration and never snapshotted
    -- onto an event (see rule QueryEvents' guidance on
    -- event_owner_scope_satisfied).
    requires:
        not exists existing
        or existing.tag_mappings.all(m => tag_mappings.any(n => n.key = m.key))

    let schema_changed = exists existing and existing.schema != schema
    let initial_schedule_position = if scheduling_newly_enabled: now else: null

    ensures:
        if exists existing:
            existing.schema = schema
            existing.schema_version =
                if schema_changed: existing.schema_version + 1 else: existing.schema_version
            existing.tag_mappings = tag_mappings
            existing.owner_tag_key = owner_tag_key
            existing.sensitive_fields = sensitive_fields
            existing.private_fields = private_fields
            existing.external_creation_allowed = external_creation_allowed
            existing.direct_creation_allowed = direct_creation_allowed
            existing.system_triggered_allowed = system_triggered_allowed
            existing.system_triggered_schedule = system_triggered_schedule
            existing.missed_occurrence_policy = missed_occurrence_policy
            existing.schedule_position =
                if scheduling_newly_enabled: now else: existing.schedule_position
            existing.event_read_allowed = event_read_allowed
        else:
            EventType.created(
                bounded_context: bounded_context,
                name: name,
                schema: schema,
                schema_version: 1,
                tag_mappings: tag_mappings,
                owner_tag_key: owner_tag_key,
                sensitive_fields: sensitive_fields,
                private_fields: private_fields,
                external_creation_allowed: external_creation_allowed,
                direct_creation_allowed: direct_creation_allowed,
                system_triggered_allowed: system_triggered_allowed,
                system_triggered_schedule: system_triggered_schedule,
                missed_occurrence_policy: missed_occurrence_policy,
                schedule_position: initial_schedule_position,
                last_fired_at: null,
                event_read_allowed: event_read_allowed
            )
}

rule RegisterCommandType {
    when: RegisterCommandType(access_mapping, bounded_context, name, schema, tag_mappings,
        sensitive_fields, private_fields, rest_trigger_allowed, owner_tag_key?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = bounded_context
    requires: bounded_context.status = active
    requires: valid_schema(schema)
    requires: valid_tag_mappings(schema, tag_mappings)
    requires: valid_sensitive_fields(schema, sensitive_fields)

    -- The very same valid_private_fields RegisterEventType applies,
    -- reused unchanged rather than mirrored: it asks a question about a
    -- schema and a Set<PrivateField>, and a CommandType declares the
    -- identical shape an EventType does - the same reason
    -- valid_sensitive_fields and valid_owner_tag_key are shared. Its
    -- contract is stated once above rule RegisterEventType and is not
    -- restated here. Required rather than optional for the same reason
    -- sensitive_fields is.
    requires: valid_private_fields(schema, private_fields)

    -- The very same valid_owner_tag_key RegisterEventType applies, reused
    -- unchanged rather than mirrored: it asks a question about a
    -- Set<TagMapping> and a key, and a CommandType's mappings are the
    -- identical shape an EventType's are - the same reason derive_tags
    -- takes both types interchangeably. So the contract is the one stated
    -- above rule RegisterEventType, and it is not restated here. What it
    -- rejects is a registration naming a key nothing in this same call
    -- derives, rather than registering an owner dimension no command can
    -- ever satisfy: an owner that cannot be established is one no scoped
    -- caller can ever read past (see rule FetchCommands' guidance on
    -- command_owner_scope_satisfied).
    requires: valid_owner_tag_key(tag_mappings, owner_tag_key)

    -- Same overlap constraint as RegisterEventType, for the same reason: a
    -- command's consistency_tags are derived from its plaintext payload
    -- and are not reached by ForgetSubject either.
    requires: not tag_mappings.any(m => sensitive_fields.any(s => s.field = m.field))

    -- And the same two private_fields overlap constraints, on the same
    -- terms: Command.consistency_tags carry no per-field redaction any
    -- more than Event.tags do, and a field already stored as ciphertext
    -- has nothing for a plaintext redaction rule to protect. See the note
    -- above rule RegisterEventType rather than a second copy here.
    requires: not tag_mappings.any(m => private_fields.any(p => p.field = m.field))
    requires: not sensitive_fields.any(s => private_fields.any(p => p.field = s.field))

    let existing = CommandType{bounded_context: bounded_context, name: name}

    -- The same backwards-compatibility guard as RegisterEventType, for the
    -- same reason: a command payload schema evolves additively too.
    requires:
        not exists existing
        or schema_is_backwards_compatible(existing.schema, schema)

    -- Additive-only for the same reason as RegisterEventType, protecting
    -- CommandConsistencyTagsMatchDeclaredMappings: commands already
    -- recorded a consistency_tags set drawn from the keys declared so far.
    --
    -- And, exactly as there, additive-only deliberately does not extend to
    -- owner_tag_key. It is a pointer into tag_mappings rather than a
    -- member of it, re-validated fresh on every registration by
    -- valid_owner_tag_key above rather than accumulated across them, so a
    -- later registration may freely change which key it names or clear it
    -- back to null - as long as whatever it names is still a key those
    -- mappings produce. Nothing is stranded by that: the consistency_tags
    -- themselves are what history carries, and the keys carrying them stay
    -- additive-only regardless of which one is currently pointed at as the
    -- owner. What changing it does change is who may read the type's
    -- commands from that point on, immediately and for its whole history
    -- alike, since ownership is decided live at read time against the
    -- type's current declaration and never snapshotted onto a command (see
    -- rule FetchCommands' guidance on command_owner_scope_satisfied).
    requires:
        not exists existing
        or existing.tag_mappings.all(m => tag_mappings.any(n => n.key = m.key))

    let schema_changed = exists existing and existing.schema != schema

    ensures:
        if exists existing:
            existing.schema = schema
            existing.schema_version =
                if schema_changed: existing.schema_version + 1 else: existing.schema_version
            existing.tag_mappings = tag_mappings
            existing.owner_tag_key = owner_tag_key
            existing.sensitive_fields = sensitive_fields
            existing.private_fields = private_fields
            existing.rest_trigger_allowed = rest_trigger_allowed
        else:
            CommandType.created(
                bounded_context: bounded_context,
                name: name,
                schema: schema,
                schema_version: 1,
                tag_mappings: tag_mappings,
                owner_tag_key: owner_tag_key,
                sensitive_fields: sensitive_fields,
                private_fields: private_fields,
                rest_trigger_allowed: rest_trigger_allowed
            )
}

-- Re-registering a projection that already holds state, resolved. The
-- reconciliation loop above runs on every deploy, so RegisterProjection
-- must never be the thing that starts a replay: a projection is rebuilt
-- only when an admin explicitly asks for it (RebuildProjection below).
-- What a registration does instead depends on whether the incoming change
-- can honestly be applied to content that was already folded from the
-- stream.
--
-- Trivial changes are applied in place, to the live Projection, exactly as
-- they always were:
--   - sync flipping true -> false. A sync projection sits at its bounded
--     context's latest committed sequence by construction, so an async one
--     carrying on from there has missed nothing.
--   - adding a consumed EventType that has no committed events in this
--     bounded context yet. Nothing was missed by not having consumed it,
--     so there is nothing to replay for it - and unlike the schema case
--     this is a question the stream itself can answer, so it is checked
--     rather than assumed.
--
-- Every other change is staged as a ProjectionRebuild and touches the live
-- Projection not at all: not its schema, not its schema_version, not its
-- consumed_event_types, not its sync flag, not its caught_up_to. It keeps
-- answering exactly what it answered before, under its current definition,
-- for as long as nobody triggers the rebuild:
--   - schema changed. What the projection already holds was folded under
--     the previous definition; only a replay produces the new shape.
--   - a consumed EventType added or dropped where that type does have
--     committed events. Both directions are treated alike: an added type
--     has history the projection never folded, a dropped type has history
--     it did fold and must now forget. This supersedes the earlier reading
--     that a drop is structurally harmless - harmless to the invariants is
--     a different claim from leaving a correct projection behind.
--   - sync flipping false -> true. This is the case that had no safe
--     answer before: an async projection may be lagging at that moment, so
--     declaring it sync in place would leave it stale-but-declared-sync
--     until it caught up. Staging removes that window rather than naming
--     it - the live projection stays async at its own position, and only
--     becomes sync at promotion, by which point the rebuilt content is at
--     the latest committed sequence already. SyncProjectionNeverLags needs
--     no exemption for the flip and gets none.
--
-- Staging is idempotent the same way registration is: a non-trivial change
-- with a pending rebuild already staged overwrites that pending row rather
-- than accumulating rows, so redeploying the same binary stages the same
-- definition once. A non-trivial change arriving while a rebuild is
-- already building leaves that build strictly alone - it runs to
-- completion and promotion under the definition it was triggered with -
-- and lands in a new pending row alongside it, which needs its own
-- RebuildProjection call once the current one finishes. At most one
-- pending and one building rebuild coexist per projection (see
-- UniqueRebuildPerProjectionAndStatus).
--
-- Promotion is automatic, and is the only way a rebuild ends in a changed
-- projection - the one other way it ends, an admin discarding it while it
-- is still pending, changes nothing a reader sees at all (see the note
-- above DiscardProjectionRebuild). It is a background-process concern in
-- the same register as the async project() consumer (see the note above
-- the rules), not an admin action of its own: once
-- a building rebuild's caught_up_to reaches its bounded context's latest
-- committed sequence, its schema, schema_version, consumed_event_types,
-- sync and caught_up_to replace the live Projection's, the content that
-- projection held under the old definition is discarded, and the
-- ProjectionRebuild row ceases to exist. QueryProjection is unaffected
-- throughout: it reads "the live Projection", which quietly becomes the
-- new one at promotion. No new rejection kind, no wire-contract change for
-- readers, and no window in which a caller is answered from a half-built
-- projection - while a rebuild is building, the projection answers from
-- its existing content, at its existing position, under its existing
-- schema. A rebuild is a second, private fold that nothing reads until it
-- is complete, not a projection taken offline in view of its callers.
--
-- A first-time registration stages nothing and rebuilds nothing, but it is
-- not unconditionally blank either. Registering a sync projection into a
-- bounded context that already holds events of a type it consumes folds
-- that history in there and then, before the registration is complete, and
-- the new Projection is created already caught up to its context's latest
-- committed sequence. The asymmetry with an async projection is real
-- rather than incidental: an async one is folded forward by a background
-- consumer that reads a null caught_up_to as "start from the beginning",
-- so pre-existing history reaches it on the first tick no matter when it
-- was registered, whereas a sync projection is only ever updated by the
-- transaction committing an event it consumes and so has no mechanism at
-- all for events that predate it. Doing this inline needs none of the
-- staging machinery above, and for a reason that machinery makes explicit:
-- staging exists to keep a live projection answering under its old
-- definition while a replay runs, and a projection nobody has been able to
-- query yet has no such reader to protect.

rule RegisterProjection {
    when: RegisterProjection(access_mapping, bounded_context, name, schema,
        consumed_event_types, sync)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = bounded_context
    requires: bounded_context.status = active
    requires: valid_schema(schema)

    -- Every EventType a projection consumes must belong to the same
    -- bounded context: a projection is built from its own context's
    -- stream, and the async consumer and the inline sync path both walk
    -- exactly that stream.
    requires: consumed_event_types.all(et => et.bounded_context = bounded_context)

    let existing = Projection{bounded_context: bounded_context, name: name}

    -- The same backwards-compatibility guard as the two type registrations,
    -- for the same reason: Projection.schema_version carries the identical
    -- "changes must stay backwards compatible" contract, and a client
    -- consuming the generated GraphQL type is exactly the reader that
    -- contract protects.
    requires:
        not exists existing
        or schema_is_backwards_compatible(existing.schema, schema)

    -- Still no additive-only constraint on consumed_event_types, unlike
    -- RegisterEventType's tag_mappings. Tag mapping keys are additive
    -- because stored events already carry tags under them and
    -- EventTagsMatchDeclaredMappings checks that history against the
    -- current mappings. A projection's built content isn't modelled here,
    -- so there is no equivalent invariant to strand and a drop is
    -- accepted rather than rejected - it is staged for a rebuild instead
    -- (below), which is what makes accepting it safe.
    let schema_changed = exists existing and existing.schema != schema

    -- The consumed types this registration adds or drops, in either
    -- direction, and whether the stream holds anything for them. An added
    -- or dropped type with no committed events changes nothing about what
    -- the projection would have folded, so it needs no replay; one with
    -- history does, whichever direction it moved in - see the note above.
    let previously_consumed = if exists existing: existing.consumed_event_types else: {}
    let changed_event_types =
        (consumed_event_types - previously_consumed)
            + (previously_consumed - consumed_event_types)
    let consumed_change_has_history =
        changed_event_types.any(et =>
            Events.any(e => e.bounded_context = bounded_context and e.event_type = et))

    let becoming_sync = exists existing and existing.sync = false and sync = true

    -- Every event this bounded context has ever committed, named once
    -- because the first-time case below needs both whether it holds
    -- anything relevant and where it currently ends. Expressed as a
    -- filter() rather than "Events where bounded_context = bounded_context"
    -- only because the trigger parameter shares the field's name; it means
    -- exactly what that projection would have meant.
    let context_events = filter(Events, e => e.bounded_context = bounded_context)

    -- A first-time registration is not always a blank one. An async
    -- projection created into a context that already has history needs no
    -- special treatment: caught_up_to = null means "start from the
    -- beginning" to the background consumer, which folds that history in
    -- on its very first tick regardless of when the projection was
    -- registered. A sync projection has no such consumer - it is only ever
    -- updated inline, by the transaction that commits an event it consumes
    -- - so nothing would ever go back for events committed before it
    -- existed, and it would sit at null until some new event of a consumed
    -- type happened to arrive. This names that case; the ensures below is
    -- what closes it.
    let first_time_sync_has_history =
        not exists existing
        and sync
        and consumed_event_types.any(et => context_events.any(e => e.event_type = et))

    let rebuild_needed =
        exists existing
        and (schema_changed or consumed_change_has_history or becoming_sync)

    -- At most one pending rebuild exists per projection
    -- (UniqueRebuildPerProjectionAndStatus), so this is the row to
    -- restage when one is already waiting. A building rebuild is
    -- deliberately not looked up: it is never touched from here.
    let staged = ProjectionRebuild{projection: existing, status: pending}

    ensures:
        if not exists existing:
            Projection.created(
                bounded_context: bounded_context,
                name: name,
                schema: schema,
                schema_version: 1,
                consumed_event_types: consumed_event_types,
                sync: sync,
                -- The bounded context's latest committed sequence, not
                -- merely the latest one of a consumed type: the history is
                -- folded through the same black-box project() an async
                -- projection's consumer uses, which likewise advances
                -- caught_up_to past every event it walks whether or not
                -- the projection consumed it (see the note above the
                -- rules). Anything less would leave a sync projection
                -- declared caught up while committed events in its own
                -- context sat ahead of it, which is precisely what
                -- SyncProjectionNeverLags forbids. Folded in full before
                -- this registration is considered complete, and safe to do
                -- inline here specifically because a brand-new projection
                -- has no live readers to protect from a fold in progress -
                -- unlike RebuildProjection's stage/replay/promote, which
                -- exists because a live Projection does have them.
                caught_up_to:
                    if first_time_sync_has_history: highest_sequence(context_events)
                    else: null
            )
        else if rebuild_needed:
            if exists staged:
                staged.schema = schema
                staged.schema_version =
                    if schema_changed: existing.schema_version + 1 else: existing.schema_version
                staged.consumed_event_types = consumed_event_types
                staged.sync = sync
                staged.caught_up_to = null
            else:
                ProjectionRebuild.created(
                    projection: existing,
                    schema: schema,
                    schema_version:
                        if schema_changed: existing.schema_version + 1 else: existing.schema_version,
                    consumed_event_types: consumed_event_types,
                    sync: sync,
                    caught_up_to: null,
                    status: pending
                )
        else:
            -- A trivial change, by definition one the schema did not take
            -- part in, so neither schema nor schema_version is touched
            -- here: schema_changed being true is itself enough to make a
            -- registration non-trivial.
            existing.consumed_event_types = consumed_event_types
            existing.sync = sync
}

-- The admin action the staging above waits for, and the only thing in this
-- spec that starts a replay. It is deliberately separate from
-- RegisterProjection rather than a branch within it: registration is
-- driven by a deploy, this is driven by a person deciding the cost of a
-- replay is worth paying now. Gated the same way registration is, by an
-- admin-level grant for the projection's own bounded context - but not,
-- unlike registration, on that context still being active. A rebuild only
-- replays already-committed history: it writes no new command and no new
-- event, which is the only thing an archived context forbids (see entity
-- BoundedContext), and it is the same reason QueryProjection, FetchEvents
-- and FetchCommands require no active context either. The requirement was
-- here by analogy to RegisterProjection, which does need an active context
-- because registration is part of a deploy-time reconciliation loop that
-- means nothing against an archived one; that analogy does not hold for a
-- replay-only action, so it is gone.
--
-- From the flip onwards the rebuild catches up from the start of the
-- bounded context's stream under its staged definition, advancing its own
-- caught_up_to through the same black-box mechanism an async projection
-- already uses (see the project() and await_projection_caught_up notes
-- above the rules); this spec pins down the observable caught_up_to
-- contract and nothing beyond it. What happens when it arrives is
-- promotion, described in the note above RegisterProjection.

rule RebuildProjection {
    when: RebuildProjection(access_mapping, projection)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = projection.bounded_context

    let staged = ProjectionRebuild{projection: projection, status: pending}

    -- Nothing staged is a rejection, not a silent no-op: an admin calling
    -- this expects a replay to start, and when the running binary declares
    -- exactly what the projection already serves there is nothing to
    -- replay. The same treatment RevokeToken gives an already-revoked
    -- token. A rebuild already building is not a second trigger either -
    -- this only ever finds a pending row.
    requires: exists staged
    requires: staged.status = pending

    ensures:
        staged.status = building
}

-- The other exit from a pending rebuild, and the only thing in this spec
-- that ends one without a replay. An admin who looks at a staged rebuild
-- and decides the replay is not worth paying for says so here: the pending
-- row ceases to exist and the live Projection carries on serving its own
-- current definition, untouched, exactly as it was while the row sat
-- there. Nothing is replayed, nothing is promoted, and nothing a reader
-- sees changes - dropping a staged definition is the one operation on a
-- rebuild that costs nothing.
--
-- Gated exactly as RebuildProjection is, and for the same reasons -
-- including the absence of an active-context requirement. Discarding
-- writes no command and no new event, and the archived case is the one
-- that most needs it: RegisterProjection does require an active context,
-- so a rebuild staged before its context was archived can never be
-- restaged, superseded or reconciled away, and without this it would sit
-- pending forever against a stream that no longer grows. It is not
-- restricted to that case, though. An admin who staged a change and
-- thought better of it before triggering it is making the same decision
-- against a live context, and there is no reason to make them wait for a
-- deploy to unmake it.
--
-- Only a pending rebuild can be discarded. A build already running is left
-- to finish under the definition it was triggered with: promotion is what
-- ends it, which is the same reason RegisterProjection never touches a
-- building row either.
--
-- Discarding is a decision about now, not a standing refusal. Against an
-- active context the next deploy declaring that same definition stages it
-- again, and correctly so - the running binary still says that is what the
-- projection should be. What a discard buys is that the staged row and the
-- replay it implies stop waiting on an admin who has already decided
-- against them; it does not overrule what the binary declares.

rule DiscardProjectionRebuild {
    when: DiscardProjectionRebuild(access_mapping, projection)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = projection.bounded_context

    let staged = ProjectionRebuild{projection: projection, status: pending}

    -- Nothing staged is a rejection rather than a silent no-op, the same
    -- treatment RebuildProjection gives it: an admin calling this expects
    -- there to be a staged definition to drop. A rebuild already building
    -- is not discardable either - this only ever finds a pending row.
    requires: exists staged
    requires: staged.status = pending

    ensures:
        not exists staged
}

-- The four token-minting rules below are deliberately uniform: one rule
-- per AccessToken variant, each gated by an admin-level RoleAccessMapping
-- for the bounded context that owns the type being scoped to. Minting a
-- REST credential is itself a GraphQL admin operation - the REST track
-- never issues its own credentials.

rule CreateExternalEventToken {
    when: CreateExternalEventToken(access_mapping, event_type, scope?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = event_type.bounded_context

    -- scope is carried through unvalidated and is the minted token's own,
    -- independent of the minting admin's - see rule CreateEventReadToken's
    -- own note below, which states that reasoning once for all four of
    -- these rules. What a scoped token here may then do with it is restrict
    -- what it creates rather than what it reads (see variant
    -- ExternalEventToken.scope).
    ensures:
        ExternalEventToken.created(
            id: generate_token_id(),
            event_type: event_type,
            secret: generate_token_secret(),
            scope: scope,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

rule CreateDirectCreationToken {
    when: CreateDirectCreationToken(access_mapping, event_type, scope?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = event_type.bounded_context

    -- scope on the same terms as the rule above - see rule
    -- CreateEventReadToken's own note below, and variant
    -- DirectCreationToken.scope.
    ensures:
        DirectCreationToken.created(
            id: generate_token_id(),
            event_type: event_type,
            secret: generate_token_secret(),
            scope: scope,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

rule CreateCommandToken {
    when: CreateCommandToken(access_mapping, command_type, scope?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = command_type.bounded_context

    -- scope on the same terms as the two rules above, restricting what may
    -- be triggered - see rule CreateEventReadToken's own note below, and
    -- variant CommandToken.scope.
    ensures:
        CommandToken.created(
            id: generate_token_id(),
            command_type: command_type,
            secret: generate_token_secret(),
            scope: scope,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

rule CreateEventReadToken {
    when: CreateEventReadToken(access_mapping, event_type, scope?, start_from?, start_at_sequence?, start_at_time?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = event_type.bounded_context

    -- Omitted, start_from is beginning; the binding is what both the
    -- guards below and the ensures at the bottom then read, so the
    -- default is written once rather than three times.
    let resolved_start_from = start_from ?? beginning

    -- scope is carried through unvalidated, the same treatment
    -- GrantRoleAccessMapping gives the scope it records: an opaque
    -- caller-chosen string compared for equality at use time and never
    -- interpreted here (see variant EventReadToken, and rule QueryEvents'
    -- guidance on event_owner_scope_satisfied). Omitted, the token is
    -- minted unrestricted within its event type - which is what every
    -- token minted before this argument existed is.
    --
    -- It is the minted token's own scope, and independent of the minting
    -- admin's: the grant authorising this call is checked for level and
    -- bounded context alone, and access_mapping.scope has no bearing on
    -- what may be named here. An admin holding an unscoped staff grant may
    -- mint a company-scoped token, and that is the ordinary case rather
    -- than an oversight - narrowing a REST credential is an act of issuing
    -- one, not an act of delegating the issuer's own reach.
    --
    -- Both paragraphs above are stated here once and hold for all four
    -- token-minting rules, not for this one alone: the three above take a
    -- scope on precisely these terms, and each refers back here rather than
    -- repeating them. They differ only in what a scoped token may then do
    -- with it - restrict what it creates or triggers, rather than what it
    -- reads (see rule AuthoriseCommandSubmission's guidance on
    -- tag_owner_scope_satisfied for the write-side half).
    --
    -- start_from, unlike scope, belongs to this rule alone and to no
    -- other minting rule. It is a one-time choice made here, at minting
    -- time, about where this token's first ConsumeEvents call begins, and
    -- it has no bearing on what the token may ever read - only on where
    -- in history it starts. A token minted latest is refused every
    -- private field, served every sensitive field as stored ciphertext,
    -- and filtered by event_read_allowed, by the caller's own filters and
    -- by its own scope exactly as a token minted beginning is. Omitted,
    -- it is beginning, which is where every token minted before this
    -- argument existed starts. See variant EventReadToken.start_from and
    -- rule ConsumeEvents.
    --
    -- Two of the four values need a second argument to go with them, and
    -- the three guards below are the whole of what that costs: at_sequence
    -- demands start_at_sequence and forbids start_at_time, at_time demands
    -- start_at_time and forbids start_at_sequence, and beginning and
    -- latest forbid both. Naming one without its value, or with the other
    -- one's, is a malformed request and is refused as such rather than
    -- quietly resolved to some default - a caller that asked to start
    -- somewhere in particular and did not say where has not asked for
    -- beginning, it has asked for something incoherent.
    requires:
        resolved_start_from = at_sequence
            implies (start_at_sequence != null and start_at_time = null)
    requires:
        resolved_start_from = at_time
            implies (start_at_time != null and start_at_sequence = null)
    requires:
        (resolved_start_from = beginning or resolved_start_from = latest)
            implies (start_at_sequence = null and start_at_time = null)

    ensures:
        EventReadToken.created(
            id: generate_token_id(),
            event_type: event_type,
            secret: generate_token_secret(),
            scope: scope,
            start_from: resolved_start_from,
            start_at_sequence: start_at_sequence,
            start_at_time: start_at_time,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

-- One revocation rule for every AccessToken variant: whichever type a
-- token is scoped to, the admin who may revoke it is the admin of the
-- bounded context that owns that type. token_scope names that context,
-- and is the only place the variants differ here - CommandToken points
-- at a CommandType, the other three at an EventType.

rule RevokeToken {
    when: RevokeToken(access_mapping, token)

    let token_scope =
        if token.purpose = CommandToken: token.command_type.bounded_context
        else: token.event_type.bounded_context

    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = token_scope
    requires: token.status = active
    ensures:
        token.status = revoked
        token.revoked_at = now
}

-- The five rules below manage Role and RoleAccessMapping themselves - the
-- access-control layer that gates every rule above. Four of them require
-- an existing active superadmin Role, which resolves the obvious
-- circularity (access control needs access control) for every case except
-- the very first superadmin Role. CreateSuperadmin is how that one comes
-- into being, and it is the only rule in this file that no Role
-- authorises.
--
-- At its own first process startup, if no active superadmin Role exists
-- yet, SkilJ generates a random, high-entropy bootstrap secret and prints
-- it once to its own startup log. The secret is held in memory and nowhere
-- else: never written to Postgres, never read from an environment variable
-- or a configuration file, never persisted anywhere at all. How it is
-- generated is a black box here, exactly as AccessToken.secret's
-- generation is (see generate_token_secret) - the spec owns that it is
-- unguessable and unstored, not which primitive produces it.
--
-- It is regenerated fresh on every startup, for as long as no superadmin
-- exists. Restarting before anyone has claimed it invalidates the previous
-- one and prints a new one in its place. That is the design rather than a
-- side effect of it: there is no durable secret sitting anywhere waiting
-- to leak, and an operator who loses the printed value restarts to get
-- another instead of being locked out of their own deployment.
--
-- Deliberately not a configuration value naming the initial admin's IdP
-- subject, which is the obvious alternative and the worse one. A
-- configured value is exactly the kind of thing that ends up in a process
-- listing, a container spec, a CI log and a secret manager, and it stays
-- there, readable, long after the one moment it mattered. A secret that
-- exists only in one process's memory, for one startup, has no such
-- resting places. It is also why the initial admin's external identity is
-- configured nowhere: it is supplied by whoever claims the secret, in the
-- same call.
--
-- Claiming it is CreateSuperadmin(bootstrap, bootstrap_secret, name,
-- external_subject) - a GraphQL mutation like the four below, but the one
-- that faces no resolved Role, since structurally it cannot: no Role
-- exists yet for the very first call. It faces the BootstrapSecret
-- directly, in the same shape the REST surfaces face an AccessToken
-- variant rather than a resolved identity (see entity BootstrapSecret for
-- why it is not one of those variants, and surface SuperadminBootstrap).
--
-- Two guards carry the gate proper. The secret presented must match the
-- one currently held in memory, and no active superadmin Role may exist -
-- the same `not exists` idiom AddBoundedContext uses for its name
-- uniqueness. The second is what closes the door structurally rather than
-- by convention: the instant an active superadmin exists, CreateSuperadmin
-- can never fire again, whatever is done with a printed secret afterwards
-- and however many times the process restarts. There is deliberately no
-- way to reopen it. A documented reopening path is a documented way in,
-- and no operational convenience is worth that.
--
-- The one state in which the door is open again is the state in which it
-- was open to begin with: no active superadmin Role at all. After the
-- first claim that is only reachable by revoking every superadmin Role,
-- which takes superadmin authority to do (see RevokeRole). So it is not a
-- bypass - it is the same self-healing that per-restart regeneration
-- gives, applied to a deployment whose superadmins have all been revoked
-- and which would otherwise be permanently unmanageable.
--
-- A third guard is inherited rather than specific to bootstrapping: the
-- external_subject claimed must not already be actively claimed, exactly
-- as in CreateRole. Without it UniqueActiveExternalSubject would break in
-- the reopened case above, where ordinary non-superadmin Roles may already
-- exist and hold subjects of their own.
--
-- What a successful call hands back is the created Role's own details -
-- its id, name and external_subject - and nothing else. In particular no
-- SkilJ-issued credential to log in with: this Role authenticates from
-- here on with a JWT from the trusted external IdP whose subject claim is
-- the external_subject just bound, exactly like every other Role (see the
-- identity resolution note above entity Role). SkilJ issues and stores no
-- credential of its own for any Role, and this is not the exception - the
-- bootstrap secret authorises one act of creation and is not a login.
--
-- The admin bounded context's own provenance is stamped at that same first
-- startup, but is independent of everything above: its created_at, and its
-- created_by pointing at the skilj SystemCreator marker, are written when
-- SkilJ first starts, whether or not CreateSuperadmin is ever called and
-- however long it takes to be. That provenance has the same problem this
-- mechanism has - nothing existed yet to have created it either - but not
-- the same timing, and unlike the bootstrap secret it is stamped once and
-- never restamped by a later restart. See the note above the Defaults
-- section.
--
-- Owning the bootstrap in the library rather than leaving it to the
-- consuming application, a migration script or a separate CLI binary is
-- the point rather than a convenience: it is what keeps Role's schema
-- entirely internal to SkilJ, with no raw SQL and no internal write path
-- handed to consumers just to get started. CreateSuperadmin is modelled as
-- a direct effect for the same reason as the token rules above.

rule CreateSuperadmin {
    -- name and external_subject are supplied by whoever holds the secret:
    -- claiming the bootstrap is exactly the act of registering which
    -- external IdP identity is to operate as the initial superadmin.
    when: CreateSuperadmin(bootstrap, bootstrap_secret, name, external_subject)

    -- Closes the door, permanently, the moment there is a superadmin to
    -- close it behind - see the note above.
    requires: not exists Role{superadmin: true, status: active}

    -- The secret presented must be the one currently held in memory.
    -- Compared the way an AccessToken secret is: constant time, so a failed
    -- attempt leaks nothing about how much of it was right (see the REST
    -- token presentation note above the surfaces).
    requires: secret_matches(bootstrap_secret, bootstrap.secret)

    -- Guards UniqueActiveExternalSubject, exactly as CreateRole does.
    requires: not exists Role{external_subject: external_subject, status: active}

    ensures:
        Role.created(
            id: generate_token_id(),
            external_subject: external_subject,
            name: name,
            superadmin: true,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

rule CreateRole {
    -- external_subject is supplied explicitly by the superadmin: creating
    -- a Role is exactly the act of registering which external IdP
    -- identity maps to it. SkilJ mints no credential of its own here.
    when: CreateRole(caller, name, superadmin, external_subject)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true

    -- Guards UniqueActiveExternalSubject: an external identity that is
    -- already actively claimed cannot be claimed a second time, so
    -- resolving a verified subject claim to a Role stays unambiguous.
    requires: not exists Role{external_subject: external_subject, status: active}

    ensures:
        Role.created(
            id: generate_token_id(),
            external_subject: external_subject,
            name: name,
            superadmin: superadmin,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

rule RevokeRole {
    when: RevokeRole(caller, role)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true
    requires: role.status = active
    ensures:
        role.status = revoked
        role.revoked_at = now
        for mapping in role.access_mappings where status = active:
            mapping.status = revoked
            mapping.revoked_at = now
}

rule GrantRoleAccessMapping {
    when: GrantRoleAccessMapping(caller, role, bounded_context, level, can_read_sensitive, scope?)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true
    requires: role.status = active
    requires: bounded_context.status = active
    requires: not exists RoleAccessMapping{role: role, bounded_context: bounded_context, status: active}

    -- scope is carried through unvalidated, the same treatment
    -- can_read_sensitive's boolean already gets: it is an opaque
    -- caller-chosen string compared for equality at read time and never
    -- interpreted here (see entity RoleAccessMapping, and rule
    -- QueryProjection's guidance on owner_scope_satisfied). Omitted, the
    -- mapping is created unrestricted within its bounded context - which
    -- is what every grant made before this argument existed is.
    ensures:
        RoleAccessMapping.created(
            role: role,
            bounded_context: bounded_context,
            level: level,
            can_read_sensitive: can_read_sensitive,
            scope: scope,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

rule RevokeRoleAccessMapping {
    when: RevokeRoleAccessMapping(caller, access_mapping)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    ensures:
        access_mapping.status = revoked
        access_mapping.revoked_at = now
}

-- ForgetSubject is not part of the superadmin-only group above: it is a
-- per-context admin operation, like ArchiveBoundedContext and the token
-- rules earlier - an admin-level RoleAccessMapping for a context can forget
-- subjects within it, the same way it can archive that context.

rule ForgetSubject {
    when: ForgetSubject(access_mapping, key)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: access_mapping.level = admin
    requires: access_mapping.bounded_context = key.bounded_context
    requires: key.status = active
    ensures:
        key.status = destroyed
        key.destroyed_at = now
}

-- The four rules below are the whole of the private-field mechanism's own
-- behaviour. Everything else about it is declaration (EventType and
-- CommandType's private_fields, validated at registration) or rendering
-- (the third redaction rule in render_event and render_command), and
-- neither of those needs a rule of its own.
--
-- Two of the three kinds are shareable and one is not, which is why there
-- are two minting rules rather than three: own and addressed each have a
-- determinate default reader who can decide to let someone else in, while
-- a team field has nobody to do the deciding - the team's name is the
-- grant, and a Role either carries it or does not (see enum
-- PrivateFieldKind). ListPrivateFieldGrants therefore never has anything
-- to list for a team field, and that is a property of the mechanism
-- rather than a gap in the listing.
--
-- Events and commands get a rule each rather than one polymorphic rule
-- over both, the same shape and for the same reason CreateExternalEvent
-- and CreateDirectEvent are two rules: what is being pointed at differs,
-- everything after it is identical, and folding the two into one would
-- mean a rule whose parameters contradict each other in every call. The
-- revocation half needs no such split - it names an existing grant, which
-- already knows which of the two it holds - so there is one of it.
--
-- None of the three is admin-gated, anywhere, and that is the defining
-- trait of the mechanism rather than an omission. Every other grant in
-- this file is made for a caller by someone with more authority than it -
-- a superadmin for a RoleAccessMapping, an admin for an AccessToken -
-- because those grants hand out capabilities the recipient never had. A
-- private-field grant hands out nothing the grantor did not already hold:
-- it lets a second Role see exactly what the first one can already see of
-- one record, or of the records it is itself the default reader of. There
-- is nothing for an admin to be protecting, so requiring one would only
-- make the ordinary case - a caller sharing its own record with a
-- colleague - an administrative ticket.

rule GrantPrivateFieldAccessForEvent {
    when: GrantPrivateFieldAccessForEvent(access_mapping, grantee, event?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: grantee.status = active

    -- A grant never spans bounded contexts, the same as everything else
    -- here: a named event must belong to the grantor's own. Vacuous for a
    -- blanket grant, which names no event to check - it is bound to the
    -- grantor's context by the mapping it was made under instead.
    requires:
        event = null
        or event?.bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context

    -- The grantor must genuinely be this record's own default private
    -- reader. Checked only when a record is named: a blanket grant cannot
    -- be checked against records that do not exist yet, so it is trusted
    -- here and re-derived at every read instead - see the guidance below.
    requires:
        event = null
        or is_default_private_reader(event, access_mapping)

    ensures:
        PrivateFieldGrant.created(
            bounded_context: access_mapping.bounded_context,
            grantor: access_mapping.role,
            grantee: grantee,
            event: event,
            command: null,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )

    @guidance
        -- is_default_private_reader(record, access_mapping) is a black box
        -- in the same register as event_owner_scope_satisfied and
        -- command_owner_scope_satisfied, and it is documented here once
        -- because this is the first rule that reaches for it.
        -- GrantPrivateFieldAccessForCommand below, and the render step in
        -- render_event and render_command, refer back to this paragraph
        -- rather than repeating it - one predicate with several call
        -- sites, never two implementations of the same question.
        --
        -- It takes an Event and a Command interchangeably, and that is
        -- load-bearing rather than convenient, the same way it is for
        -- protect_sensitive_fields: private_fields is declared identically
        -- on EventType and CommandType, and both records carry the two
        -- things the predicate reads - a metadata.client_id and a payload.
        --
        -- It holds when either of two things is true, checked against the
        -- record's own type's private_fields:
        --   - some own-kind field is declared and
        --     record.metadata.client_id equals access_mapping.role.id.
        --     The caller is the record's creator. client_id is the Role's
        --     internal id for anything a GraphQL caller produced (see rule
        --     AuthoriseCommandSubmission), never a value the caller
        --     reading it supplies.
        --   - some addressed-kind field is declared and
        --     access_mapping.role.external_subject equals the value that
        --     field's own addressee_field names in this record's payload.
        --     The caller is the party the record itself addressed. This is
        --     the identical match a sensitive field's subject already gets
        --     against external_subject in render_event, resolved against a
        --     payload field rather than an EncryptionKey's subject_value.
        --
        -- It does not hold otherwise, and in particular does not hold at
        -- all for a record whose type declares only team-kind private
        -- fields, or none: there is no default reader to be, so there is
        -- nothing to share and the grant is refused rather than made
        -- empty. Nor does it hold for a caller presenting no Role - a REST
        -- bearer token is nobody's client_id and carries no
        -- external_subject - which is the same fail-closed treatment the
        -- owner-scope predicates already give an owner nothing
        -- established.
        --
        -- Decided live against the record's type's current declaration,
        -- never snapshotted, exactly as owner_tag_key is: a registration
        -- that adds or drops a private field changes who is a default
        -- reader of that type's whole history from that moment on, and so
        -- changes which grants can still be made over it. Grants already
        -- made are unaffected as rows and re-derived as reach - see the
        -- render-time re-check in the render_event note above rule
        -- DeliverToSubscriptions.
        --
        -- A blanket grant - event and command both null - is the one call
        -- that reaches no such check, because there is no record to ask
        -- about. It is trusted when made and re-derived per record at
        -- every read, which is what stops it being a wider trust boundary
        -- than the per-record form: a reader coming through a blanket
        -- grant sees a private field only where the grantor would itself
        -- have been that record's default reader, so the grant never
        -- reaches past what the grantor could have handed over one record
        -- at a time.
}

rule GrantPrivateFieldAccessForCommand {
    when: GrantPrivateFieldAccessForCommand(access_mapping, grantee, command?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active
    requires: grantee.status = active

    -- The same two checks GrantPrivateFieldAccessForEvent applies just
    -- above, on the same terms and against the same predicate, differing
    -- only in which record is being pointed at. Its guidance is not
    -- restated here.
    requires:
        command = null
        or command?.bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context
    requires:
        command = null
        or is_default_private_reader(command, access_mapping)

    ensures:
        PrivateFieldGrant.created(
            bounded_context: access_mapping.bounded_context,
            grantor: access_mapping.role,
            grantee: grantee,
            event: null,
            command: command,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

rule RevokePrivateFieldAccess {
    when: RevokePrivateFieldAccess(access_mapping, grant)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active

    -- Only the Role that made the grant may end it. Not an admin, not a
    -- superadmin, and not the grantee - the exact counterpart to the
    -- mechanism being self-service on the way in. A grant is one caller's
    -- own decision about its own records in both directions.
    requires: access_mapping.role = grant.grantor

    requires: grant.status = active
    ensures:
        grant.status = revoked
        grant.revoked_at = now
}

-- Reading back what has been shared, and the one rule here that writes
-- nothing. It follows ListBoundedContexts' shape rather than the rules
-- around it, and for the same reason that one does: there is nothing to
-- dogfood into an Event when nothing changes.
--
-- Two audiences, one rule. A caller listing its own outgoing grants is
-- reading its own decisions back, which no level should have to be
-- granted for - so any active mapping may do it. A caller listing someone
-- else's is doing compliance or oversight, which is what every other
-- listing in this file gates behind admin, and this one is gated the
-- same. The distinction is a stated precondition rather than an implicit
-- one, and it is one rule rather than two because two rules sharing a
-- trigger with overlapping preconditions is an ambiguity, not a
-- clarification: the admin case genuinely includes the self case.
--
-- Nothing is ever listed for a team-kind private field, and nothing is
-- missing when nothing is: team membership is the grant, so there is no
-- row for it to have made and none for this to find (see enum
-- PrivateFieldKind).

rule ListPrivateFieldGrants {
    when: ListPrivateFieldGrants(access_mapping, grantor?)
    requires: access_mapping.status = active

    -- Own grants to anyone; anyone else's to an admin only.
    requires:
        grantor = null
        or grantor = access_mapping.role
        or access_mapping.level = admin

    -- Bound before the collection below so the caller's choice stays
    -- nameable inside it, where grantor is each grant's own field - the
    -- same shape QueryEvents uses for its tags argument.
    let subject = grantor ?? access_mapping.role

    let listed =
        PrivateFieldGrants where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context
            and grantor = subject

    ensures:
        PrivateFieldGrantsListed(
            access_mapping: access_mapping,
            grants: listed
        )

    @guidance
        -- Scoped to the caller's own bounded context, the same as every
        -- other read on this track: a grant made in another context is
        -- not merely unlisted but invisible, even to an admin, since an
        -- admin grant is per context too.
        --
        -- Revoked grants are listed alongside active ones rather than
        -- filtered out. What this answers is "what have I shared, and what
        -- have I stopped sharing" - and a revocation nobody can see is a
        -- revocation nobody can confirm happened. Filtering by status is
        -- part of the wire contract this spec leaves deferred, along with
        -- result shape and paging (see the scope header's exclusions).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant UniqueEventTypeNamePerContext {
    for a in EventTypes:
        for b in EventTypes:
            a != b and a.bounded_context = b.bounded_context implies a.name != b.name
}

invariant UniqueCommandTypeNamePerContext {
    for a in CommandTypes:
        for b in CommandTypes:
            a != b and a.bounded_context = b.bounded_context implies a.name != b.name
}

invariant UniqueProjectionNamePerContext {
    for a in Projections:
        for b in Projections:
            a != b and a.bounded_context = b.bounded_context implies a.name != b.name
}

invariant UniqueRebuildPerProjectionAndStatus {
    -- At most one pending and at most one building ProjectionRebuild per
    -- Projection, so "the staged next definition" and "the rebuild
    -- currently replaying" are each a single, unambiguous row. That is
    -- what lets RegisterProjection restage by overwriting the pending row
    -- rather than accumulating rows, and RebuildProjection trigger "the"
    -- pending one without choosing between candidates. The two coexisting
    -- is the deliberate case: a non-trivial registration arriving mid-build
    -- stages alongside the build rather than disturbing it.
    for a in ProjectionRebuilds:
        for b in ProjectionRebuilds:
            a != b and a.projection = b.projection implies a.status != b.status
}

invariant SubscriptionScopedToGrantContext {
    -- A Subscription only ever belongs to the bounded context its own
    -- access_mapping is granted against - the structural form of
    -- EventSubscription's GrantScopedToBoundedContext.
    for s in Subscriptions:
        s.bounded_context = s.access_mapping.bounded_context
}

invariant SyncProjectionNeverLags {
    -- What Projection.sync buys, stated as a property rather than as a
    -- mechanism: once a sync projection has been updated at least once,
    -- no committed event in its bounded context can sit ahead of it,
    -- since every subsequent one is applied in the same transaction as
    -- the event that feeds it. Phrased against Events directly, the same
    -- way DynamicConsistencyBoundaryHonoured compares Commands with
    -- Events, rather than through a "latest sequence" helper this spec
    -- doesn't otherwise have.
    --
    -- Deliberately scoped to caught_up_to != null: a sync projection that
    -- has never yet been updated is exempted rather than treated as a
    -- violation, since a fresh registration has no committed transaction
    -- to have piggybacked on yet. This invariant only states what must
    -- hold once a sync projection has actually caught up, not how or when
    -- it first gets there.
    --
    -- A projection becoming sync is not a second case of that gap. sync
    -- only ever flips false -> true through a rebuild, and a rebuild is
    -- promoted already caught up (see the note above RegisterProjection),
    -- so there is no moment at which a Projection is declared sync while
    -- trailing. Quantified over Projections alone for the same reason: a
    -- building ProjectionRebuild declaring sync = true while it is still
    -- replaying is untouched by this, and correctly so - nothing reads it.
    for p in Projections:
        for e in Events:
            (p.sync = true and p.caught_up_to != null and e.bounded_context = p.bounded_context)
                implies p.caught_up_to >= e.sequence
}

invariant DynamicConsistencyBoundaryHonoured {
    -- A command decides based on events matching its consistency_tags,
    -- up to consistency_boundary (the highest sequence observed at
    -- decision time). Any event with a matching tag and a higher
    -- sequence must be one of this command's own triggered events -
    -- never a foreign event that slipped in between the command's read
    -- and its commit.
    for c in Commands:
        for e in Events:
            (e.bounded_context = c.bounded_context and c.consistency_tags.any(t => t in e.tags)
                and e.sequence > c.consistency_boundary)
                implies e in c.triggered_events
}

invariant SequenceIsGaplessPerBoundedContext {
    for e in Events:
        e.sequence = 0 or Events.any(other => other.bounded_context = e.bounded_context and other.sequence = e.sequence - 1)
}

invariant ConsistencyTagKeysAreDeclared {
    -- A command may only consistency-check against tag keys that an
    -- EventType in the command's own bounded context actually declares
    -- (and therefore indexes). This is the structural form of "can we
    -- verify an index exists for this tag at runtime".
    --
    -- Deliberately scoped to c.bounded_context, not all EventTypes: the
    -- only events this command can ever match are its own context's (see
    -- ProcessCommand's matching_events and
    -- DynamicConsistencyBoundaryHonoured), so an identically named key
    -- declared in some unrelated context indexes nothing this command
    -- will read - see the namespacing note on TagMapping.key.
    --
    -- Held within one reconciliation pass by registering EventTypes before
    -- CommandTypes (see the note above RegisterEventType), not by any
    -- check in ProcessCommand itself - a CommandType shipped ahead of an
    -- EventType it anticipates, in an earlier release, can still violate
    -- this for as long as that gap lasts.
    for c in Commands:
        for tag in c.consistency_tags:
            c.bounded_context.event_types.any(et => et.tag_mappings.any(m => m.key = tag.key))
}

invariant EventTagsMatchDeclaredMappings {
    for e in Events:
        for tag in e.tags:
            e.event_type.tag_mappings.any(m => m.key = tag.key)
}

invariant CommandConsistencyTagsMatchDeclaredMappings {
    for c in Commands:
        for tag in c.consistency_tags:
            c.command_type.tag_mappings.any(m => m.key = tag.key)
}

invariant SystemTriggeredClientIdIsSystem {
    -- The other three origins record a real originator in client_id (a
    -- token id, or the Role id behind the submitting RoleAccessMapping). A
    -- system-triggered event has none, so it records the literal
    -- "system" rather than borrowing an identity it doesn't have.
    for e in Events:
        e.origin = SystemTriggered implies e.metadata.client_id = "system"
}

invariant CorrelationIdIsAlwaysRecorded {
    -- "Every stored record belongs to some chain" as a property of stored
    -- state rather than as four rules happening to agree with each other.
    -- ProcessCommand, CreateExternalEvent, CreateDirectEvent and
    -- CreateSystemEvent are between them the only ways a Command or an
    -- Event comes into being, and each either uses the correlation_id its
    -- caller named or generates one - but that is four separate ensures
    -- clauses, and nothing about their agreement is checkable from any one
    -- of them. Stating it here is the same move AdminContextIsPermanent and
    -- ScheduledTypeIsFullyConfigured make: a later rule that also writes a
    -- Command or an Event cannot quietly reintroduce a record belonging to
    -- no chain at all.
    --
    -- causation_id is deliberately not asserted alongside it, and the
    -- asymmetry is the whole point of having two fields. A null
    -- causation_id is a root - a submission its caller originated, a direct
    -- creation, a scheduler occurrence - which is a real and common state,
    -- not a missing value. A null correlation_id would be a record that
    -- cannot be reassembled into any chain, including its own, which is
    -- what this rules out. See the correlation note above the rules.
    for c in Commands:
        c.metadata.correlation_id != null
    for e in Events:
        e.metadata.correlation_id != null
}

invariant ScheduledTypeIsFullyConfigured {
    -- "No default, ever" as a property of stored state rather than as a
    -- guard on one rule. A type that fires on a schedule knows when it
    -- fires, what it owes for an occurrence nobody was watching for, and
    -- how far its schedule has been accounted for - all three, or it is not
    -- scheduled. RegisterEventType is what enforces the first two and
    -- establishes the third, but stating it here makes it checkable in its
    -- own right, so a later rule that also switches scheduling on cannot
    -- quietly reintroduce a scheduled type with no policy and let
    -- CreateSystemEvent decide what to do about it.
    for t in EventTypes:
        t.system_triggered_allowed = true implies
            t.system_triggered_schedule != null
            and t.missed_occurrence_policy != null
            and t.schedule_position != null
}

invariant FiredOccurrenceIsAccountedFor {
    -- The two positions cannot contradict each other: an occurrence that
    -- produced an event is one the schedule position has accounted for, so
    -- last_fired_at never runs ahead of schedule_position. They are equal
    -- whenever the most recent occurrence to be accounted for was one that
    -- fired - always, under replay_backlog - and diverge exactly where a
    -- policy passed an occurrence over.
    for t in EventTypes:
        t.last_fired_at != null implies
            t.schedule_position != null and t.last_fired_at <= t.schedule_position
}

invariant SystemCreatedContextIsTheAdminContext {
    -- The counterpart of the above for bounded contexts. AddBoundedContext
    -- always records the calling superadmin Role, so the system marker is
    -- the admin context's alone: no other context may claim SkilJ as its
    -- creator, and the admin context never acquires a Role it was not
    -- created by.
    for bc in BoundedContexts:
        bc.created_by.kind = SystemCreator implies bc = admin
}

invariant AdminContextIsPermanent {
    -- The admin context never stops accepting work. ArchiveBoundedContext
    -- is the only rule that can move a context out of active, and it
    -- guards against this one; stating it here makes the property
    -- checkable in its own right rather than an emergent consequence of
    -- that guard, so a later rule that also archives cannot quietly
    -- reintroduce the hole.
    admin.status = active
}

invariant TemplateIsNeverItselfTemplated {
    -- One level, and this is what makes it one. A context named as some
    -- other context's template has no template of its own, so a tenant is
    -- never a template and the chain nobody wants to walk cannot be built
    -- - see the note above rule CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate for why
    -- precedence and cycles are the two questions this closes.
    --
    -- Stating it here rather than leaving it to
    -- CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate's guard is the same move
    -- AdminContextIsPermanent makes: the property is checkable in its own
    -- right, so a later rule that also sets a template cannot quietly
    -- reintroduce the second level.
    for bc in BoundedContexts:
        bc.template != null implies bc.template.template = null
}

invariant RevokedRoleImpliesMappingsRevoked {
    for r in Roles:
        r.status = revoked implies r.access_mappings.all(t => t.status = revoked)
}

invariant UniqueActiveAccessPerRoleAndContext {
    -- A Role holds at most one active grant per bounded context, so
    -- "what can this Role do in this context" is never ambiguous.
    for a in RoleAccessMappings:
        for b in RoleAccessMappings:
            a != b and a.role = b.role and a.bounded_context = b.bounded_context
                implies not (a.status = active and b.status = active)
}

invariant UniqueActiveExternalSubject {
    -- At most one active Role per external_subject, so a verified subject
    -- claim from the IdP resolves to exactly one Role. Two active Roles
    -- claiming the same external identity would make identity resolution
    -- ambiguous, and therefore authorisation ambiguous with it.
    for a in Roles:
        for b in Roles:
            a != b and a.external_subject = b.external_subject
                implies not (a.status = active and b.status = active)
}

invariant UniqueActiveEncryptionKeyPerSubject {
    -- At most one active key per (bounded context, subject), so
    -- protect_sensitive_fields' get-or-create lookup is never ambiguous.
    for a in EncryptionKeys:
        for b in EncryptionKeys:
            a != b and a.bounded_context = b.bounded_context
                and a.subject_key = b.subject_key and a.subject_value = b.subject_value
                implies not (a.status = active and b.status = active)
}

invariant UniqueReadCursorPerToken {
    -- At most one cursor per EventReadToken, so ConsumeEvents'
    -- get-or-create lookup is never ambiguous. Unqualified by status,
    -- unlike UniqueActiveEncryptionKeyPerSubject above: a cursor has no
    -- lifecycle of its own - it exists or it does not - and is never
    -- superseded by a fresh one.
    for a in ReadCursors:
        for b in ReadCursors:
            a != b implies a.token != b.token
}

invariant PrivateFieldGrantNamesAtMostOneRecord {
    -- A grant points at one event, at one command, or at neither - never
    -- at both. Neither is the blanket form rather than a malformed one,
    -- which is why this bounds the pair rather than requiring one of them
    -- (see entity PrivateFieldGrant). Both minting rules already write
    -- null into whichever field they are not for, so nothing this
    -- specification does can violate it; it is stated because the shape it
    -- rules out is one a reader of the entity would otherwise have to
    -- infer from those two rules being the only writers.
    for g in PrivateFieldGrants:
        not (g.event != null and g.command != null)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The four actors below are all GraphQL-track identities: each is a
-- RoleAccessMapping, reached from the Role a verified external JWT's
-- subject claim resolved to (see the identity resolution note above
-- entity Role). Nothing here is established by the JWT itself: the
-- status and level conditions below are evaluated against current state
-- on every operation, so revocation bites immediately. The REST track
-- has no actors of its own - an AccessToken variant is the party a REST
-- surface faces directly. SuperadminBootstrap is the one GraphQL surface
-- with no actor either, for a related reason: there is no Role to resolve
-- when the first one does not yet exist, so it faces the BootstrapSecret
-- directly in the same way (see entity BootstrapSecret).

actor ReadAccess {
    -- Any active RoleAccessMapping authorises read: write and admin grants
    -- include read implicitly, since they can already do strictly more.
    -- Faced by ProjectionQuery and EventSubscription.
    identified_by: RoleAccessMapping where status = active
}

actor WriteAccess {
    identified_by: RoleAccessMapping where status = active and level in {write, admin}
}

actor AdminAccess {
    identified_by: RoleAccessMapping where status = active and level = admin
}

actor SensitiveDataAccess {
    -- Authorises reading the decrypted value of sensitive fields, on top
    -- of whatever level the underlying RoleAccessMapping already grants.
    -- Orthogonal to level rather than a level of its own, so it is not
    -- what a surface faces: ProjectionQuery faces ReadAccess and reads
    -- can_read_sensitive off that same mapping (see its
    -- SensitiveFieldsStayProtected guarantee). A caller who is this actor
    -- is by construction also a ReadAccess one.
    identified_by: RoleAccessMapping where status = active and can_read_sensitive = true
}

actor Superadmin {
    identified_by: Role where status = active and superadmin = true
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- REST token presentation, written once here and pointed at from each of
-- the four REST surfaces below (ExternalEventIngestion,
-- DirectEventCreation, CommandTrigger, EventFetch), the same way the
-- in-memory event cache note above the rules is. An AccessToken is
-- presented as:
--
--     Authorization: Bearer <id>.<secret>
--
-- The id half is the non-secret AccessToken.id, so the server looks the
-- token row up by primary key - a single indexed lookup, no scan over
-- candidate tokens. The secret half is then verified against that row's
-- stored hash with a constant-time comparison, so a failed verification
-- leaks nothing timing-wise about how much of the secret was right. Both
-- the hashing scheme and the comparison are black boxes here, the same
-- register as generate_token_secret and generate_token_id: the spec owns
-- that the secret is never stored or compared in plaintext, not which
-- primitive does it.
--
-- This is the REST track only. GraphQL callers present a JWT from the
-- trusted external IdP instead and hold no SkilJ-issued secret at all
-- (see the identity resolution note above entity Role); the two tracks
-- share no credential and no presentation.

surface ExternalEventIngestion {
    facing adapter: ExternalEventToken
    context event_type: EventType
        where this = adapter.event_type and external_creation_allowed = true and adapter.status = active

    provides:
        SubmitExternalEvent(
            adapter, event_type, payload, source_content, source_context?,
            dedupe_partition_key?, dedupe_sequence?,
            correlation_id?, causation_id?)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToEventType
        -- A token only authorises submitting events for the EventType it
        -- was issued for; it grants no other capability.

    @guarantee WriteScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared
        -- Within the one type it was issued for, a token that names a scope
        -- (see ExternalEventToken.scope) may create events only for the
        -- owner it names. This is the write-side half of the mechanism
        -- EventFetch's EventsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared describes on the read
        -- side - one owner dimension, declared on the event type and
        -- validated against its own tag mappings, governing both what a
        -- scoped token may write and what a scoped reader may later see. The
        -- general write-side reasoning, including why a single-record write
        -- rejects rather than filters, is stated in full by
        -- CommandSubmission's guarantee of this name and is not repeated
        -- here.
        --
        -- The scope checked is the token's own, as it is on EventFetch and
        -- for the same reason: a bearer credential carries no
        -- RoleAccessMapping, and the minting admin's own scope has no
        -- bearing on what the token it issued may create (see rule
        -- CreateEventReadToken, whose note covers all four minting rules).
        -- A token naming no scope, which is every token unless the minting
        -- admin named one, submits exactly as it always has; so does any
        -- submission for an event type that declares no owner dimension.

    @guarantee EveryEventGetsACorrelationId
        -- correlation_id and causation_id are both optional, and this is the
        -- surface most likely to supply them: an adapter in front of a
        -- broker is forwarding a message something upstream already caused,
        -- and these are where it passes that chain on. Whatever it sends is
        -- stored verbatim, never reshaped and never interpreted - an
        -- upstream UUID, a traceparent-style string or a broker's own
        -- message id all survive the trip intact, since nothing here
        -- constrains their form beyond a length bound.
        --
        -- An adapter supplying neither is not left out: the event is given a
        -- freshly generated correlation_id all the same, and causation_id
        -- stays null, which says truthfully that this library was told of no
        -- cause rather than that there was none. See the correlation note
        -- above the rules.
        --
        -- Neither value takes any part in deduplication. A redelivery is
        -- recognised by dedupe_partition_key and dedupe_sequence and by
        -- nothing else, so two submissions differing only in correlation_id
        -- are the same message, and a resubmission bearing a fresh
        -- correlation_id creates no event if its sequence is at or below the
        -- watermark.

    @guarantee OmittingTheDedupePairChangesNothing
        -- dedupe_partition_key and dedupe_sequence are optional, and
        -- omitting them is a supported, permanent way to submit rather than
        -- a legacy shape: a submission carrying neither has nothing looked
        -- up and nothing recorded against it, and creates its event every
        -- single time, exactly as every submission behaved before the pair
        -- existed. An adapter that never supplies them is never
        -- deduplicated, never has a submission silently produce no event,
        -- and never has to opt out of anything to keep that.
        --
        -- They travel as a pair. An adapter supplying exactly one of them
        -- is refused outright, which is a rejection of a malformed request
        -- and is not the same thing as the accepted no-op below.

    @guarantee ARedeliveryProducesNoEventAndNoOutcome
        -- A submission whose dedupe_sequence is at or below the highest
        -- this adapter has had an event written for under this
        -- dedupe_partition_key creates no event at all: no
        -- ExternalTriggered, no sequence consumed, no projection updated,
        -- nothing written. It is accepted rather than refused: an
        -- at-least-once source replaying a partition after a crash is doing
        -- what it is meant to do, and being told it made a bad request
        -- would be untrue.
        --
        -- What it is not answered with is any account of what an earlier
        -- message did. Unlike a duplicate idempotency key on
        -- CommandSubmission, which returns the original outcome, a
        -- watermark remembers one number and not which event any particular
        -- past message produced, so there is no original outcome to return
        -- and none is fabricated (see rule CreateExternalEvent).
        --
        -- Scoped to this adapter and this partition key alone, so two
        -- adapters that independently chose the same partition key string
        -- never deduplicate each other's messages.

    @guidance
        -- REST only. Token-authenticated capabilities are deliberately
        -- kept off the GraphQL surface: GraphQL is the Role/
        -- RoleAccessMapping track, REST is the AccessToken track, and a
        -- caller holds a credential for one or the other.
        --
        -- The token is presented as "Authorization: Bearer <id>.<secret>"
        -- - see the REST token presentation note above the surfaces.
}

surface DirectEventCreation {
    facing adapter: DirectCreationToken
    context event_type: EventType
        where this = adapter.event_type and direct_creation_allowed = true and adapter.status = active

    provides:
        SubmitDirectEvent(adapter, event_type, payload, correlation_id?, causation_id?)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToEventType
        -- A token only authorises creating events for the EventType it
        -- was issued for; it grants no other capability.

    @guarantee WriteScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared
        -- Within the one type it was issued for, a token that names a scope
        -- (see DirectCreationToken.scope) may create events only for the
        -- owner it names - the read-side sibling being EventFetch's
        -- EventsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared, and the general write-side
        -- reasoning being CommandSubmission's guarantee of this name. This
        -- is ExternalEventIngestion's guarantee of this name applied
        -- unchanged to direct creation, differing only in which token
        -- variant carries the scope, and restates neither.
        --
        -- The scope checked is the token's own, not the minting admin's (see
        -- rule CreateEventReadToken). A token naming no scope, which is
        -- every token unless the minting admin named one, creates exactly as
        -- it always has; so does any creation for an event type that
        -- declares no owner dimension.

    @guarantee EveryEventGetsACorrelationId
        -- ExternalEventIngestion's guarantee of this name applied unchanged
        -- to direct creation, and not restated: supplied values stored
        -- verbatim, a correlation_id generated when none is named, and
        -- causation_id left null when nothing upstream is. The only
        -- difference is how often a caller has either to give - a direct
        -- creation is more often the start of a chain than a link in one.

    @guidance
        -- REST only, the same as external event ingestion, including the
        -- "Authorization: Bearer <id>.<secret>" presentation described in
        -- the note above the surfaces.
}

surface EventFetch {
    facing token: EventReadToken
    context event_type: EventType
        where this = token.event_type and event_read_allowed = true and token.status = active

    exposes:
        event_type.name
        event_type.schema
        event_type.schema_version

    provides:
        FetchEvents(token, event_type, filters, after_sequence?)
        ConsumeEvents(token, ack_mode?, filters)
        AcknowledgeEvents(token, sequence)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToEventType
        -- A token only authorises reading events of the one EventType it
        -- was issued for; it grants no write capability at all, and no
        -- visibility into any other type. This holds for all three reads
        -- alike - the token is the whole of the permission model here,
        -- and a server-side cursor adds reach into nothing it did not
        -- already reach.

    @guarantee EventsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared
        -- Within the one type it was issued for, a token that names a
        -- scope (see EventReadToken.scope) only reaches events that belong
        -- to it. This is the REST track's half of the same mechanism
        -- EventQuery's guarantee of this name describes on the GraphQL
        -- track, differing only in whose scope is checked: there it is the
        -- grant's, here it is the token's own, since a bearer credential
        -- carries no RoleAccessMapping and the minting admin's own scope
        -- has no bearing on what the token it issued may read (see rule
        -- CreateEventReadToken). A token naming no scope, which is every
        -- token unless the minting admin named one, reaches everything of
        -- its type exactly as it always has; so does any token whose event
        -- type declares no owner dimension, whatever the token's scope
        -- holds.
        --
        -- All three reads filter, none rejects, because all three range
        -- over many events: an event owned by someone else, or one whose
        -- owner is not established at all - no tag under the declared key,
        -- or one holding null - is silently excluded from what fetching
        -- and consuming serve, and the call itself still succeeds.
        -- Ownership has to be provable, not merely unrefuted. A
        -- server-side cursor adds no reach past this either, exactly as it
        -- adds none past TokenScopedToEventType above: an event excluded
        -- for scope is passed over without being served and without the
        -- cursor moving past it, the same treatment a filtered-out event
        -- already gets. AcknowledgeEvents is untouched by any of this - it
        -- serves nothing, only moving a position the caller supplies (see
        -- rule QueryEvents' guidance on event_owner_scope_satisfied).

    @guarantee SensitiveFieldsStayEncrypted
        -- Sensitive fields are delivered as stored, which is to say as
        -- ciphertext. Both paths to decryption belong to the GraphQL
        -- track and neither can be reached from here: a bearer token
        -- carries no RoleAccessMapping and so no can_read_sensitive, and
        -- the self-subject path is structurally unavailable rather than
        -- merely withheld - an EventReadToken is scoped to an EventType
        -- and tied to no Role, so there is no verified caller identity to
        -- match against a field's subject. A caller needing its own
        -- sensitive data reads it over GraphQL.

    @guarantee PrivateFieldsStayRedacted
        -- A field its EventType marked private is delivered as null here,
        -- always, to every caller, on both the fetch and the consume path.
        -- Not narrowed, not conditional and not grantable: there is no
        -- call that reaches one, and no grant anyone can be given that
        -- would.
        --
        -- The reasoning is the sibling of SensitiveFieldsStayEncrypted's
        -- above, arriving at the same place from the opposite direction. A
        -- sensitive field is safe on this track by being stored encrypted,
        -- so delivering it as stored is already enough. A private field is
        -- stored in plaintext - it needs no key and survives no erasure,
        -- which is the whole point of it (see value PrivateField) - so
        -- delivering it as stored would disclose it, and it is redacted on
        -- the way out instead.
        --
        -- All three of the ways a caller can be entitled to a private
        -- field are stated in terms of a Role, and this track has none, so
        -- each fails closed structurally rather than by omission. An
        -- EventReadToken is scoped to an EventType and tied to no Role: it
        -- is nobody's client_id, so it is no record's creator; it carries
        -- no Role.name, so it is in no team; and it carries no
        -- external_subject, so it is nobody's addressee. A
        -- PrivateFieldGrant cannot reach it either, since a grant names a
        -- Role as its grantee and there is no Role here to name - so no
        -- amount of sharing on the GraphQL side ever opens a private field
        -- on this one. A caller needing one reads it over GraphQL, exactly
        -- as a caller needing its own sensitive data does.

    @guarantee LatestEventsAlwaysAvailable
        -- A fetch always sees every committed event of its type that
        -- matches the request, including the most recently committed
        -- ones, and the recent end of that history is served without a
        -- database round trip. Older events are answered more slowly but
        -- just as completely: the in-memory cache bounds how fast a read
        -- is answered, never how far back a caller may read.

    @guarantee CursorIsPerTokenAndItsModeIsFixed
        -- A server-side read position belongs to one EventReadToken and
        -- to nothing finer: there is no consumer name to pass, and two
        -- independent positions mean two tokens. Whichever ack_mode a
        -- token's first ConsumeEvents call chose is the one it keeps for
        -- that token's lifetime - a later call may omit it or restate it,
        -- and is rejected if it names the other one - so the delivery
        -- guarantee a consumer is running under never changes underneath
        -- it.

    @guarantee DeliveryFollowsAckMode
        -- auto_advance is at-most-once: serving advances the cursor, so
        -- an event served to a caller that dies before processing it is
        -- never served again. That is the same guarantee EventSubscription
        -- makes under DeliveryIsAtMostOnce, reached here by a cursor
        -- rather than by a live connection.
        --
        -- manual_ack is at-least-once, and the one place in this
        -- specification that guarantee exists: nothing is passed over
        -- unacknowledged, and the price is that a caller consuming twice
        -- without acknowledging in between is served the same events
        -- twice. Handling those duplicates is the caller's own
        -- responsibility - idempotent processing, say - and never
        -- something this library de-duplicates on its behalf. "Consuming
        -- twice" here means two calls that each find the claim clear or
        -- expired (see ReadCursor.checked_out_at) - a second call while a
        -- first caller's own claim is still live is served nothing rather
        -- than a duplicate, so two callers racing each other never both
        -- act on the same unacknowledged batch at once. The batch is
        -- still redelivered, exactly as before that existed, to whichever
        -- caller next finds the claim clear or expired - this narrows
        -- when a duplicate can be handed out, never whether one
        -- eventually is.

    @guidance
        -- REST only, with the token presented as
        -- "Authorization: Bearer <id>.<secret>" - see the note above the
        -- surfaces. Deliberately coarse otherwise: this establishes the
        -- capability and its scoping, not the query wire contract. Paging
        -- and cursor encoding stay deferred with the rest of that
        -- contract, as does the real-time websocket complement to this
        -- pull-based fetch - what that complement will be is settled
        -- though, down to reusing this token, this opt-in, the
        -- Subscription/Filter model and EventSubscription's delivery and
        -- resume answer; only its wire contract is outstanding (see the
        -- scope header's exclusions). A filter whose operator doesn't fit
        -- the field it names is rejected outright, not merely
        -- unsatisfiable - see valid_filters in the note above rule
        -- FetchEvents.
        --
        -- Three ways to read the one EventType this token was issued for,
        -- all pull-based and all under this same permission model.
        -- FetchEvents is client-tracked and unchanged: the caller keeps
        -- its own position and passes after_sequence on every call, which
        -- stays the simplest thing for a caller with somewhere durable to
        -- keep a cursor. ConsumeEvents is server-tracked in either ack
        -- mode: SkilJ keeps the position, keyed by the token alone (see
        -- entity ReadCursor and the note above rule ConsumeEvents).
        -- AcknowledgeEvents moves a manual_ack cursor forward once the
        -- caller has actually processed what it was served. Mixing the
        -- client-tracked read with the server-tracked one on a single
        -- token is not forbidden, but it means two positions that know
        -- nothing of each other: FetchEvents neither reads the cursor nor
        -- moves it.
        --
        -- This is the read the per-bounded-context in-memory event cache
        -- is primarily there for (see the note above the rules): its
        -- startup warm-up count - configured when the process starts,
        -- defaulting to the 1000 most recent events per bounded context -
        -- is a tuning decision about how far back reads stay fast, not
        -- about what this surface will answer.
}

surface ProjectionQuery {
    facing access_mapping: ReadAccess
    context projection: Projection
        where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context

    exposes:
        projection.name
        projection.schema
        projection.schema_version

    provides:
        QueryProjection(access_mapping, projection, key?, wait_for_sequence?)

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- A grant only authorises querying projections belonging to its
        -- own bounded context; a projection in any other context is not
        -- merely unreadable but invisible. Read level is enough - write
        -- and admin grants include it, since they can already do strictly
        -- more - which is why this faces ReadAccess rather than a level of
        -- its own.

    @guarantee GrantScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared
        -- Within its own bounded context, a grant that names a scope (see
        -- RoleAccessMapping.scope) only authorises querying projection
        -- instances that belong to it. A projection may declare which tag
        -- key names its "owner" dimension - in deployed configuration
        -- only, with no registration surface and nothing an admin can
        -- read it back from - and each instance's own owner value is
        -- derived from the tags of the events that built it, never
        -- supplied by the caller querying it. An instance owned by
        -- someone else, or one whose owner is not established at all -
        -- including a key nothing has touched yet - is not merely
        -- unreadable but invisible, exactly as a projection in another
        -- bounded context is above: ownership has to be provable here,
        -- not merely unrefuted. A grant naming no scope, which is every
        -- grant unless a superadmin named one, is unrestricted within its
        -- context and queries exactly as it always has; so is any query
        -- against a projection that declares no owner dimension,
        -- whatever the grant's scope holds. This is authorisation, not
        -- decryption: it decides which instances answer at all, before
        -- SensitiveFieldsStayProtected below decides what an answering
        -- instance may show (see rule QueryProjection's guidance on
        -- owner_scope_satisfied).

    @guarantee TeamGatedWhenDeclared
        -- Within its own bounded context, a projection may declare which
        -- Role name is required to query it at all - in deployed
        -- configuration only, with no registration surface and nothing an
        -- admin can read it back from, exactly as the owner tag key above
        -- - and one that declares a required team only answers queries
        -- from a grant whose own Role.name equals it exactly. Membership
        -- is the grant: there is nothing to share and nothing to revoke,
        -- and entitlement ends the moment the Role is no longer named
        -- that, which is the same semantics a team-kind private field
        -- already carries, applied to the whole projection rather than to
        -- one field of one record. To a Role named anything else the
        -- projection is not merely unreadable but invisible, exactly as a
        -- projection in another bounded context is above. A projection
        -- that declares no required team is unrestricted by this
        -- guarantee, which is every projection today. This is independent
        -- of GrantScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared above rather than a refinement
        -- of it: a projection may declare both an owner dimension and a
        -- required team, and a query must satisfy both - neither
        -- substitutes for the other, and failing either rejects (see rule
        -- QueryProjection's guidance on team_only_satisfied).

    @guarantee SensitiveFieldsStayProtected
        -- A projection built from a field its source EventType marked
        -- sensitive stays protected exactly as the source event is:
        -- sensitivity is declared once, on the EventType, and inherited
        -- rather than re-declared per projection - there is no
        -- per-projection sensitive-field declaration to keep in step
        -- with it, or to forget. Decryption is decided once per query
        -- against the very same RoleAccessMapping this surface faces,
        -- taking the queried instance's own key as the subject, under
        -- either of two grants: the bounded-context-wide
        -- can_read_sensitive, or the caller's own verified IdP identity
        -- matching that key - a Role reading data about itself needs no
        -- separate grant. A caller with neither is never handed a
        -- decrypted value, not even one it is expected to discard (see
        -- QueryProjection's guidance on read_projection). The deliberate
        -- limitation this carries: an instance whose own key is not
        -- itself a real subject value - an unkeyed projection, or one
        -- keyed on something else entirely - offers no subject for
        -- either grant to be checked against, so none of its content
        -- ever decrypts here, not even for a can_read_sensitive caller.
        -- EventSubscription's push is the contrasting case: it decides
        -- per field, each against that field's own subject.

    @guarantee ReadYourWritesWhenRequested
        -- A query naming a wait_for_sequence is never answered from a
        -- position behind it: either the projection has reached that
        -- sequence and answers, or the query fails. It is never silently
        -- answered from a stale position. A query naming no sequence
        -- makes no such promise and answers from wherever the projection
        -- currently is. A failure here is reported as a timeout
        -- specifically - distinguishable from any other rejection this
        -- surface can produce - so a caller knows to retry rather than
        -- treat it as permanent (see the requires clause in
        -- QueryProjection).

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only: a query, qualified by bounded context per the
        -- unified-graph naming scheme, authenticated as a Role and gated
        -- by that Role's RoleAccessMapping - the read counterpart to
        -- CommandSubmission's mutation. The REST track has no equivalent:
        -- a token-holding caller pulls raw events via EventFetch instead.
        --
        -- Richly typed rather than opaque: the GraphQL type this surface
        -- answers with is generated from Projection.schema, which is a
        -- black box here in exactly the same register as
        -- EventType.schema and CommandType.schema. That is what lets a
        -- client consume a real, typed subset of the graph and introspect
        -- it, rather than parsing a blob. How a schema becomes a concrete
        -- GraphQL type, and field/argument naming with it, stays deferred
        -- (see the scope header's exclusions).
        --
        -- key names which instance of the projection to answer from, and
        -- is optional for the reason it is optional in the rule: a
        -- projection holding one shared value is queried without one, so
        -- its callers see no change from this argument existing at all. A
        -- projection holding many - one row per customer, one per course
        -- - is read by naming the row wanted. Which keys exist is decided
        -- by the fold that built them, per event (see the project() note
        -- above the rules), not declared at registration, so nothing here
        -- enumerates them: this surface answers for the key it is given
        -- and exposes no listing of the rest.
        --
        -- wait_for_sequence is the read-your-writes lever and the only
        -- thing that distinguishes an async projection from a sync one
        -- from out here. The intended use is submit-then-read: a caller
        -- submits a command, takes the highest sequence among the events
        -- it triggered, and passes that as wait_for_sequence on the
        -- follow-up query, so it observes its own write rather than the
        -- state that preceded it. Omitting it is the cheap path for
        -- callers that tolerate lag. A sync projection satisfies any
        -- sequence immediately, so the argument costs such a caller
        -- nothing. How long the wait lasts before the query fails is a
        -- process-start configuration knob, not a per-query argument or a
        -- fixed value - see await_projection_caught_up's note above the
        -- rules - and a timeout is reported distinguishably from any
        -- other rejection this surface can produce, so a caller knows to
        -- retry rather than treat it as permanent (see
        -- ReadYourWritesWhenRequested above). The exact GraphQL error
        -- shape that carries that distinction stays deferred with the
        -- rest of the wire contract (see the scope header's exclusions).
}

surface EventSubscription {
    facing access_mapping: ReadAccess

    provides:
        CreateAllEventsSubscription(access_mapping, event_types, from_sequence?)
        CreateEventTypeSubscription(access_mapping, event_type, filters, from_sequence?)

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- A grant only authorises subscribing within its own bounded
        -- context - either kind - and only ever delivers events from
        -- that context. Read level is enough; write and admin grants
        -- include it, the same reasoning as ProjectionQuery.

    @guarantee EventsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared
        -- And within that context, a subscription never receives an event
        -- it isn't scoped to. A grant that names a scope (see
        -- RoleAccessMapping.scope) is delivered only those events whose
        -- own derived owner value equals it, and only where the event's
        -- type declares an owner dimension at all - the same mechanism
        -- EventQuery's guarantee of this name describes for the pull side,
        -- against the same grant, applied here per pushed event. This
        -- narrows delivery on top of whatever the subscription already
        -- narrows by: it applies to both kinds alike, whether the
        -- subscription names event types or names one type and filters
        -- within it. A grant naming no scope, which is every grant unless a
        -- superadmin named one, is delivered exactly what it always was; so
        -- is any event whose type declares no owner dimension.
        --
        -- Not delivering is the whole of what happens: an event another
        -- owner holds is silently not pushed, never pushed and then
        -- withheld, and its absence is not a gap this caller could notice
        -- or should treat as one - DeliveryIsAtMostOnce's "no gaps" is a
        -- promise about what this subscription is entitled to, and an
        -- event it is not entitled to was never part of that. Ownership
        -- has to be provable, not merely unrefuted: an event whose owner
        -- is not established at all - no tag under the declared key, or
        -- one holding null - is not delivered either. Decided live on
        -- every delivery against the grant's current scope, exactly as the
        -- grant's status is (see rule QueryEvents' guidance on
        -- event_owner_scope_satisfied).

    @guarantee FreshEventsOnly
        -- A subscription never replays history: delivery starts strictly
        -- after from_sequence, fixed once at creation time (see
        -- CreateAllEventsSubscription/CreateEventTypeSubscription and
        -- DeliverToSubscriptions). A caller wanting current state first
        -- queries it separately - FetchEvents or QueryProjection - and,
        -- for a gap-free handoff into the live feed, supplies the
        -- sequence it read up to as from_sequence here.

    @guarantee DeliveryIsAtMostOnce
        -- While the connection is live, every matching event arrives
        -- once, in sequence order: no duplicates, no gaps, and nothing to
        -- acknowledge back. Nothing is held for a connection that is not
        -- live. A subscription lasts exactly as long as its connection,
        -- so an event committed while that connection is down reaches
        -- this caller not at all - it is not queued, retried or replayed
        -- afterwards. A transport-level disconnect is therefore the only
        -- way an event can be missed here, and it is by the same token
        -- the only signal that one was: a live stream has no gap to watch
        -- for, so there is no separate sequence-monitoring duty on the
        -- client beyond noticing that its connection dropped.

    @guarantee RevocationClosesTheConnection
        -- A subscription whose backing grant is revoked does not merely
        -- fall silent: the server closes its connection, and closes it
        -- with a reason the caller can tell apart from an ordinary
        -- transport drop. This follows directly from
        -- DeliveryIsAtMostOnce - a live connection is the caller's only
        -- evidence that it has missed nothing - so a revoked
        -- subscription left open would be indistinguishable from a
        -- bounded context with nothing to say, and the caller would wait
        -- on it indefinitely believing itself current.
        --
        -- The two closes call for different responses, which is why they
        -- are told apart. An ordinary drop means "reconnect from the last
        -- sequence you received": the client-driven handoff described
        -- under FreshEventsOnly, with a read-back over FetchEvents or
        -- QueryProjection for the span committed while away. This one
        -- means the subscription is permanently over - the grant it
        -- delivered to no longer exists, so neither a fresh subscription
        -- nor a QueryProjection read-back under that same grant will be
        -- accepted, and reconnecting under it is pointless until a
        -- superadmin grants access again. Nothing the caller was entitled
        -- to is left unseen either: delivery stops at the revocation,
        -- which is the same instant the entitlement does.

    @guarantee DeliverySpansInstances
        -- Which instance a load balancer put this connection on is
        -- neither something the caller has to know nor something that can
        -- narrow what it hears. An event committed on any instance is
        -- delivered to every matching live subscription on every instance,
        -- and a revocation applied on any instance closes matching
        -- subscriptions on every instance - the same close, carrying the
        -- same distinguishable reason, as if the revocation had arrived
        -- here (see RevocationClosesTheConnection). The instances are
        -- symmetric and reach each other only through the one Postgres
        -- database they share; none of them discovers, elects or
        -- addresses another (see the scope header's exclusions).
        --
        -- Reaching another instance is at most once, exactly as delivery
        -- to the caller is (see DeliveryIsAtMostOnce), and a reach that
        -- does not arrive is a lateness rather than a correctness
        -- failure, because Postgres stays the source of truth throughout
        -- and nothing here is decided from what an instance remembers.
        -- For an event, what is lost is promptness on this instance's
        -- live feed and nothing else: the event is committed, readable
        -- over FetchEvents, QueryEvents and QueryProjection, and picked
        -- up by the caller's own reconnect handoff from the last sequence
        -- it received. For a revocation, delivery has already stopped
        -- regardless - every delivery re-checks the grant's durable
        -- status live rather than trusting a snapshot (see the note above
        -- DeliverToSubscriptions) - so what a lost reach delays is the
        -- reasoned close, leaving a revoked connection open and silent
        -- until it drops rather than leaking anything to it.

    @guarantee SensitiveFieldsStayProtected
        -- A sensitive field is delivered decrypted only when the
        -- receiving access_mapping is entitled to it: either
        -- can_read_sensitive is true on the mapping (the same
        -- bounded-context-wide grant ProjectionQuery reads), or the
        -- caller's own verified identity matches that field's own
        -- subject (see render_event's note above DeliverToSubscriptions).
        -- A caller entitled to neither still receives the event, with
        -- that field left as ciphertext exactly as EventFetch and
        -- ProjectionQuery leave it - never decrypted and then filtered
        -- afterwards.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL subscription, not a query or mutation: the real-time
        -- counterpart to ProjectionQuery, the same way CommandSubmission
        -- is the mutation counterpart to a query. A subscription's
        -- lifetime is the live connection's - created when the client
        -- issues the subscription operation, ended when it unsubscribes
        -- or disconnects. There is no separate cancel mutation and no
        -- state that outlives the connection beyond what from_sequence
        -- lets a caller hand to a fresh one on reconnect.
        --
        -- Reconnecting is therefore creating a subscription, not resuming
        -- one: the dropped subscription no longer exists to resume. The
        -- client supplies as from_sequence the last sequence it actually
        -- received - each delivered event carries its own - and, if it
        -- cares about what was committed while it was away, reads that
        -- span back over FetchEvents or QueryProjection first. That is
        -- deliberately the same move as the initial handoff into the live
        -- feed described under FreshEventsOnly, driven entirely by the
        -- client, rather than a second resume mechanism owned by the
        -- server. The websocket complement to EventFetch adopts this same
        -- answer unchanged when it arrives (see the scope header's
        -- exclusions).
        --
        -- The one close the client must not treat that way is a revoked
        -- grant's (see RevocationClosesTheConnection). That the close
        -- carries a reason, and that the reason is distinguishable from
        -- an ordinary transport drop, is guaranteed here; the exact close
        -- code or error payload carrying it stays deferred with the rest
        -- of the wire contract, exactly as ProjectionQuery's timeout
        -- distinction does (see the scope header's exclusions).
        --
        -- AllEventsSubscription.event_types narrows which event types are
        -- delivered (empty means every type in the bounded context) but
        -- offers no per-field filtering the way EventTypeSubscription
        -- does - see the note on that field. A caller wanting field-level
        -- filtering across several types opens one EventTypeSubscription
        -- per type instead of a single AllEventsSubscription.
        --
        -- No context clause: CreateAllEventsSubscription is scoped
        -- entirely by access_mapping.bounded_context, and
        -- CreateEventTypeSubscription's event_type carries its own
        -- bounded_context, checked directly in each rule's requires -
        -- there is no single entity both actions share the way
        -- ProjectionQuery shares Projection.
}

surface EventQuery {
    facing access_mapping: AdminAccess
    context event: Event
        where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context

    exposes:
        event.metadata.created_at
        event.origin

    provides:
        QueryEvents(access_mapping, event_types, tags?, after_sequence?)
        CountEvents(access_mapping, event_types, tags?)
        InspectEvent(access_mapping, event)

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level grant only authorises reading events appended
        -- into its own bounded context - browsing them, counting them and
        -- inspecting one of them alike. An event in any other context is
        -- not merely unreadable but invisible, the same way a command in
        -- another context is to CommandQuery and a projection in another
        -- context is to ProjectionQuery. Naming one directly is no way
        -- around that either: this surface exists only for events of the
        -- grant's own context, so another context's event is not something
        -- InspectEvent can be pointed at.

    @guarantee EventsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared
        -- Within its own bounded context, a grant that names a scope (see
        -- RoleAccessMapping.scope) only reaches events that belong to it.
        -- An event type may declare which of its own tag keys names its
        -- owner dimension - registered, and validated against that type's
        -- tag mappings, unlike the projection-side declaration
        -- ProjectionQuery's GrantScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared describes - and
        -- each event's own owner value is derived from its payload the way
        -- every tag is, never supplied by the caller reading it. A grant
        -- naming no scope, which is every grant unless a superadmin named
        -- one, reaches everything in its context exactly as it always has;
        -- so does any read of an event whose type declares no owner
        -- dimension, whatever the grant's scope holds.
        --
        -- Browsing and counting filter. An event owned by someone else, or
        -- one whose owner is not established at all - no tag under the
        -- declared key, or one holding null - is silently excluded from
        -- queryEvents' results and from countEvents' count alike, and the
        -- query itself still succeeds. The count is over exactly the
        -- population the browse would show, so a scoped caller cannot
        -- learn another owner's volume from an aggregate it cannot see the
        -- members of.
        --
        -- inspectEvent is the exception, because it names one event rather
        -- than ranging over many: there the same unmet ownership rejects
        -- the read outright, and what the caller is left with is the
        -- framing another bounded context's events already get above -
        -- invisible, not merely unreadable. Ownership has to be provable
        -- here, not merely unrefuted. This is authorisation, not
        -- decryption: it decides which events answer at all, before
        -- SensitiveFieldsStayProtected below decides what an answering
        -- event may show (see rule QueryEvents' guidance on
        -- event_owner_scope_satisfied).

    @guarantee SensitiveFieldsStayProtected
        -- A field its EventType marked sensitive is stored as ciphertext
        -- and read back that way unless the caller is entitled to it,
        -- decided per field against the very same RoleAccessMapping this
        -- surface faces, under either of two grants: the
        -- bounded-context-wide can_read_sensitive, or the caller's own
        -- verified IdP identity matching that field's subject. Admin level
        -- alone is not one of them - can_read_sensitive is orthogonal to
        -- level. A caller with neither grant still receives the event,
        -- with that field left as ciphertext exactly as ProjectionQuery,
        -- EventSubscription and CommandQuery leave it, never decrypted and
        -- then redacted afterwards.
        --
        -- This covers browsing and inspecting. Counting is outside it
        -- because there is nothing for it to cover: a count discloses no
        -- field content, only how many events matched filters the caller
        -- supplied itself.

    @guarantee LatestEventsAlwaysAvailable
        -- A query always sees every committed event of its bounded context
        -- that matches the request, including the most recently committed
        -- ones, and the recent end of that history is served without a
        -- database round trip. Older events are answered more slowly but
        -- just as completely: the in-memory cache this surface shares with
        -- EventFetch bounds how fast a read is answered, never how far
        -- back a caller may read. A count is answered over that same
        -- complete population, not over the cached window.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only, and queries rather than mutations: the
        -- admin-facing pull that neither existing event read provides.
        -- EventSubscription pushes and never replays history (see its
        -- FreshEventsOnly), so it answers nothing about the past;
        -- EventFetch pulls, but over REST, for one EventType, on a bearer
        -- token with no Role behind it (see its TokenScopedToEventType).
        -- A Role holding an admin grant had no way in before this - see
        -- the note above rule QueryEvents for why this is admin-facing
        -- rather than part of ordinary read-level reading.
        --
        -- The context clause is what makes InspectEvent's scoping
        -- structural rather than a check - an event outside the grant's
        -- bounded context is not addressable here at all - the same
        -- pattern ProjectionQuery uses for a Projection. QueryEvents and
        -- CountEvents range over that same context rather than over the
        -- one event, the way FetchCommands ranges over its context's
        -- commands.
        --
        -- Deliberately coarse on the wire contract otherwise: this
        -- establishes the capability and its scoping, not how a query, its
        -- result shape, or paging and cursor encoding are expressed - all
        -- still deferred with the rest of that contract, exactly as they
        -- are for EventFetch, ProjectionQuery and CommandQuery (see the
        -- scope header's exclusions).
}

surface SnapshotInspection {
    facing access_mapping: AdminAccess

    provides:
        InspectSnapshot(access_mapping, snapshot_name, tag_value)

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- A grant only authorises inspecting summaries belonging to its
        -- own bounded context - the same scoping EventQuery/ProjectionQuery
        -- already give their own context, established here through
        -- access_mapping.bounded_context directly rather than a separate
        -- context clause (see the guidance below for why this surface
        -- has none).

    @guarantee GrantScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared
        -- Within its own bounded context, a grant that names a scope (see
        -- RoleAccessMapping.scope) only authorises inspecting summaries
        -- that belong to it. A summary may declare which tag key names its
        -- "owner" dimension - in deployed configuration only, with no field
        -- on any entity here and no registration surface to read it back
        -- from, the same register the tag key the summary is itself keyed
        -- on is already drawn from (see the guidance below for why this
        -- surface has nothing registered behind it at all). That key need
        -- not be the one the summary is keyed on: a summary keyed per
        -- account may still be owner-scoped by company. Each recorded
        -- summary's own owner value is derived as events are folded into
        -- it - from the tag, on whichever event touched it, whose key
        -- matches that declaration - and is never supplied by the caller
        -- inspecting it, exactly as the projection-side owner value is
        -- derived rather than sent. That is the closest sibling to this
        -- one, declaration and all: see rule QueryProjection's guidance on
        -- owner_scope_satisfied for the three-holds/fails-closed contract,
        -- which this surface applies unchanged rather than restating.
        --
        -- This surface names one summary rather than ranging over many -
        -- there is only ever the one record behind a (snapshot_name,
        -- tag_value) pair - so unmet ownership rejects the read outright
        -- and filters nothing, the same single-record treatment
        -- inspectEvent and QueryProjection give. What a rejected caller is
        -- left with is the framing another bounded context's summaries
        -- already get above: invisible, not merely unreadable. Ownership
        -- has to be affirmatively provable here, not merely unrefuted.
        --
        -- One consequence worth stating plainly: for a scoped caller, a
        -- summary owned by someone else and a genuinely cold tag_value
        -- nothing has touched yet fail closed alike, and are not
        -- distinguishable from each other. The cold case does not get the
        -- "nothing recorded yet, not a failure" answer UnknownNameIsAnError
        -- below describes, because an owner nothing has established is the
        -- case an attacker reaches for - the same stricter treatment
        -- QueryProjection gives its own untouched key. Both stay distinct
        -- from naming a snapshot_name that was never defined for this
        -- bounded context at all, which remains the real, reported error
        -- UnknownNameIsAnError makes it; a rejection here is neither that
        -- error nor the cold answer.
        --
        -- A grant naming no scope, which is every grant unless a superadmin
        -- named one, inspects exactly as it always has; so does any
        -- inspection of a summary that declares no owner dimension,
        -- whatever the grant's scope holds. This is layered on top of
        -- GrantScopedToBoundedContext above rather than instead of it: that
        -- scoping holds unchanged, and a scope only narrows further within
        -- it - the same relationship ProjectionQuery's own two guarantees
        -- have.

    @guarantee UnknownNameIsAnError
        -- snapshot_name naming something that was never defined for this
        -- bounded context at all is a real, reported error - distinct
        -- from a real, defined one that simply has nothing recorded yet
        -- for tag_value, which answers with nothing rather than an error
        -- (the same "cold instance, not a failure" treatment
        -- QueryProjection already gives an untouched projection key).

    @guarantee SensitiveFieldsStayProtected
        -- Whatever this surface answers with is full derived content, the
        -- same sensitivity class EventQuery's own raw event content
        -- already is - which is why this faces AdminAccess rather than
        -- ReadAccess the way ProjectionQuery does, even though the shape
        -- otherwise rhymes with a projection lookup.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only, admin-facing, read-only - the one place the
        -- optional summary named in the guidance above ProcessCommand's
        -- own decide() call is itself observable. No context clause:
        -- unlike Event or Projection, a summary is never created by any
        -- rule in this specification - it exists only as compiled,
        -- deployed configuration, the same register CommandType.tag_mappings
        -- itself is drawn from, with no registration surface of its own
        -- and so nothing here for a context clause to range over.
        -- access_mapping alone is enough to scope every answer to one
        -- bounded context, the same way QueryEvents/CountEvents scope
        -- through it without a context clause either.
        --
        -- Deliberately not folded into ProjectionQuery, and not
        -- expressed as a kind of Projection: a Projection is
        -- eventually-consistent, admin-rebuildable read-model
        -- infrastructure with no obligation to the freshness a live
        -- decision needs - see the guidance above ProcessCommand's own
        -- decide() call for the full reasoning. Keeping this surface
        -- separate, rather than a QueryProjection variant, is what keeps
        -- an ordinary projection rebuild from ever being able to affect
        -- what a live command decision sees.
        --
        -- Deliberately coarse on the wire contract otherwise, the same
        -- register as EventQuery/ProjectionQuery: this establishes the
        -- capability and its scoping, not the exact result shape (which
        -- fields answer for tag_key, the recorded version, how far
        -- events have been folded in, and the summary's own content) -
        -- deferred with the rest of that contract (see the scope
        -- header's exclusions).
}

surface CommandSubmission {
    facing access_mapping: WriteAccess
    context command_type: CommandType
        where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context and bounded_context.status = active

    provides:
        SubmitCommand(
            access_mapping, command_type, payload, idempotency_key?,
            correlation_id?, causation_id?)

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- A write-level grant only authorises submitting commands for
        -- CommandTypes belonging to its own bounded context.

    @guarantee WriteScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared
        -- Within its own bounded context, a grant that names a scope (see
        -- RoleAccessMapping.scope) may submit commands only for the owner it
        -- names. This is the write-side half of the mechanism CommandQuery's
        -- CommandsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared describes on the read side, the
        -- same way that guarantee is itself the command-side half of
        -- EventQuery's EventsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared - one owner dimension,
        -- declared once on the command type and validated against its own
        -- tag mappings, governing both what a scoped caller may write and
        -- what it may later read back. The owner value is derived from the
        -- submitted payload exactly as every tag is, never named separately
        -- by the caller, so it is not something a submitter can assert
        -- around.
        --
        -- It rejects rather than filters: exactly one command is being
        -- authorised here, not a range, so there is nothing to filter it out
        -- of and the submission is refused outright. That is the same
        -- single-record treatment inspectEvent and SnapshotInspection give
        -- their own reads, and the counterpart to the filtering
        -- CommandsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared does over many commands. A
        -- rejection on this ground is reported as such rather than as a
        -- decide() rejection: nothing reaches decide() at all.
        --
        -- A grant naming no scope, which is every grant unless a superadmin
        -- named one, submits exactly as it always has; so does any
        -- submission for a command type that declares no owner dimension,
        -- whatever the grant's scope holds.

    @guarantee EveryCommandGetsACorrelationId
        -- correlation_id and causation_id are both optional, and a caller
        -- supplying neither is not thereby left out of the chain: the stored
        -- Command is given a freshly generated correlation_id, and every
        -- event that command triggers inherits it, so a submission is always
        -- reassemblable as a chain afterwards whether or not the caller named
        -- one. A caller that does supply one - a UI tying several submissions
        -- to one user action, an adapter forwarding an upstream id - has that
        -- value used verbatim and never replaced.
        --
        -- causation_id is different in kind and is never generated: it names
        -- the one thing that directly caused this submission, and a caller
        -- that names none had no such thing, which is a root rather than a
        -- gap. It is stored as supplied and left null otherwise. Neither
        -- value is interpreted, and neither affects whether the command is
        -- accepted - only that it is at most 200 characters, which is a
        -- malformed-request rejection and nothing to do with the domain (see
        -- valid_correlation_id in the correlation note above the rules).

    @guarantee OmittingTheKeyChangesNothing
        -- idempotency_key is optional, and omitting it is a supported,
        -- permanent way to submit rather than a legacy shape: a submission
        -- carrying no key has nothing looked up and nothing remembered
        -- against it, and is processed as new every single time, exactly as
        -- every submission behaved before keys existed. A caller that never
        -- supplies one is never deduplicated, never answered with an
        -- earlier submission's outcome, and never has to opt out of
        -- anything to keep that.

    @guarantee DuplicateAcceptanceReturnsTheOriginalOutcome
        -- A submission whose idempotency_key already produced an accepted
        -- outcome for this command type in this bounded context is answered
        -- with that original outcome - the same triggered events - and
        -- decide() is not called again, no second Command is created and no
        -- second event is appended. It is the original answer even when
        -- events committed since would make a fresh decision differ; that
        -- is what the key is for, not an imprecision tolerated for
        -- cheapness. A caller can tell such an answer apart from a fresh
        -- acceptance rather than having to infer it.
        --
        -- Bounded to acceptances on purpose: a key that produced a
        -- rejection has nothing remembered against it, and a resubmission
        -- bearing it is decided fresh, every time (see rule
        -- ProcessCommand). Scoped to the bounded context and command type
        -- alone, and not to the submitting Role.

    @guarantee MatchingEventsRequiresAdminLevel
        -- The matching_events set surfaced on a rejection (see the
        -- guidance below) is returned only when access_mapping.level =
        -- admin - not for any write-level grant this surface otherwise
        -- accepts for submission itself. matching_events is full event
        -- content, the same visibility EventQuery's own AdminAccess
        -- requires (see its SensitiveFieldsStayProtected), so a
        -- write-level grant able to submit commands is not thereby able
        -- to browse the event history submission reads from. A
        -- write-level caller's rejection still carries rejection_reason
        -- and rejection_kind as usual, with matching_events withheld.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only: a mutation, qualified by bounded context per the
        -- unified-graph naming scheme, authenticated as a Role and gated
        -- by that Role's RoleAccessMapping. The token-authenticated way
        -- to trigger the same command over REST is CommandTrigger; both
        -- converge on ProcessCommand.
        --
        -- A rejection also returns the matching_events rule ProcessCommand
        -- itself computed and handed to decide() - the same "fresh at
        -- commit time" set that rule's own note describes, not a
        -- snapshot from an earlier read, so a caller debugging an
        -- unexpected rejection sees exactly what decide() saw. Scoped to
        -- a rejection only - an accepted submission's own matching_events
        -- isn't a further question the caller needs answered, since
        -- acceptance already tells them what happened. CommandTrigger's
        -- own REST response does not carry this: that surface stays
        -- narrowly scoped to an automated caller acting on an outcome,
        -- not debugging one. See MatchingEventsRequiresAdminLevel above
        -- for the access-level restriction on top of that scoping.
}

surface CommandTrigger {
    facing token: CommandToken
    context command_type: CommandType
        where this = token.command_type and rest_trigger_allowed = true and token.status = active

    provides:
        TriggerCommand(
            token, command_type, payload, idempotency_key?,
            correlation_id?, causation_id?)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToCommandType
        -- A token only authorises triggering the one CommandType it was
        -- issued for; it grants no other capability, and in particular no
        -- way to read anything back beyond the command's own outcome.

    @guarantee WriteScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared
        -- Within the one type it was issued for, a token that names a scope
        -- (see CommandToken.scope) may trigger commands only for the owner
        -- it names. This is the REST track's half of the guarantee of this
        -- name on CommandSubmission, which states the reasoning in full -
        -- the shared owner dimension with CommandQuery's read side, the
        -- derived-not-asserted owner value, and why a single-record write
        -- rejects rather than filters - and is not restated here.
        --
        -- The one difference is whose scope is checked: the token's own, not
        -- the grant's, since a bearer credential carries no
        -- RoleAccessMapping and the minting admin's own scope has no bearing
        -- on what the token it issued may trigger (see rule
        -- CreateEventReadToken, whose note covers all four minting rules).
        -- A token naming no scope, which is every token unless the minting
        -- admin named one, triggers exactly as it always has; so does any
        -- trigger of a command type that declares no owner dimension.

    @guarantee EveryCommandGetsACorrelationId
        -- The same guarantee CommandSubmission carries, on the same terms
        -- and for the same reasons, and not restated here: a correlation_id
        -- is generated when the caller names none, used verbatim when it
        -- does, and inherited by every event the command triggers, while
        -- causation_id is never generated and is null for a trigger with
        -- nothing upstream. This is the track where a caller most often does
        -- have something to name - an automated caller acting on a message
        -- it consumed elsewhere forwards that chain's own id here.

    @guarantee OmittingTheKeyChangesNothing
        -- idempotency_key is optional here too, with exactly the force it
        -- has on CommandSubmission: a trigger carrying no key has nothing
        -- looked up and nothing remembered against it, and is processed as
        -- new every single time, exactly as every trigger behaved before
        -- keys existed. An automated caller that never supplies one keeps
        -- its existing behaviour unchanged and opts out of nothing.

    @guarantee DuplicateAcceptanceReturnsTheOriginalOutcome
        -- A trigger whose idempotency_key already produced an accepted
        -- outcome for this command type in this bounded context is answered
        -- with that original outcome, without decide() running again and
        -- without a second Command or a second event - the same guarantee
        -- CommandSubmission carries, backed by the same recorded outcome
        -- (see rule ProcessCommand).
        --
        -- The same one, literally: a key is scoped to the bounded context
        -- and command type and to nothing else, so a command triggered here
        -- and one submitted over CommandSubmission under that same key for
        -- that same command type are one key, not two. The two tracks share
        -- the record exactly as they already share ProcessCommand.

    @guidance
        -- REST only, with the token presented as
        -- "Authorization: Bearer <id>.<secret>" - see the note above the
        -- surfaces. Intended for AI and other automated callers, which
        -- is why the command type must opt in via rest_trigger_allowed:
        -- a command being submittable by a Role over GraphQL does not
        -- make it triggerable by a token.
}

surface CommandQuery {
    facing access_mapping: AdminAccess

    provides:
        FetchCommands(access_mapping, command_types, after?, before?, triggered_event?, after_command?)

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level grant only authorises reading commands submitted
        -- into its own bounded context; a command in any other context is
        -- not merely unreadable but invisible, the same way a projection
        -- in another context is to ProjectionQuery. This holds for the
        -- reverse lookup too: naming an event from another bounded context
        -- is not a way into that context's commands.

    @guarantee CommandsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared
        -- Within its own bounded context, a grant that names a scope (see
        -- RoleAccessMapping.scope) only reaches commands that belong to it.
        -- A command type may declare which of its own tag keys names its
        -- owner dimension, registered and validated against that type's own
        -- tag mappings exactly as an event type's is, and each command's
        -- own owner value is derived from its payload the way every tag is,
        -- never supplied by the caller reading it. This is the command-side
        -- half of the guarantee EventQuery's
        -- EventsScopedToOwnerWhenDeclared states in full, and does not
        -- restate it: a grant naming no scope reaches everything in its
        -- context exactly as it always has, and so does any read of a
        -- command whose type declares no owner dimension.
        --
        -- It filters, and only filters. There is no single-command read
        -- here for inspectEvent's rejecting form to have a counterpart in -
        -- fetchCommands ranges over many commands, so one owned by someone
        -- else, or one whose owner is not established at all, is silently
        -- excluded from its result and the fetch itself still succeeds.
        -- That covers the reverse lookup as much as the type and time
        -- filters: naming an event whose command the grant does not own
        -- yields nothing, rather than that command anyway. This is
        -- authorisation, not decryption: it decides which commands answer
        -- at all, before SensitiveFieldsStayProtected below decides what an
        -- answering command may show (see rule FetchCommands' guidance on
        -- command_owner_scope_satisfied).

    @guarantee SensitiveFieldsStayProtected
        -- A field its CommandType marked sensitive is stored as
        -- ciphertext and read back that way unless the caller is entitled
        -- to it, decided per field against the very same RoleAccessMapping
        -- this surface faces, under either of two grants: the
        -- bounded-context-wide can_read_sensitive, or the caller's own
        -- verified IdP identity matching that field's subject. Admin level
        -- alone is not one of them - can_read_sensitive is orthogonal to
        -- level. A caller with neither grant still receives the command,
        -- with that field left as ciphertext exactly as ProjectionQuery
        -- and EventSubscription leave it, never decrypted and then
        -- redacted afterwards.
        --
        -- Erasure reaches these fields with nothing extra: a command and
        -- an event naming the same subject encrypt under the same
        -- EncryptionKey, so ForgetSubject makes both unreadable at once,
        -- to this surface and to anyone reading the stored rows directly
        -- (see the protect_sensitive_fields note above the rules).

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only, and a query rather than a mutation: the audit
        -- counterpart to CommandSubmission's mutation. Admin-facing on
        -- purpose - see the note above rule FetchCommands for why reading
        -- other callers' raw command payloads is not a read-level
        -- capability. The REST track has no equivalent and is not meant
        -- to grow one: a CommandToken triggers exactly one command type
        -- and reads nothing back (see CommandTrigger's
        -- TokenScopedToCommandType).
        --
        -- No context clause, for the same reason EventSubscription has
        -- none: a fetch is scoped entirely by
        -- access_mapping.bounded_context and may span several
        -- CommandTypes at once, so there is no single entity the action
        -- hangs off the way ProjectionQuery hangs off a Projection.
        --
        -- Deliberately coarse on the wire contract otherwise: this
        -- establishes the capability, its scoping and its filters, not
        -- how a query, its result shape, or paging and cursor encoding
        -- are expressed - all still deferred with the rest of the wire
        -- contract, exactly as they are for EventFetch and
        -- ProjectionQuery (see the scope header's exclusions).
}

surface TypeRegistration {
    facing access_mapping: AdminAccess
    context bounded_context: BoundedContext
        where this = access_mapping.bounded_context

    exposes:
        for projection in bounded_context.projections:
            projection.name
            for rebuild in projection.rebuilds:
                rebuild.status
                rebuild.schema_version
                rebuild.caught_up_to

        for scheduled_type in bounded_context.event_types where system_triggered_allowed = true:
            scheduled_type.name
            scheduled_type.system_triggered_schedule
            scheduled_type.missed_occurrence_policy
            scheduled_type.last_fired_at
            scheduled_type.schedule_position

        for event_type in bounded_context.event_types:
            event_type.name
            event_type.schema
            event_type.schema_version
            event_type.tag_mappings
            event_type.owner_tag_key
            event_type.sensitive_fields
            event_type.private_fields
            event_type.external_creation_allowed
            event_type.direct_creation_allowed
            event_type.system_triggered_allowed
            event_type.event_read_allowed

        for command_type in bounded_context.command_types:
            command_type.name
            command_type.schema
            command_type.schema_version
            command_type.tag_mappings
            command_type.owner_tag_key
            command_type.sensitive_fields
            command_type.private_fields
            command_type.rest_trigger_allowed

    provides:
        RegisterEventType(access_mapping, bounded_context, name, schema, tag_mappings,
            sensitive_fields, private_fields, external_creation_allowed, direct_creation_allowed,
            system_triggered_allowed, event_read_allowed, system_triggered_schedule?,
            missed_occurrence_policy?, owner_tag_key?)
            when bounded_context.status = active
        RegisterCommandType(access_mapping, bounded_context, name, schema, tag_mappings,
            sensitive_fields, private_fields, rest_trigger_allowed, owner_tag_key?)
            when bounded_context.status = active
        RegisterProjection(access_mapping, bounded_context, name, schema,
            consumed_event_types, sync)
            when bounded_context.status = active
        RebuildProjection(access_mapping, projection)
        DiscardProjectionRebuild(access_mapping, projection)

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level grant only authorises registering event types,
        -- command types and projections into its own bounded context,
        -- never another one. For a projection this extends to what it
        -- reads: every EventType it consumes must belong to that same
        -- context, so registration is never a way to build a view across
        -- the boundary. The same scoping governs what this surface shows:
        -- the rebuilds visible here are those of its own context's
        -- projections, and a projection in any other context is not merely
        -- unrebuildable but invisible. The scheduled event types shown here
        -- are scoped identically: its own context's, and no other's - and
        -- so is the full event type and command type listing: an event
        -- type or command type registered against any other bounded
        -- context is not merely unlisted but invisible here, the same as
        -- everything else this surface shows.

    @guarantee ScheduleStateIsShared
        -- What an admin reads about a scheduled event type here - its
        -- policy, when it last fired and how far its schedule has been
        -- accounted for - is one shared answer per event type, not one per
        -- running instance. Which replica answers the query cannot change
        -- it, and neither can a deploy: re-registering a type leaves both
        -- positions exactly where the scheduler left them (see the note
        -- above rule RegisterEventType). The last-fired time is the
        -- occurrence's own instant rather than the wall clock of the write,
        -- so under a catch-up firing it reads as the occurrence that was
        -- owed, which is the question an admin is actually asking.

    @guarantee RegistrationReachesEveryInstance
        -- A registration is a fact about the bounded context, not about
        -- whichever instance the call happened to land on. An EventType,
        -- CommandType or Projection registered anywhere becomes
        -- submittable, queryable and subscribable on every instance, with
        -- no restart and no redeploy of the others - and the same holds
        -- for a bounded context created, archived or deleted elsewhere
        -- (see BoundedContextCreation, BoundedContextArchival and
        -- BoundedContextDeletion). So an admin never has to know or
        -- arrange which instance a load balancer sent the call to, and a
        -- reconciliation loop running on one instance is not registering
        -- for that instance alone (see the note above RegisterEventType).
        --
        -- Reached the same way EventSubscription's DeliverySpansInstances
        -- is, and with the same limits: through the one shared Postgres
        -- database and nothing else, with symmetric instances that never
        -- address each other, and at most once. Registered state is
        -- durable before any instance is told about it, so an instance
        -- that misses the notice is behind rather than wrong - it serves
        -- the types it already knows correctly, rejects the new one as
        -- unregistered rather than mishandling it, and comes into line as
        -- soon as it reads registered state again, at its next startup
        -- reconciliation pass at the latest.

    @guarantee SchemaEvolutionStaysCompatible
        -- Re-registering an EventType, CommandType or Projection with a
        -- schema that would break an existing reader is rejected outright,
        -- never silently accepted and versioned - enforced by
        -- schema_is_backwards_compatible (see the note above
        -- RegisterEventType). A field already declared is never removed
        -- and never tightened from optional to required; a newly added
        -- field must be optional. A first-time registration has no prior
        -- schema to break and is unaffected.

    @guarantee RegistrationNeverRebuilds
        -- Registering a projection never replays a stream and never
        -- disturbs what a projection is already serving. A change that
        -- could not honestly be applied to already-folded content is
        -- staged and waits; only an explicit RebuildProjection call starts
        -- the replay, and only promotion - automatic, and complete by
        -- construction - changes what a reader sees. A deploy therefore
        -- costs nothing and breaks nothing, however far the declared
        -- definition has moved. Discarding a staged rebuild costs nothing
        -- either, and for the same reason: it drops the waiting definition
        -- and leaves the live projection serving exactly what it served
        -- before.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only. Typically driven by a startup reconciliation loop
        -- rather than by ad-hoc human calls, so the same shape is expected
        -- to be submitted repeatedly and unchanged. Still a GraphQL
        -- mutation, for manual and operational use.
        --
        -- The loop cannot reach a bounded context nobody has been granted
        -- access to. This surface faces AdminAccess, so a context created
        -- but not yet granted registers nothing on any deploy, however
        -- often the loop runs: a superadmin has to run
        -- GrantRoleAccessMapping for some Role on that context first. See
        -- the note above rule AddBoundedContext for why that step is
        -- deliberately manual and what an ungranted context can and cannot
        -- do.
        --
        -- Projections are reconciled the same way as event and command
        -- types, and belong here for the same reason: a projection's
        -- schema, the event types it consumes and whether it updates
        -- synchronously are all compiled into the running binary, so the
        -- same loop that registers types registers them too. schema_version
        -- advances only when schema itself changed; consumed_event_types
        -- and sync are reconciled to the given values on every call when
        -- the change is trivial, and staged for a rebuild when it is not.
        --
        -- What re-registration does not do is rebuild what a projection
        -- already holds. RebuildProjection is that action, and it is here
        -- rather than on a surface of its own because it completes the
        -- same reconciliation: the deploy declares the new definition, an
        -- admin decides when the replay it implies is paid for. It names
        -- the projection alone - what to rebuild it into is whatever the
        -- last registration staged, never a fresh definition supplied at
        -- trigger time. See the note above RegisterProjection.
        --
        -- Because that decision is a person's rather than a deploy's, the
        -- staged rebuilds are readable here: for each of the context's
        -- projections, whether a rebuild is pending or building, which
        -- staged definition is waiting (schema_version) and how far a
        -- running one has replayed (caught_up_to). Without it an admin's
        -- only notice that a rebuild is outstanding is having run the
        -- deploy that staged it, and a projection may drift arbitrarily
        -- far from what its own binary declares unnoticed. Deliberately
        -- coarse, as everywhere else on this track: this establishes that
        -- the pending rebuild is visible and scoped, not how it is paged
        -- or shaped on the wire (see the scope header's exclusions).
        --
        -- RebuildProjection does not require an active bounded context - a
        -- replay writes nothing, so archival does not forbid it (see the
        -- note above the rule) - and this surface no longer binds active
        -- contexts only either. A rebuild staged before its context was
        -- archived therefore stays reachable, and the staged rebuilds of an
        -- archived context's projections stay readable, which is the whole
        -- point of showing them: a stranded row an admin cannot see is a
        -- stranded row an admin cannot act on.
        --
        -- Acting on one means one of two things, and both are here.
        -- RebuildProjection pays for the replay; DiscardProjectionRebuild
        -- drops the staged definition and leaves the live projection
        -- serving what it already serves. Neither is narrowed by archival,
        -- so a rebuild staged before its context was archived is no longer
        -- stranded at all: an admin can still run it, and can now also
        -- decide it is not worth running. A pending rebuild is a decision
        -- waiting to be made, not one nobody can unmake. Discard reaches
        -- pending rows only - a rebuild already replaying runs to
        -- promotion (see the note above the rule).
        --
        -- Scheduled event types are read here for the same reason and in
        -- the same shape, rather than from a surface of their own. A
        -- schedule is registered state of this context, declared by the
        -- same deploy-time loop that registers everything else, and the
        -- thing an admin needs to know about it - when it last fired - is
        -- an outcome nobody can see from the registration alone, exactly as
        -- a staged rebuild is. A separate surface would repeat this one's
        -- facing, context and scoping guarantee to add a single read to the
        -- same admin's view of the same context.
        --
        -- Three values per scheduled type, and each answers a different
        -- question: missed_occurrence_policy is what this type owes for an
        -- occurrence nobody was watching for, last_fired_at is when it last
        -- actually produced an event, and schedule_position is how far its
        -- schedule has been accounted for. The last two are equal in the
        -- ordinary case and diverge exactly when a policy passed something
        -- over, so the gap between them is the answer to "did we skip
        -- anything, and how much" - which cannot be read off either one
        -- alone. Types that were never opted into scheduling are left out
        -- rather than listed with three empty values.
        --
        -- Read-only, deliberately. There is no action here to fire a
        -- schedule by hand, reset a position or replay a backlog: the
        -- scheduler faces no actor at all (see the note above rule
        -- CreateSystemEvent), and the way to change what it does is to
        -- change what the type declares and re-register it.
        --
        -- The active-context condition sits on the three Register* actions
        -- instead, one guard each. That is where it always belonged:
        -- registration is part of a deploy-time reconciliation loop and
        -- means nothing against an archived context, and each of those
        -- rules already requires it, so the guards restate the rule rather
        -- than adding to it. Nothing else on this surface is narrowed by
        -- archival.
}

surface EventTypeAdminOperations {
    facing access_mapping: AdminAccess
    context event_type: EventType
        where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context

    provides:
        CreateExternalEventToken(access_mapping, event_type, scope?)
        CreateDirectCreationToken(access_mapping, event_type, scope?)
        CreateEventReadToken(access_mapping, event_type, scope?, start_from?, start_at_sequence?, start_at_time?)

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level grant only authorises minting tokens for
        -- EventTypes belonging to its own bounded context.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only, even though every token minted here is used over
        -- REST: issuing a credential is an admin operation, so it lives
        -- on the admin track.
}

surface CommandTypeAdminOperations {
    facing access_mapping: AdminAccess
    context command_type: CommandType
        where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context

    provides:
        CreateCommandToken(access_mapping, command_type, scope?)

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level grant only authorises minting tokens for
        -- CommandTypes belonging to its own bounded context.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only, the same as EventTypeAdminOperations. Separate
        -- from it only because the thing being scoped to is a CommandType
        -- rather than an EventType.
}

surface TokenRevocation {
    facing access_mapping: AdminAccess
    context token: AccessToken where status = active

    let token_scope =
        if token.purpose = CommandToken: token.command_type.bounded_context
        else: token.event_type.bounded_context

    provides:
        RevokeToken(access_mapping, token)
            when token_scope = access_mapping.bounded_context

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level grant only authorises revoking tokens whose
        -- scoped type - EventType or CommandType - belongs to its own
        -- bounded context. Which context that is per variant is spelled
        -- out by token_scope in RevokeToken.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only, and covers every AccessToken variant: revocation
        -- is one operation regardless of what the token was scoped to.
}

surface BoundedContextArchival {
    facing access_mapping: AdminAccess
    context bounded_context: BoundedContext
        where this = access_mapping.bounded_context and status = active

    provides:
        ArchiveBoundedContext(access_mapping, bounded_context)

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level grant only authorises archiving its own
        -- bounded context, never another one.
}

surface BoundedContextDeletion {
    facing caller: Superadmin
    context bounded_context: BoundedContext where status = archived

    provides:
        DeleteBoundedContext(caller, bounded_context)

    @guarantee SuperadminOnly
        -- Deleting a bounded context is an operation on the set of
        -- contexts a deployment has, not one within a context, so it is
        -- gated by Role.superadmin rather than by a per-context
        -- RoleAccessMapping - a deliberately higher bar than
        -- BoundedContextArchival's, which an admin-level grant reaches.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only, and separate from BoundedContextArchival despite
        -- the neighbouring subject: this surface faces a different party
        -- and applies only to contexts archiving has already finished
        -- with, so the two cannot share a context binding.
        --
        -- Irreversible, and the only irreversible operation here besides
        -- ForgetSubject. Everything scoped to the context goes with it -
        -- see the note above rule DeleteBoundedContext for what that
        -- covers and for why the name becomes available again afterwards.
}

surface SubjectErasure {
    facing access_mapping: AdminAccess
    context key: EncryptionKey
        where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context and status = active

    provides:
        ForgetSubject(access_mapping, key)

    @guarantee GrantScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level grant only authorises forgetting subjects within
        -- its own bounded context.

    @guidance
        -- Exposed as a GraphQL mutation. There is no un-forget: destroying
        -- an EncryptionKey is irreversible by design.
}

surface BoundedContextCreation {
    facing caller: Superadmin

    provides:
        AddBoundedContext(caller, name)

    @guarantee SuperadminOnly
        -- Creating a bounded context isn't "for" any existing context,
        -- so it is gated by Role.superadmin rather than a per-context
        -- RoleAccessMapping.

    @guidance
        -- A successful call leaves a context nobody can use yet: creation
        -- grants no RoleAccessMapping, not even to the caller, and every
        -- per-context operation - type registration included - needs one.
        -- The next step is always GrantRoleAccessMapping on surface
        -- AccessManagement; see the note above rule AddBoundedContext for
        -- why the two are separate acts.
}

surface BoundedContextDirectory {
    facing caller: Superadmin

    exposes:
        for bounded_context in BoundedContexts:
            bounded_context.name
            bounded_context.status
            bounded_context.created_at
            bounded_context.created_by
            bounded_context.template
            for mapping in bounded_context.access_mappings:
                mapping.role.name
                mapping.role.id
                mapping.role.external_subject
                mapping.level
                mapping.can_read_sensitive
                mapping.status

    provides:
        ListBoundedContexts(caller)

    @guarantee SuperadminOnly
        -- Listing bounded contexts isn't "for" any existing context, so it
        -- is gated by Role.superadmin rather than a per-context
        -- RoleAccessMapping - the read counterpart to
        -- BoundedContextCreation's mutation, gated the same way.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only, and a query rather than a mutation. It exists for
        -- admin frontends that would otherwise have to introspect the
        -- schema to find out which bounded contexts a deployment has: the
        -- directory answers that directly, with each context's name,
        -- status and provenance.
        --
        -- The access mappings come with it deliberately. A superadmin
        -- already sees every one of these values through CreateRole and
        -- AccessManagement, so surfacing them here discloses nothing new -
        -- it only answers "who currently has what access to this bounded
        -- context" in one place, instead of a separate query per context.
        --
        -- The template comes with it for a plainer reason: without it
        -- nothing anywhere tells a superadmin which contexts are tenants,
        -- of what, and which are templates - and
        -- ResyncBoundedContextFromTemplate on surface
        -- BoundedContextTemplating is an action nobody can aim without
        -- knowing that. Absent for the ordinary case, which is most of
        -- them.
        --
        -- Wire contract deferred as everywhere else: result shape, paging
        -- and cursor encoding, and GraphQL type and argument naming stay
        -- with the rest of that contract (see the scope header's
        -- exclusions).
}

surface BoundedContextTemplating {
    facing caller: Superadmin

    provides:
        CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate(caller, template, name, role, level,
            can_read_sensitive, scope?)
        ResyncBoundedContextFromTemplate(caller, bounded_context)

    @guarantee SuperadminOnly
        -- Both actions are operations on the set of contexts a deployment
        -- has rather than operations within one, so both are gated by
        -- Role.superadmin rather than by a per-context RoleAccessMapping -
        -- the same bar BoundedContextCreation and BoundedContextDeletion
        -- are at, for the same reason. That extends to the type
        -- registrations an application performs: they run on the
        -- superadmin's authority, not on the grant this surface makes and
        -- not on any grant the tenant holds (see the note above rule
        -- CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate).

    @guarantee AccessGrantedWithCreation
        -- Creating a templated context grants access into it in the same
        -- call, and this is the one place in this spec where creation and
        -- granting are a single act. It is a stated exception to the
        -- principle the note above rule AddBoundedContext sets out - that
        -- the two are separate because they are two decisions - not an
        -- erosion of it: AddBoundedContext still grants nothing to
        -- anybody, and nothing here grants anything to the caller. What
        -- this action does is let a superadmin name the tenant context and
        -- the Role that operates it in one audited decision, at a level, a
        -- sensitivity and a read scope it chose explicitly. There is still
        -- no self-service path and no grant that arrives as a by-product
        -- of something else.
        --
        -- The scope is settable here rather than only on
        -- GrantRoleAccessMapping because no later call could set it on
        -- this grant: invariant UniqueActiveAccessPerRoleAndContext
        -- refuses a second active mapping for the pair, so the grant a
        -- tenant is created with is the grant it keeps short of a revoke
        -- and a re-grant (see the note above rule
        -- CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate).

    @guarantee TemplateIsNeverATenant
        -- A context named as a template has no template of its own, so
        -- templating is exactly one level deep and a tenant can never
        -- become somebody else's template. Enforced as a guard and
        -- restated as invariant TemplateIsNeverItselfTemplated; see the
        -- note above rule CreateBoundedContextFromTemplate for why
        -- precedence and cycles are the two things this closes.

    @guarantee PropagationIsAlwaysAsked
        -- A template's later changes never reach its tenants on their own.
        -- Registering a new event type against a template writes nothing
        -- to any tenant; a tenant changes only when someone calls
        -- ResyncBoundedContextFromTemplate for that tenant. There is no
        -- schedule, no cascade and no fan-out on deploy, and a deployment
        -- with a thousand tenants makes a thousand explicit calls or none.

    @guarantee ResyncIsRepeatableAndAdditive
        -- Resyncing is safe to repeat: every registration it triggers is
        -- the same idempotent upsert a deploy-time reconciliation loop
        -- performs, so a tenant already in step is left exactly as it was.
        -- It never removes anything either - a type a tenant has and the
        -- template does not is untouched - and it never replays a stream:
        -- a projection change that cannot honestly be applied in place is
        -- staged and waits for an explicit RebuildProjection, exactly as
        -- on TypeRegistration (see RegistrationNeverRebuilds).

    @guidance
        -- Exposed as GraphQL mutations, alongside the other
        -- superadmin-facing bounded context operations. This is the
        -- ops-facing answer to "one bounded context per tenant": adding a
        -- tenant is one call by an operator, with no code change, no
        -- redeploy and no per-tenant registration pass.
        --
        -- No context binding, for the same reason surface
        -- AccessManagement has none: a superadmin already reaches every
        -- bounded context, so the template and the tenant are named as
        -- arguments rather than scoped by the surface. Which contexts are
        -- templates and which are tenants is read from surface
        -- BoundedContextDirectory, which shows each context's template.
        --
        -- The two actions are here together because they are one
        -- lifecycle: creation stamps a tenant out of a template's current
        -- registrations, and resync is the same stamping applied again
        -- later. Everything about what "applying a template" means - and
        -- in particular that it is a resubmission of registrations rather
        -- than a copy of rows - is in the note above rule
        -- ApplyTemplateRegistrations.
        --
        -- What a superadmin still has to do by hand is everything outside
        -- the template's declared types: this creates no tokens, no
        -- subscriptions and no further grants, and it does not archive or
        -- delete anything. A templated context is archived and deleted
        -- through BoundedContextArchival and BoundedContextDeletion like
        -- any other, and so is a template: archiving one leaves its
        -- tenants holding everything they have with nowhere new to resync
        -- from, and deleting one leaves them holding it as ordinary
        -- untemplated contexts, since DeleteBoundedContext clears the link
        -- on each of them (see the note above that rule). Neither
        -- operation is refused on account of a tenant, and neither takes
        -- anything away from one.
        --
        -- Wire contract deferred as everywhere else: argument and result
        -- shapes, and GraphQL type and mutation naming, stay with the rest
        -- of that contract (see the scope header's exclusions).
}

surface SuperadminBootstrap {
    facing bootstrap: BootstrapSecret

    provides:
        CreateSuperadmin(bootstrap, bootstrap_secret, name, external_subject)

    @guarantee ClosesPermanentlyOnFirstClaim
        -- Reachable only while no active superadmin Role exists anywhere -
        -- the instant one does, this action can never fire again, for any
        -- caller, with any secret, however many times the process
        -- restarts. There is no reopening path while a superadmin remains
        -- active; see the note above rule CreateSuperadmin for the one
        -- state that does reopen it (every superadmin Role revoked) and
        -- why that is self-healing rather than a bypass.

    @guarantee NoCredentialIssued
        -- A successful call hands back only the created Role's own id,
        -- name and external_subject - never a secret to authenticate
        -- with. The Role it creates authenticates from then on with a
        -- JWT from the trusted external IdP, exactly like every other
        -- Role (see the identity resolution note above entity Role).
        -- SkilJ issues no login credential of its own for any Role, and
        -- the bootstrap secret is not the exception: it authorises one
        -- act of creation, not a session.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only, and the one surface with no Role-based actor
        -- behind it - see the Actor Declarations note above for why it
        -- faces BootstrapSecret directly instead. The full mechanism -
        -- how the secret is generated, why it lives in memory only, and
        -- what closes and reopens this surface - is spelled out in the
        -- note above rule CreateSuperadmin rather than repeated here.
}

surface AccessManagement {
    facing caller: Superadmin

    provides:
        CreateRole(caller, name, superadmin, external_subject)
        RevokeRole(caller, role)
        GrantRoleAccessMapping(caller, role, bounded_context, level, can_read_sensitive, scope?)
        RevokeRoleAccessMapping(caller, access_mapping)

    @guarantee SuperadminOnly
        -- Only an active Role with superadmin = true may create or
        -- revoke Roles, or grant/revoke RoleAccessMappings on their behalf -
        -- including its own. There is no self-service path.

    @guarantee ExternalIdentityRegisteredExplicitly
        -- A Role is bound to an external IdP identity only because a
        -- superadmin named that identity when creating it. No Role comes
        -- into being by a JWT merely presenting an unrecognised subject
        -- claim: an unmatched claim resolves to no Role and authorises
        -- nothing.

    @guidance
        -- Exposed as GraphQL mutations. The very first superadmin Role
        -- cannot come from here - CreateRole requires a superadmin caller
        -- and none exists yet - so it comes from surface
        -- SuperadminBootstrap instead, claimed with a secret SkilJ prints
        -- to its own startup log rather than any caller-supplied value.
        -- See the note above rule CreateSuperadmin for the full mechanism.
}

surface PrivateFieldGrantManagement {
    facing access_mapping: ReadAccess

    let own_grants =
        PrivateFieldGrants where bounded_context = access_mapping.bounded_context
            and grantor = access_mapping.role

    exposes:
        for grant in own_grants:
            grant.grantee.id
            grant.grantee.name
            grant.event
            grant.command
            grant.status
            grant.created_at

    provides:
        GrantPrivateFieldAccessForEvent(access_mapping, grantee, event?)
        GrantPrivateFieldAccessForCommand(access_mapping, grantee, command?)
        RevokePrivateFieldAccess(access_mapping, grant)
        ListPrivateFieldGrants(access_mapping, grantor?)

    @guarantee SharingIsSelfServiceAndRevocationIsTheGrantorsAlone
        -- The only surface in this file where a grant is made with no
        -- admin and no superadmin anywhere in the call, and deliberately
        -- so: a caller sharing a private field hands over nothing it did
        -- not already hold itself, so there is nothing for a higher
        -- authority to be protecting (see the note above rule
        -- GrantPrivateFieldAccessForEvent). The same asymmetry governs the
        -- way out. A grant is revocable only by the Role that made it -
        -- not by an admin, not by a superadmin, and not by the grantee -
        -- so nobody can quietly end a sharing decision on someone else's
        -- behalf.
        --
        -- A grantor may only ever name a record it is genuinely the
        -- default private reader of: the creator of an own-kind field's
        -- record, or the party an addressed-kind field points at. Naming
        -- anything else is refused outright rather than recorded and
        -- silently ignored at read time.

    @guarantee BlanketGrantsAreReDerivedAtRenderTime
        -- A grant naming no record at all is a convenience, never a wider
        -- trust boundary. It cannot be checked when it is made, because
        -- the records it covers need not exist yet, so it is checked
        -- instead at every read against the specific record being read:
        -- the grantee sees a private field only where the grantor would
        -- itself have been that record's default reader at that moment. A
        -- blanket grant therefore never reaches past what the same grantor
        -- could have handed over one record at a time, and it stops
        -- reaching a record the moment the grantor stops being its default
        -- reader - including when a re-registration changes what the type
        -- declares private (see the render_event note above rule
        -- DeliverToSubscriptions).

    @guarantee TeamFieldsNeedNoGrant
        -- A team-kind private field is visible to any Role whose own name
        -- is the one the field declares, immediately and automatically,
        -- with nothing granted and nothing to grant. Membership is the
        -- grant. So nothing here shares a team field, nothing here revokes
        -- one, and ListPrivateFieldGrants never lists anything for one -
        -- not because the listing is incomplete but because there is no
        -- row and never was. Changing who sees a team field means changing
        -- which Roles carry that name, which is surface AccessManagement's
        -- business rather than this one's.

    @guarantee ListingScopedToGrantorUnlessAdmin
        -- Any active grant may list its own outgoing grants, at any level:
        -- reading back a decision you made yourself needs no authority
        -- beyond having made it. Listing a different Role's grants is the
        -- oversight half and requires admin level, the same as every other
        -- cross-caller listing on this track. Either way it is scoped to
        -- the caller's own bounded context - a grant made in another
        -- context is invisible here, admin or not.

    @guidance
        -- GraphQL only, and by construction rather than by policy: all
        -- three visibility rules a private field can follow are stated in
        -- terms of a Role - its id as a record's creator, its name as a
        -- team, its external_subject as an addressee - so a REST bearer
        -- token has no identity for any of them to test, and there is
        -- nothing for a REST counterpart to this surface to authenticate.
        --
        -- Faced by ReadAccess - any active mapping, at any level - rather
        -- than by a write-level one, and that is a deliberate choice
        -- rather than the loosest available default. The two shareable
        -- kinds reach their default readers by different routes. An
        -- own-kind field's default reader is always write-level or above
        -- already, because producing the record in the first place took a
        -- write grant (see rule AuthoriseCommandSubmission), so facing
        -- WriteAccess would have cost that kind nothing. An addressed-kind
        -- field's default reader is whoever the payload named, and a
        -- payload can name anyone the bounded context does business with,
        -- at whatever level their own grant happens to hold. Blocking a
        -- read-level addressee from re-sharing something it was
        -- personally and individually entitled to read would be a
        -- restriction with no reason behind it: the real gate on sharing
        -- is is_default_private_reader, which asks whether this caller is
        -- the record's creator or its addressee, not what level its grant
        -- carries. Every rule this surface provides checks that gate and
        -- checks no level, so the facing bound and the rules agree.
        --
        -- A revoked Role loses its outgoing grants' effect without those
        -- grants being touched, and needs no cascade of its own for that.
        -- RevokeRole already revokes every RoleAccessMapping the Role
        -- holds, and every read on this track is gated by an active
        -- mapping, so a revoked grantee reaches no surface to read a
        -- shared field through. The grant rows stay standing and stay
        -- listable, which is what an audit of who was shared with wants.
        --
        -- Deliberately coarse on the wire contract, as everywhere else on
        -- this track: this establishes the capability, who may exercise it
        -- and how far it reaches, not how the mutations and the query are
        -- named, shaped, paged or filtered (see the scope header's
        -- exclusions).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- None. What a deleted template leaves behind, the question templated
-- tenants arrived with, is answered where the deletion itself happens
-- rather than here: DeleteBoundedContext clears the template link on
-- every context templated from the one it ends, so each of them becomes
-- an ordinary untemplated bounded context and carries on with everything
-- it was registered with. Deletion stays unconditional, depending on
-- nothing outside the context being deleted - which is exactly why the
-- other candidate answer, refusing the deletion while any tenant still
-- references the template, was not taken. Archival needs no answer of its
-- own: a tenant whose template is archived keeps everything and merely
-- has nowhere new to be resynced from (see the note above rule
-- ResyncBoundedContextFromTemplate).
--
-- What a scheduler owes for an occurrence it was not running for,
-- the question standing here before that one, is answered - and answered by
-- refusing to answer it once for everybody. Both directions can lose or
-- duplicate real work, so the choice is the bounded context author's:
-- EventType.missed_occurrence_policy is stated per event type at
-- registration, with no default and no fallback, and a registration that
-- opts into scheduling without choosing is rejected outright (see enum
-- MissedOccurrencePolicy and rule RegisterEventType). It is asked for only
-- of a type that opted in; a type nothing schedules is never asked.
--
-- The three choices are guards on rule CreateSystemEvent, evaluated
-- against durable, registered state rather than against anything a process
-- remembers: EventType.schedule_position accounts for every occurrence at
-- or before an instant, and EventType.last_fired_at records the occurrence
-- the most recent event stood for. One shared value each per event type,
-- so several instances against one database agree on what has fired, a
-- deploy neither re-fires nor skips, and an admin can read both -
-- alongside the policy itself - on surface TypeRegistration. skip needs
-- one thing the position cannot supply on its own, since a firing that is
-- six hours late and one that is two seconds late leave the same state
-- behind: rule SkipMissedOccurrences is where a resuming scheduler says
-- which it was.
--
-- Dotted-path addressing into nested payload fields, the question standing
-- here before that one, is answered in full too: TagMapping.field,
-- SensitiveField.field/subject_field and Filter.field each accept a
-- two-segment path, sensitivity encrypts at the leaf, and the existence
-- check that makes an addressed field permanent resolves one level deeper
-- (see the payload schema shape note above entity CommandType, and the
-- derive_tags, protect_sensitive_fields, valid_filters and
-- schema_is_backwards_compatible notes above the rules). Lists of nested
-- objects and any second level of nesting remain excluded, but as a
-- decided cap with stated reasoning rather than an unresolved question.
